/* Che cucino? - dati: ricette-en */
window.DATI = window.DATI || {};
DATI["ricette-en"] = {"1":{"n":"Aglio, olio e peperoncino","p":["Bring the water to the boil and salt it once it's boiling: 10 g of salt per litre.","Slice the garlic thinly and warm 6 tablespoons of oil in a pan over a low heat with the garlic and the crumbled chilli.","As soon as the garlic barely starts to colour, turn off the heat: if it goes dark it makes everything bitter.","Drain the spaghetti very al dente, keeping back a cup of the cooking water.","Toss in the pan for a minute, adding cooking water a little at a time until the sauce turns creamy.","Off the heat, stir in the chopped parsley."],"o":"The secret is the cooking water: its starch binds the oil and stops it sliding to the bottom of the plate."},"2":{"n":"Cacio e pepe","p":["Toast the crushed peppercorns in a dry pan for a minute, until fragrant.","Add a ladle of pasta water to the pan and let it simmer.","Cook the pasta in a small amount of water, so it is starchier.","In a bowl, grate the pecorino and loosen it with warm (never boiling) pasta water until you have a smooth cream.","Drain the pasta al dente, toss it in the pan with the pepper, then take it off the heat.","Add the pecorino cream, stirring quickly: the pan must be warm, not hot, or the cheese splits."],"o":"Pecorino turns stringy above 65 °C. If that happens, add cold water and whisk again."},"3":{"n":"Pasta alla Norma","p":["Cut the aubergines into cubes, salt them in a colander and leave them to drain for 30 minutes.","Pat them dry and fry them in hot oil a few at a time, then drain on kitchen paper.","In a pan, fry the garlic gently, add the passata, season with salt and cook for 20 minutes.","Cook the pasta al dente and toss it in the sauce with half the aubergines.","Serve topped with the remaining aubergines, grated ricotta salata and basil."],"o":"Salting the aubergines matters: they lose their bitter water and soak up far less oil."},"4":{"n":"Pasta e fagioli","p":["Finely chop the celery, carrot and onion and soften them in oil over a gentle heat for 10 minutes.","Add the drained beans, the passata and a litre of hot water. Season with salt and cook for 30 minutes.","Blend a third of the beans and return them to the pot: that is what makes it creamy.","Add the pasta straight into the soup and cook, stirring often, adding water if it gets too dry.","Leave to rest off the heat for 10 minutes before serving with a drizzle of raw oil and rosemary."],"o":"Serve it warm, not piping hot: that's when you really taste the beans."},"5":{"n":"Carbonara","p":["Cut the guanciale into thick strips and brown it in a cold pan with no added fat, over a medium heat.","When it is golden and the fat has melted, turn off the heat and set aside.","Beat 4 egg yolks with the grated pecorino and plenty of pepper into a thick cream.","Cook the pasta al dente and drain it, keeping the cooking water.","Toss the pasta in the guanciale fat off the heat, then add the egg cream, stirring in a couple of tablespoons of water.","Serve at once with the crispy guanciale on top."],"o":"The pan must never be on the heat when the egg goes in: the residual heat is more than enough."},"6":{"n":"Pasta with basil pesto","p":["Wash the basil and dry it very thoroughly: water makes it oxidise.","Pound, or blend in short pulses, the basil, garlic, pine nuts and a pinch of salt, keeping everything cold.","Add the grated cheeses and pour in the oil in a thin stream until creamy.","Cook the trofie with diced potato and green beans in the same water.","Drain and dress off the heat with the pesto loosened with two tablespoons of pasta water."],"o":"Pesto is never cooked. If you heat it, it turns dark and bitter."},"7":{"n":"Risotto alla milanese","p":["Chop the onion very finely and soften it in half the butter without letting it colour.","Toast the rice over a high heat for two minutes, until the grains feel hot to the touch.","Deglaze with the wine and let it evaporate.","Add the boiling stock one ladle at a time, stirring, for about 16 minutes.","Halfway through, dissolve the saffron in a little stock and stir it in.","Turn off the heat, wait a minute and beat in cold butter and parmesan, stirring vigorously."],"o":"The final creaming (mantecatura) is done off the heat with butter straight from the fridge: the temperature shock creates the creaminess."},"8":{"n":"Pasta alla puttanesca","p":["Warm the oil with garlic and chilli, add the anchovies and melt them, crushing them with a spoon.","Add pitted olives and rinsed capers and let them flavour the oil for two minutes.","Pour in the passata and cook for 15 minutes over a medium heat. Taste before salting: anchovies and capers are already salty.","Drain the pasta al dente and toss it in the sauce with a little cooking water.","Finish with chopped parsley."],"o":"This is one dish where salt is tasted for at the end, not added at the start."},"9":{"n":"Pasta with courgettes and pancetta","p":["Brown the diced pancetta in a pan until crisp and set it aside.","In the same fat, soften the thinly sliced onion.","Add the courgettes in rounds, season with salt and cook over a high heat for 10 minutes so they stay firm.","Drain the pasta al dente and toss it in the pan with a ladle of pasta water.","Stir in the parmesan off the heat and return the crispy pancetta."],"o":"Cook courgettes over a high heat and a few at a time, otherwise they stew and go soft."},"10":{"n":"Pasta with tuna and cherry tomatoes","p":["Fry the unpeeled garlic clove in the oil, then remove it.","Add the halved cherry tomatoes and let them soften for 8 minutes, crushing a few.","Add the olives and the drained tuna, stir and turn off the heat almost straight away.","Drain the pasta al dente and toss for a minute with a little cooking water.","Finish with chopped parsley and a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Add the tuna at the end: if it cooks too long it turns dry and stringy."},"11":{"n":"Gnocchi with tomato and basil","p":["Fry the garlic in oil, add the passata, season with salt and cook for 20 minutes over a gentle heat.","Add the hand-torn basil only in the last two minutes.","Drop the gnocchi into boiling salted water and lift them out with a slotted spoon as soon as they float.","Dress them straight in the pan with the sauce and parmesan."],"o":"Drain gnocchi the moment they float, not a second later: they keep cooking in the sauce."},"12":{"n":"Chickpea and rosemary soup","p":["Warm the oil with crushed garlic and a sprig of rosemary, then remove them.","Add the drained chickpeas and the passata, cover with hot water and cook for 20 minutes.","Blend half the soup and return it to the pot.","Cook the pasta straight in the soup, stirring often.","Serve with a drizzle of raw oil, pepper and chopped fresh rosemary."],"o":"Infusing the rosemary in the oil at the start gives far more aroma than adding it at the end."},"13":{"n":"Lemon chicken","p":["Cut the breast into 1 cm slices and dust them lightly with flour, shaking off the excess.","Heat oil and butter and brown the slices for two minutes on each side over a high heat.","Deglaze with the juice of one lemon and half a glass of water; season with salt and pepper.","Let it reduce for 3-4 minutes until the sauce coats the spoon.","Finish with grated lemon zest and parsley."],"o":"The flour isn't a coating, it's there to thicken the sauce: a few grams are enough."},"14":{"n":"Meatballs in tomato sauce","p":["Soak the bread in the milk for 10 minutes, then squeeze it out.","Mix the meat, bread, egg, parmesan, chopped garlic, parsley, salt and pepper. Work it as little as possible.","Shape 40 g meatballs and brown them in a pan until golden all over.","Make the sauce with oil, passata and salt and cook for 10 minutes.","Drop the meatballs into the sauce and cook covered over a low heat for 30 minutes."],"o":"Overworking compacts the meat: the meatballs turn rubbery instead of tender."},"15":{"n":"Escalopes with mushrooms","p":["Clean the mushrooms with a damp cloth and slice them. Don't wash them under the tap.","Sauté them over a high heat with garlic and oil until the liquid has evaporated, then salt them and set aside.","Flour the slices of meat and brown them for a minute on each side.","Deglaze with the wine, return the mushrooms and let it reduce for 5 minutes.","Finish with chopped parsley."],"o":"Salt the mushrooms at the end: salt makes them release water and they end up boiled."},"16":{"n":"Beef stew with potatoes","p":["Brown the meat in a little oil over a very high heat, a little at a time, until well browned. Set aside.","In the same pot, make a soffritto with chopped onion, carrot and celery.","Return the meat, deglaze with the red wine and let it evaporate.","Add the passata and enough hot water to cover, season with salt and cook covered over a very low heat for an hour.","Add the potatoes in chunks and continue for 30 minutes uncovered, until the sauce is thick."],"o":"The initial browning is everything: that's where the flavour comes from, not from the long cooking."},"17":{"n":"Cotolette alla milanese","p":["Pound the cutlets lightly without tearing the meat.","Dip them in beaten egg and then in breadcrumbs, pressing well.","Melt plenty of butter in a pan and fry over a medium heat, 4 minutes per side.","Drain on kitchen paper, salt only now and serve with lemon wedges."],"o":"Salting before frying draws out water and makes the crumb coating come away."},"18":{"n":"Chicken with olives and cherry tomatoes","p":["Brown the chicken pieces in hot oil with garlic and rosemary, skin side down, until golden.","Deglaze with the wine and let it evaporate.","Add cherry tomatoes and olives; season with salt and pepper.","Cover and cook for 30 minutes over a gentle heat, uncovering for the last 10 to let it reduce."],"o":"Brown the skin for a long time without moving it: that's what flavours the pan juices."},"19":{"n":"Salsiccia e friarielli","p":["Trim the turnip tops, keeping the leaves and florets, and blanch them for 5 minutes in salted water.","In a wide pan, heat oil, garlic and chilli.","Brown the sausage in pieces until golden, then set it aside.","Sauté the drained greens in the same pan juices for 10 minutes.","Return the sausage and let everything cook together for five minutes."],"o":"Blanching removes the harshest bitterness and shortens the time in the pan."},"20":{"n":"Pork roast in milk","p":["Tie the meat and brown it in butter and oil on all sides until well coloured.","Season with salt, add sage and garlic and pour in hot milk to come two-thirds of the way up.","Cook uncovered over a very low heat for an hour and a half, turning now and then.","Take out the meat; blend the cooking juices if you want them smooth or leave them curdled, as tradition has it.","Slice the roast only after 10 minutes' rest and spoon the sauce over."],"o":"The milk curdles: those golden curds are the sauce, not a mistake."},"21":{"n":"Homemade burgers","p":["Chop the onion very finely, soften it, then let it cool.","Mix the meat, onion, crumbled bread, egg, mustard, salt and pepper without overworking.","Shape four 2 cm thick patties and press a dimple into the centre with your thumb.","Cook in a very hot pan for 4 minutes per side without ever pressing them down."],"o":"The dimple is there because the meat puffs up as it cooks: without it you end up with a ball."},"22":{"n":"Spaghetti alle vongole","p":["Purge the clams in cold salted water for at least two hours, changing the water.","Open them in a covered pan over a high heat with a drizzle of oil, then shell two-thirds of them.","Strain the liquid they released through a fine sieve: it is the sauce.","In a pan heat oil, garlic and chilli, deglaze with the wine and add the strained liquid.","Drain the pasta very al dente and finish cooking it in the pan with the liquid.","Add the clams and parsley only at the very last moment."],"o":"No salt is added: the clams' own salt is always enough."},"23":{"n":"Baked fish fillets with potatoes","p":["Slice the potatoes 3 mm thick, dress them with oil and salt and spread them in a baking tin.","Bake at 200 °C for 20 minutes until they start to colour.","Lay the fillets on top with the cut cherry tomatoes, olives and sliced garlic.","Season with oil, oregano, salt and pepper.","Return to the oven for 15-18 minutes, until the fish flakes with a fork."],"o":"The potatoes need a head start: they take twice as long as the fish."},"24":{"n":"Sicilian-style tuna","p":["Slice the onions thinly and stew them gently in oil for 15 minutes with two tablespoons of water.","Add cherry tomatoes, olives and rinsed capers and cook for 5 minutes.","Sear the tuna steaks for a minute on each side in a separate very hot pan.","Lay them on the sauce, sprinkle with oregano and leave for two minutes off the heat to take on the flavours."],"o":"Tuna cooked for more than two minutes per side turns dry and grey."},"25":{"n":"Mussels marinara","p":["Clean the mussels, scraping the shells and pulling off the beards.","Heat oil and garlic in a wide pot and add the crushed cherry tomatoes.","Tip in the mussels, splash in the wine and cover: they'll be ready in 5-6 minutes, as soon as they open.","Discard any that stay closed; add parsley and pepper.","Serve with toasted bread to mop up the juices."],"o":"Don't add salt: the mussels already release sea water."},"26":{"n":"Salt cod stew with potatoes","p":["Slice the onion and soften it in oil over a gentle heat.","Add the passata and cook for 10 minutes, then add the potatoes in large chunks and water just to cover.","After 20 minutes lay the pieces of salt cod on top without stirring.","Cover and cook for another 20 minutes over a low heat, shaking the pot instead of stirring.","Add the olives and parsley, and taste before salting."],"o":"Salt cod falls apart if you stir it: shake the pot, don't stir."},"27":{"n":"Marinated anchovies","p":["Clean the anchovies, removing head and backbone, and open them out like a book.","Lay them in a shallow container and cover them completely with lemon juice.","Leave to marinate in the fridge for 3 hours: the flesh must turn white and opaque.","Drain, arrange on a plate and dress with oil, sliced garlic, parsley and chilli.","Leave to rest for an hour before serving."],"o":"For safety, fish to be eaten raw or marinated must be frozen first: 24 hours at -20 °C in a home freezer."},"28":{"n":"Courgette and onion frittata","p":["Slice the onion and soften it in oil, then add the courgettes in rounds.","Cook over a high heat for 10 minutes until golden, season with salt and leave to cool slightly.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, salt, pepper and torn basil.","Add the vegetables and pour into a hot non-stick pan with a little oil.","Cook over a low heat for 6-7 minutes, then flip with the help of a lid and cook for another 4."],"o":"Cool the vegetables before adding them: if they're piping hot they start cooking the egg in the bowl."},"29":{"n":"Eggs in tomato sauce","p":["Fry the garlic in oil, add the passata, season with salt and cook for 15 minutes.","Make four hollows in the sauce with the back of a spoon.","Crack an egg into each hollow, cover and cook for 5-6 minutes over a low heat.","The white should be set and the yolk still soft.","Serve in the pan with basil and toasted bread."],"o":"Covering the pan cooks the top of the egg with steam, with no need to turn it."},"30":{"n":"Parmigiana di melanzane","p":["Slice the aubergines lengthways 5 mm thick, salt them and leave them to drain for an hour.","Pat them dry and fry them in hot oil, then drain on kitchen paper.","Make the sauce with oil, garlic, passata and salt and cook for 20 minutes.","Cut the mozzarella and let it drain: water is the enemy of parmigiana.","Layer sauce, aubergines, mozzarella, parmesan and basil in a dish, three or four layers.","Bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes and rest for at least 20 minutes before cutting."],"o":"Serve it warm. Straight out of the oven it falls apart and you can't taste anything."},"31":{"n":"Mozzarella in carrozza","p":["Cut the mozzarella into thick slices and let it drain well on kitchen paper.","Make four sandwiches with mozzarella and half an anchovy fillet, keeping away from the edges.","Press the edges with your fingers to seal them.","Dip in flour, then in beaten egg, leaving them to soak for a minute.","Fry in hot oil until golden on both sides and drain on kitchen paper."],"o":"Seal the edges well or the mozzarella escapes and burns in the oil."},"32":{"n":"Potato croquettes","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel them while still hot and mash them.","Season with an egg, parmesan, parsley, salt and pepper. Leave to cool.","Shape into cylinders with a small cube of mozzarella in the middle.","Roll in beaten egg and breadcrumbs, twice if you want a thicker crust.","Fry in oil at 170 °C until golden."],"o":"Work the mixture cold: when hot it's impossible to shape."},"33":{"n":"Caprese","p":["Slice the tomatoes and mozzarella 5 mm thick.","Alternate them on the plate, overlapping slightly.","Dress with oil, salt, oregano and hand-torn basil.","Leave to stand for ten minutes at room temperature before serving."],"o":"Take the mozzarella and tomatoes out of the fridge half an hour ahead: cold, they taste of nothing."},"34":{"n":"Peperonata","p":["Slice the onions and stew them gently in oil for 15 minutes.","Add the peppers in wide strips and cook over a high heat for 10 minutes.","Add the passata, season with salt and continue for 20 minutes covered.","In the last few minutes add a tablespoon of vinegar and the basil.","Serve warm or cold."],"o":"The vinegar at the end is what keeps the peppers' sweetness lively."},"35":{"n":"Melanzane a funghetto","p":["Cut the aubergines into cubes and leave them salted for half an hour to drain.","Pat them dry and brown them in a wide pan with hot oil over a high heat.","When golden, add garlic, cherry tomatoes and olives.","Cook for 10 minutes over a medium heat, season with salt and finish with basil."],"o":"Wide pan and high heat: if the aubergines are piled up they stew instead of browning."},"36":{"n":"Crispy roast potatoes","p":["Cut the potatoes into even wedges and rinse them under cold water to remove the starch.","Dry them thoroughly: this step decides how crisp they get.","Parboil for 5 minutes in salted water, drain and shake them in the colander to rough up the surface.","Toss with oil, salt, rosemary and unpeeled garlic cloves.","Roast at 220 °C for 35-40 minutes, turning them halfway."],"o":"Parboiling and shaking creates the rough surface that turns into a crust in the oven."},"37":{"n":"Sautéed chicory","p":["Boil the chicory in plenty of salted water for 10 minutes, then drain and squeeze it.","Heat oil, garlic and chilli in a wide pan.","Add the chicory and sauté over a high heat for 8-10 minutes.","Adjust the salt and serve with a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Squeezing is essential: if water is left in, the greens boil instead of taking on flavour."},"38":{"n":"Sautéed courgettes","p":["Cut the courgettes into 5 mm rounds.","Heat the oil with the crushed garlic in a wide pan.","Add the courgettes and cook over a high heat for 10 minutes without stirring too much.","Salt only at the end and finish with chopped parsley."],"o":"Salting at the start makes them release water: salt them once they're already golden."},"39":{"n":"Rice salad","p":["Cook the rice in salted water, drain it al dente and cool it under running water.","Dress it straight away with a drizzle of oil so it doesn't stick.","Hard-boil the eggs for 9 minutes and cut them into wedges.","Add all the diced ingredients and mix.","Leave in the fridge for at least an hour before serving."],"o":"Dress the rice with oil while it's hot and season it once cold: the order makes all the difference."},"40":{"n":"Caponata","p":["Fry the cubed aubergines, already salted and drained, and drain on kitchen paper.","Blanch the celery chunks for 5 minutes.","Stew the onion in oil, then add the celery, olives and rinsed capers.","Add the passata and cook for 10 minutes, then add the aubergines.","Pour in the vinegar and sugar dissolved together and let it evaporate over a high heat for two minutes.","Leave to cool completely: it's eaten the next day."],"o":"The sweet and sour goes in at the end and must be cooked off, otherwise it stays sharp and harsh."},"41":{"n":"Minestrone","p":["Chop onion, celery and carrot and soften them in oil for 10 minutes.","Add the other vegetables in cubes, the passata and the beans.","Cover with hot water, season with salt and cook for an hour over a gentle heat.","Crush a few potatoes against the side of the pot to thicken it.","Cook the pasta straight in the minestrone and serve with a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Minestrone is better the next day: always make extra."},"42":{"n":"Lentil soup","p":["Make a soffritto with chopped onion, carrot and celery.","Add the rinsed lentils, the passata and the bay leaf.","Cover with hot water and cook for 35-40 minutes, adding water if needed.","Salt only halfway through: earlier, it toughens the skins.","Serve with toasted bread and a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Small lentils don't need soaking; large ones do, for at least four hours."},"43":{"n":"Cream of pumpkin soup","p":["Soften the sliced onion in oil over a gentle heat.","Add the cubed pumpkin and potato and let them take on flavour for five minutes.","Cover with hot stock and cook for 25 minutes until very soft.","Blend with a stick blender until smooth, adjusting the thickness with stock.","Serve with parmesan, pepper, a drizzle of raw oil and chopped rosemary."],"o":"You can't taste the potato but it gives body: without it the soup stays watery."},"44":{"n":"Pasta e patate","p":["Make a soffritto with finely chopped onion, carrot and celery.","Add the potatoes in small cubes and let them cook for ten minutes, stirring.","Add the passata and hot water a little at a time, like a risotto.","When the potatoes start to break up, add the pasta and cook, stirring.","Turn off the heat, stir in the parmesan and leave to rest for five minutes: it should be creamy, not soupy."],"o":"Cook it like a risotto, adding water a little at a time: it's the potato starch that makes it creamy."},"45":{"n":"Ribollita","p":["Make a slow soffritto with onion, carrot and celery.","Add cavolo nero, savoy cabbage and potatoes in pieces, the passata and half the beans.","Cover with water and cook for an hour over a low heat.","Blend the other half of the beans and stir them into the soup.","Turn off the heat and layer stale bread and soup in a tureen. Leave to rest for an hour.","The next day, reheat it in a pan with oil: that's the real ribollita."],"o":"The name says it all: it's eaten the next day, 're-boiled'. Freshly made, it's just a soup."},"46":{"n":"Chickpeas and mussels","p":["Open the mussels in a covered pot over a high heat and strain the liquid.","Shell the mussels, keeping a few whole for presentation.","Heat oil, garlic and chilli, then add the cherry tomatoes and the drained chickpeas.","Add the strained mussel liquid and cook for 15 minutes.","Blend a third of the chickpeas to thicken, then add the mussels and parsley off the heat."],"o":"The mussel liquid is the real stock of the dish: always strain it, never throw it away."},"47":{"n":"Vegetable and cheese savoury tart","p":["Stew the sliced onion, then add the courgettes in rounds and cook for 10 minutes. Season with salt and leave to cool.","Beat the eggs with cream, parmesan, salt and pepper.","Line the tin with the shortcrust pastry on its paper and prick the base with a fork.","Spread the vegetables over it and pour the egg mixture on top.","Bake at 180 °C for 35 minutes until the top is golden and the centre is set."],"o":"Pricking the base stops the pastry puffing up and staying raw under the filling."},"48":{"n":"Quick focaccia","p":["Dissolve the yeast in 350 ml of lukewarm water with a teaspoon of sugar.","Knead with the flour and 10 g of salt, then work in 3 tablespoons of oil.","Leave to rise, covered, for an hour until doubled.","Spread it in an oiled tin, dimple it with your fingers and pour over a brine of water, oil and salt.","Leave to rest for 30 minutes and bake at 220 °C for 20 minutes."],"o":"The brine poured on before baking is what keeps focaccia soft inside and crisp on top."},"49":{"n":"Tomato crostini","p":["Toast the slices of bread in a pan or on the grill.","Rub the still-hot surface with garlic.","Dress the diced tomatoes with oil, salt, oregano and basil.","Spoon them onto the bread only when you're ready to serve."],"o":"Add the tomato at the last moment: after five minutes the bread is already soggy."},"50":{"n":"Aubergine fritters","p":["Boil or bake the aubergines until very soft, then squeeze them well.","Chop them and mix with the soaked and squeezed bread, eggs, parmesan, garlic and basil.","Adjust the texture with breadcrumbs: the mixture must hold its shape in your hand.","Shape into balls and fry in hot oil, or bake at 200 °C for 25 minutes.","Serve hot or warm, plain or in tomato sauce."],"o":"Squeezing the aubergines is the step almost everyone skips, and it's why the balls fall apart."},"51":{"n":"Tiramisù","p":["Make the coffee and leave it to cool completely.","Whisk the yolks with the sugar until pale and foamy.","Add the mascarpone a little at a time, folding from the bottom.","Whisk the egg whites to stiff peaks and fold them in gently.","Dip the savoiardi in the coffee for a second on each side and layer with the cream in two layers.","Chill for at least 4 hours and dust with cocoa only just before serving."],"o":"If raw egg worries you, whisk the yolks with the sugar over a bain-marie up to 65 °C."},"52":{"n":"Apple cake","p":["Peel the apples, slice them thinly and toss them with the juice of half a lemon.","Whisk the eggs and sugar for five minutes, until the mixture leaves a trail.","Add the lukewarm melted butter, the milk and the lemon zest.","Fold in the sifted flour and baking powder, then three-quarters of the apples.","Pour into a 24 cm tin, arrange the remaining apples on top and bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes."],"o":"Test with a skewer in the centre: if it comes out damp, it needs another ten minutes."},"53":{"n":"Jam crostata","p":["Quickly rub together the flour, cold diced butter and sugar until sandy.","Add a whole egg, a yolk and the lemon zest, and bring together without kneading.","Wrap in cling film and chill for an hour.","Roll out two-thirds of the pastry into the tin and fill with the jam, stirred to soften it.","Make strips with the rest and bake at 180 °C for 35 minutes."],"o":"Shortcrust is worked little and kept cold: if the butter melts it turns hard instead of crumbly."},"54":{"n":"Chocolate mousse","p":["Melt the chocolate over a bain-marie and let it cool slightly.","Whisk the yolks with the sugar and stir them into the chocolate.","Whip the well-chilled cream and fold it in from the bottom up.","Whisk the egg whites to stiff peaks and fold them in three additions without knocking out the air.","Pour into glasses and chill for at least 3 hours."],"o":"The chocolate must be warm, not hot: if it's too hot it scrambles the yolks."},"55":{"n":"Vitello tonnato","p":["Simmer the veal topside for an hour in water with celery, carrot, onion and white wine. Let it cool in the stock.","Hard-boil the eggs for 9 minutes and keep only the yolks.","Blend the tuna, yolks, anchovies, half the capers and a few spoonfuls of the cooking stock.","Whisk in oil in a thin stream until the sauce is smooth and thick.","Slice the meat very thinly when cold, coat with the sauce and finish with the remaining capers."],"o":"Slice the meat straight from the fridge, otherwise it crumbles."},"56":{"n":"Bagna càuda","p":["Peel the garlic cloves, remove the green shoots and stew them in milk for 30 minutes over a very low heat.","Mash them with a fork into a cream.","Add the anchovies and melt them, stirring, still over a gentle heat: it must never fry.","Pour in the oil in a thin stream with the butter and cook for another 15 minutes until thick and smooth.","Serve hot with raw and cooked vegetables cut into sticks."],"o":"Cooking the garlic in milk takes away its harshness: it's the step that makes bagna càuda digestible."},"57":{"n":"Mushroom risotto","p":["Sauté the sliced mushrooms with oil and garlic over a high heat, salt at the end and set aside.","Soften the chopped onion in half the butter, then toast the rice for two minutes.","Deglaze with the wine, then add the boiling stock one ladle at a time.","Halfway through, add two-thirds of the mushrooms.","Off the heat, beat in cold butter and parmesan, and finish with the remaining mushrooms and parsley."],"o":"If you use dried mushrooms, the strained soaking water is worth more than the stock."},"58":{"n":"Polenta with gorgonzola","p":["Bring a litre and a half of salted water to the boil.","Pour in the cornmeal in a steady stream, whisking to avoid lumps.","Cook for 45 minutes over a low heat, stirring often with a wooden spoon.","In the last five minutes stir in butter and parmesan.","Pour onto plates and lay pieces of gorgonzola on top: they will melt by themselves."],"o":"Polenta is ready when it comes away from the sides of the pot, not before."},"59":{"n":"Risi e bisi","p":["Gently fry the chopped onion and diced pancetta in oil and butter.","Add the peas and let them take on flavour for five minutes.","Add the hot stock and bring to the boil, then add the rice.","Cook, stirring, for 16 minutes: it should stay looser than a risotto.","Finish with parmesan and parsley."],"o":"It isn't a risotto and it isn't a soup: you eat it with a spoon, but it holds its shape."},"60":{"n":"Bigoli in salsa","p":["Slice the onions very thinly and stew them in oil over a very low heat for 25 minutes with two tablespoons of water.","They should melt down without ever colouring.","Add the anchovies and melt them, crushing them with the spoon.","Cook the bigoli and toss them in the sauce with a little cooking water.","Season generously with pepper and finish with parsley."],"o":"No cheese, no garlic: it's a meatless fasting dish and its strength is its simplicity."},"61":{"n":"Canederli in broth","p":["Cut the bread into cubes and moisten with lukewarm milk; leave to rest for 20 minutes.","Brown the chopped onion with the diced speck.","Add everything to the bread with eggs, flour, salt and pepper, and mix by hand.","Shape into balls the size of a mandarin, wetting your hands.","Cook them in boiling stock for 15 minutes, over a gentle heat so they don't break.","Serve in the broth with chives."],"o":"If the mixture is too soft, add flour a spoonful at a time: one too many makes them hard."},"62":{"n":"Frico with potatoes","p":["Boil the potatoes, peel them and roughly crush them.","Soften the sliced onion in butter.","Add the potatoes and the coarsely grated cheese, stirring.","Press into the pan and cook over a medium heat for 10 minutes until a crust forms.","Turn it over with the help of a plate and cook for another 8 minutes."],"o":"The Montasio should be half young and half aged: the first melts and stretches, the second gives flavour."},"63":{"n":"Trofie with pesto and potatoes","p":["Make the pesto by pounding basil, garlic, pine nuts and salt, then add cheese and oil.","In the same water cook the cubed potatoes, then after five minutes the green beans, and finally the trofie.","Drain everything together, keeping back a ladle of water.","Dress in a serving bowl with the pesto loosened with the cooking water."],"o":"Potatoes and green beans aren't an extra: in the Genoese version they are part of the dish."},"64":{"n":"Tagliatelle al ragù","p":["Finely chop the onion, carrot and celery and soften them in oil and butter for 15 minutes.","Add the chopped pancetta, then the meat, and brown over a high heat until well coloured.","Deglaze with the red wine and let it evaporate completely.","Add the passata, season with salt and cook for two hours over a very low heat, adding hot water if needed.","In the last half hour pour in the milk: it tenderises the meat and rounds off the acidity.","Toss with the tagliatelle and serve with parmesan."],"o":"Ragù isn't made in half an hour. Two hours is the minimum; three is better."},"65":{"n":"Pappa al pomodoro","p":["Fry the garlic in plenty of oil, then add the passata and cook for 15 minutes.","Add the sliced bread, broken into pieces, and the hot stock.","Stir with a wooden spoon until the bread breaks down completely, about 15 minutes.","Turn off the heat, add the basil and leave to rest for half an hour.","Serve warm with plenty of raw oil."],"o":"It's eaten warm or cold, never piping hot. And it's better the next day."},"66":{"n":"Strangozzi with truffle","p":["Warm the oil with the crushed garlic and melt the anchovies over a very low heat.","Remove the garlic and turn off the heat.","Grate or chop the truffle and add it to the warm (never hot) oil.","Cook the pasta, drain it and toss it in the sauce with a little cooking water."],"o":"Black truffle isn't cooked: above 50 °C it loses everything."},"67":{"n":"Vincisgrassi","p":["Make a slow ragù with soffritto, meat, chopped chicken livers and passata, cooked for two hours.","Make the béchamel by heating butter and flour and adding hot milk in a thin stream, stirring.","Blanch the pasta sheets if they aren't fresh.","Layer béchamel, pasta, ragù and parmesan in a dish, five or six layers.","Bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes and rest for 15 minutes before cutting."],"o":"The chicken livers are what set vincisgrassi apart from any ordinary lasagne."},"68":{"n":"Amatriciana","p":["Brown the guanciale strips in a cold pan with no fat until crisp. Set aside.","Deglaze the pan with white wine and let it evaporate.","Add the passata and chilli, salt lightly and cook for 15 minutes.","Drain the pasta al dente and toss it in the sauce.","Off the heat, return the guanciale and stir in the pecorino."],"o":"No onion and no garlic. Put the guanciale back at the end or it loses its crunch."},"69":{"n":"Arrosticini","p":["Cut the meat into 1 cm cubes, alternating lean and fatty parts.","Thread the cubes onto skewers, about eight per skewer.","Cook over hot embers or on a very hot griddle, three minutes per side.","Salt only at the end and serve at once with toasted bread."],"o":"Fat alternating with lean isn't a flaw: it's what keeps them tender."},"70":{"n":"Cavatelli with mussels","p":["Open the mussels in a covered pot and strain the liquid.","Shell two-thirds of them and keep the rest whole.","Fry garlic and chilli, add the cherry tomatoes and crush them.","Pour in the strained liquid and cook for five minutes.","Cook the cavatelli, drain them al dente and finish cooking them in the sauce with the mussels."],"o":"Never add salt: the mussel liquid already brings all the salt you need."},"71":{"n":"Orecchiette with turnip tops","p":["Trim the turnip tops, keeping the florets and tender leaves.","Cook them in the pasta water, adding them five minutes before the orecchiette.","Meanwhile heat oil, garlic and chilli and melt the anchovies.","Toast the crumbled bread in a separate pan until golden.","Drain the pasta and greens together, toss in the sauce and finish with the crunchy breadcrumbs."],"o":"Toasted breadcrumbs are the poor man's cheese: don't skip them, they give the dish its kick."},"72":{"n":"Peperoni cruschi with potatoes","p":["Boil the potatoes, peel them and cut them into large chunks.","Heat plenty of oil in a pan and fry the peperoni cruschi for just a few seconds: three or four are enough.","Drain them immediately on paper; they will turn very crisp as they cool.","In the same oil sauté the potatoes with garlic until they colour.","Crumble the peppers over the top and serve hot."],"o":"Cruschi burn in an instant and turn bitter: count seconds, not minutes."},"73":{"n":"Pasta with 'nduja","p":["Soften the chopped onion in oil over a gentle heat.","Add the 'nduja and melt it completely, stirring.","Add the passata and cook for 15 minutes: the sauce will turn orange and glossy.","Toss the pasta in the sauce with a little cooking water.","Finish with grated ricotta salata, which softens the heat."],"o":"'Nduja is already salty and very hot: taste before adding salt."},"74":{"n":"Pasta con le sarde","p":["Boil the wild fennel for 10 minutes, drain it keeping the water, and chop it.","Soften the onion, add the cleaned sardines and let them break down.","Add the fennel, soaked raisins, pine nuts and saffron dissolved in a little cooking water.","Cook the bucatini in the fennel water.","Toss together and finish with breadcrumbs toasted in a pan."],"o":"The fennel cooking water is half the flavour: never throw it away."},"75":{"n":"Artichoke caponata","p":["Clean the artichokes and keep them in water acidulated with lemon.","Cut them into wedges and fry them in hot oil, then drain.","Stew onion and celery, then add olives and capers.","Return the artichokes, add vinegar and sugar and let it evaporate over a high heat.","Leave to cool completely before serving."],"o":"Like aubergine caponata, it's best eaten the next day."},"76":{"n":"Malloreddus alla campidanese","p":["Crumble the sausage out of its casing and brown it in a pan without oil.","Add the chopped onion and soften it in the fat.","Pour in the passata and the saffron dissolved in a little water, season with salt and cook for 30 minutes.","Cook the malloreddus and toss them in the sauce.","Serve with plenty of pecorino."],"o":"Saffron isn't an affectation: it belongs to Sardinian tradition as much as to Milanese."},"77":{"n":"Seadas","p":["Knead semolina, lard and lukewarm water into a smooth dough and leave to rest for 30 minutes.","Melt the cheese over a bain-marie with the lemon zest and let it set on a plate.","Roll the dough thinly, cut out discs and enclose a disc of cheese between two sheets.","Seal the edges well with a pastry wheel.","Fry in hot oil until golden and serve at once with warm honey drizzled over."],"o":"The cheese should be slightly sour: if it's too fresh it won't melt properly and tastes of nothing."},"78":{"n":"Zuppa valdostana","p":["Blanch the savoy cabbage, cut into strips, for 10 minutes.","Toast the slices of bread in butter.","In an ovenproof dish layer bread, cabbage and sliced fontina, three layers.","Pour in hot stock almost to cover and sprinkle with cinnamon.","Bake at 180 °C for 30 minutes until the top is golden and bubbling."],"o":"A pinch of cinnamon is traditional, and it completely changes the dish."},"79":{"n":"Cavatelli with beans and mussels","p":["Open the mussels and strain the liquid, shelling most of them.","Heat oil and garlic, add the cherry tomatoes and the cooked beans.","Add the mussel liquid and cook for 10 minutes, crushing a few beans.","Cook the cavatelli and add them to the soup with the mussels.","Finish with parsley and a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Sea and land together: a home-cooking dish from Molise and Puglia."},"80":{"n":"Ratatouille","p":["Cut all the vegetables into cubes of the same size.","Cook each vegetable separately in a pan with oil, salting it at the end.","In a casserole stew the onion and garlic, then add the tomatoes and cook for 15 minutes.","Combine all the vegetables, add the thyme and cook covered for 20 minutes over a gentle heat.","Serve warm or cold, with a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Cooking the vegetables separately is tedious, but it's the difference between a ratatouille and a minestrone."},"81":{"n":"Quiche lorraine","p":["Line the tin with the shortcrust pastry, prick the base and chill for 20 minutes.","Brown the diced pancetta until golden and drain it.","Beat the eggs, cream, milk, salt, pepper and nutmeg.","Scatter the pancetta and cheese over the base and pour in the mixture.","Bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes, until puffed and golden. Serve warm."],"o":"Chilling before baking is what stops the pastry shrinking."},"82":{"n":"Tortilla de patatas","p":["Slice the potatoes 3 mm thick and the onion thinly.","Cook them in plenty of oil over a gentle heat for 25 minutes: they should stew, not fry.","Drain them well and add them to the eggs beaten with salt; leave to rest for 10 minutes.","Pour into a pan with a little oil and cook for 5 minutes over a medium heat.","Flip it with a plate and cook for another 4 minutes: the centre should stay soft."],"o":"Spain has been arguing about the onion for a century. With, in my view."},"83":{"n":"Gazpacho","p":["Soak the bread in water and squeeze it out.","Blend the tomatoes, cucumber, pepper, garlic and bread into a cream.","Add oil in a thin stream while blending: it should emulsify and turn paler.","Adjust with vinegar, salt and a little cold water.","Pass through a sieve if you want it smooth and chill for at least two hours."],"o":"Serve it very cold but not icy: below 6 °C you can't taste anything."},"84":{"n":"Greek salad","p":["Cut the tomatoes into large wedges and the cucumber into thick half-moons.","Slice the onion very thinly and leave it in cold water for 10 minutes to mellow it.","Add the olives and toss with oil, salt and oregano.","Lay the feta on top in thick slices, without mixing it in.","Finish with more oregano and oil."],"o":"Feta isn't crumbled or mixed in: it sits on top, in a single piece."},"85":{"n":"Hummus","p":["Warm the chickpeas in a little water: they blend much better when warm.","Blend them with tahini, lemon juice, garlic, cumin and salt.","Add cold water a spoonful at a time while blending until it turns pale and fluffy.","Serve spread across the plate with the back of a spoon.","Finish with oil, paprika and a few whole chickpeas."],"o":"Cold water while blending is the trick that makes it creamy rather than pasty."},"86":{"n":"Vegetable couscous","p":["Stew the onion, then add the cubed vegetables, starting with the hardest.","Add the spices, the chickpeas and water just to cover, and cook for 20 minutes.","Bring 350 ml of salted water to the boil with a drizzle of oil.","Pour it over the couscous, cover and leave to swell for 5 minutes, then fluff with a fork.","Serve the couscous with the vegetables and their broth on the side."],"o":"The broth is served on the side, so everyone decides how much to add."},"87":{"n":"Cantonese fried rice","p":["Cook the rice, drain it and let it cool: day-old rice is even better.","Make a thin omelette with the beaten eggs and cut it into strips.","Stir-fry the peas and diced ham in a very hot pan with a little oil.","Add the rice and stir-fry over a very high heat for three minutes, keeping it moving.","Add the egg, spring onion and soy sauce, mix and serve at once."],"o":"Cold rice is a must: hot rice goes sticky and turns to glue."},"88":{"n":"Pad thai","p":["Soak the noodles in lukewarm water for 20 minutes, until pliable but firm.","Make the sauce by dissolving sugar, fish sauce and lime juice together.","Stir-fry the prawns in a very hot pan, then push them to one side and scramble the eggs.","Add the drained noodles and the sauce, tossing over a very high heat for two minutes.","Add the bean sprouts and spring onion, and serve with chopped peanuts and lime."],"o":"Prepare everything beforehand: cooking takes three minutes and there's no time to chop anything."},"89":{"n":"Chicken curry","p":["Brown the bite-sized chicken pieces in a pan and set aside.","Stew the chopped onion, garlic and ginger for 10 minutes.","Add the curry powder and toast it for a minute: this step makes it fragrant rather than dusty.","Add the passata and coconut milk and cook for 15 minutes.","Return the chicken and continue for 10 minutes until the sauce coats the spoon."],"o":"Ground spices should always be toasted in fat before any liquid goes in."},"90":{"n":"Chilli con carne","p":["Brown the meat over a high heat until well browned, then set aside.","Stew the onion, garlic and pepper, add the cumin and chilli and toast them for a minute.","Return the meat and add the passata and a glass of water.","Cook for an hour over a low heat, then add the beans and continue for 20 minutes.","The teaspoon of cocoa at the end adds depth without being recognisable."],"o":"Unsweetened cocoa is the secret almost every Texan recipe has and doesn't admit to."},"91":{"n":"Guacamole","p":["Mash the avocado flesh with a fork, leaving it chunky.","Add the lime juice straight away: it stops it going brown.","Add very finely chopped onion, deseeded diced tomato and chilli.","Season with salt and finish with coriander.","Serve within an hour, with cling film pressed onto the surface."],"o":"Never blend it: guacamole should have pieces in it, otherwise it's supermarket dip."},"92":{"n":"Goulash","p":["Slice the onions and stew them in oil or lard for 20 minutes: you need almost twice as much onion as meat.","Take off the heat, add the paprika and stir: if it burns it turns bitter.","Return to the heat with the meat, the passata and water to cover.","Cook covered over a very low heat for an hour and a half.","Add the potatoes in chunks and continue for 30 minutes."],"o":"Paprika always goes in off the heat: that rule is what separates goulash from disaster."},"93":{"n":"Fish and chips","p":["Cut the potatoes into thick chips and parboil them for 5 minutes, then dry them.","Make the batter with flour, cold beer and a pinch of salt, leaving it lumpy.","Fry the chips a first time at 160 °C, drain them and raise the oil to 190 °C.","Dip the fish in the batter and fry for 5 minutes until golden.","Fry the chips again at 190 °C for two minutes and serve with salt and vinegar."],"o":"Double-frying the chips and ice-cold beer in the batter are the two things that matter."},"94":{"n":"Miso soup","p":["Soak the wakame in cold water for 10 minutes.","Bring a litre of water almost to the boil, without letting it boil.","Dissolve the miso in a cup with a little hot water, then add it to the pot.","Add cubed tofu and the seaweed and heat for a minute, never letting it boil.","Serve with sliced spring onion."],"o":"Miso must never boil: it loses its aroma and its live cultures."},"95":{"n":"French onion soup","p":["Slice the onions very thinly and stew them in butter over a very low heat for 40 minutes.","They should turn golden and sweet: it's the only step that counts, and it can't be rushed.","Sprinkle with the flour, stir for two minutes, then deglaze with the wine.","Pour in the hot stock and cook for another 20 minutes.","Fill the bowls, lay toasted bread on top and cover it with gruyère.","Grill until the cheese is golden and melting."],"o":"Forty minutes of onions is the minimum. At twenty they still taste of raw onion."},"96":{"n":"Boeuf bourguignon","p":["Marinate the meat in the wine with carrots, garlic and herbs for at least four hours, ideally overnight.","Drain the meat and dry it well, then brown it over a very high heat a little at a time.","Brown the diced pancetta, add the onions and sprinkle with flour.","Return the meat, pour in the strained marinade wine and add the vegetables.","Cook covered over a very low heat for two and a half hours.","In the last half hour add the mushrooms, sautéed separately."],"o":"Drying the meat after marinating is essential: wet meat doesn't brown, it boils."},"97":{"n":"Coq au vin","p":["Brown the chicken pieces in butter until the skin is well golden, then set aside.","In the same fat brown the pancetta and onions, add the flour and stir for two minutes.","Return the chicken, pour in the wine and add garlic, carrots and thyme.","Cook covered over a gentle heat for an hour and a quarter.","Sauté the mushrooms separately and add them for the last ten minutes.","If the sauce is too thin, take out the chicken and reduce over a high heat."],"o":"The wine must be drinkable: a bad wine stays bad when cooked, just more concentrated."},"98":{"n":"Gratin dauphinois","p":["Slice the potatoes 2 mm thick on a mandoline and don't wash them: the starch is needed to bind.","Heat the milk and cream with crushed garlic, salt, pepper and nutmeg.","Add the potatoes and cook for ten minutes over a gentle heat, stirring gently.","Pour into a buttered ovenproof dish and level it well.","Bake at 160 °C for an hour, until the top is golden and a knife slides in with no resistance."],"o":"No cheese: a true dauphinois is just potato, cream and patience."},"99":{"n":"Salade niçoise","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins and the green beans separately, keeping them firm.","Hard-boil the eggs for 9 minutes and cut them into wedges.","Arrange sliced potatoes, green beans, tomato wedges and thinly sliced onion on the plate.","Add drained tuna, olives, anchovies and eggs.","Dress with oil, salt, pepper and a tablespoon of vinegar, without tossing."],"o":"It isn't tossed, it's composed. Tossed, it becomes a rice salad without the rice."},"100":{"n":"Moules marinière","p":["Clean the mussels, scraping the shells and pulling off the beards.","Soften the very finely chopped onion in butter without colouring.","Pour in the wine and simmer for two minutes.","Add the mussels, cover and cook over a high heat for 5 minutes, shaking the pot.","Take out the mussels, add the cream to the liquid and let it reduce for a minute.","Pour the sauce over the mussels and finish with parsley."],"o":"The cream is optional, and in Normandy they argue about that too. Without it, it's drier and tastes more of the sea."},"101":{"n":"Ham and cheese crêpes","p":["Whisk the eggs, flour, milk and a pinch of salt into a smooth batter and leave it to rest for 30 minutes.","Stir in the lukewarm melted butter.","Heat a non-stick pan and pour in a small ladle of batter, swirling to spread it thinly.","Cook for a minute on each side: the first crêpe always gets thrown away, that's the law.","Fill with ham and cheese, fold into a triangle and heat in the pan until the cheese melts."],"o":"Resting the batter relaxes the gluten: without it, the crêpes tear."},"102":{"n":"Tarte tatin","p":["Peel the apples, halve and core them and sprinkle them with lemon.","In an ovenproof pan melt the sugar and butter into a light caramel.","Pack in the apples, rounded side down, and cook for 20 minutes over a medium heat.","Cover with the pastry, tucking the edges inside, and prick it.","Bake at 190 °C for 30 minutes.","Wait five minutes and turn it out onto a plate in one quick, decisive move."],"o":"Wait five minutes, no more: after that the caramel sets and the tart won't come out."},"103":{"n":"Crème brûlée","p":["Heat the cream with the split vanilla pod without letting it boil, and leave to infuse for 15 minutes.","Beat five yolks with 80 g of sugar without whisking them up: it mustn't foam.","Pour in the strained cream in a thin stream, stirring gently.","Pour into ramekins and bake in a bain-marie at 150 °C for 40 minutes: the centre should barely wobble.","Chill in the fridge for at least four hours.","Sprinkle with sugar and caramelise with a blowtorch or under a very hot grill."],"o":"If you whisk the yolks, bubbles form and the surface won't stay smooth."},"104":{"n":"Baked ratatouille","p":["Make a sauce by stewing onion and garlic and adding two diced tomatoes, for 15 minutes.","Spread it over the base of an ovenproof dish.","Slice the aubergines, courgettes and tomatoes 3 mm thick and stand them upright, alternating and overlapping.","Season with oil, salt, pepper and thyme.","Cover with baking paper and bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes, uncovering for the last ten."],"o":"This is the version you see in films: prettier and simpler than the traditional pan-cooked one."},"105":{"n":"Chicken with almonds","p":["Cut the chicken into strips and mix with a tablespoon of soy sauce and the flour.","Toast the almonds in a dry pan until golden and set aside.","Heat a drizzle of oil over a very high heat and stir-fry the chicken for three minutes, then remove it.","Stir-fry the pepper, spring onion and chopped ginger for two minutes: they should stay crunchy.","Return the chicken, add the remaining soy and half a glass of water, and let it thicken.","Add the almonds only at the end, off the heat."],"o":"Add the almonds at the end: in the sauce they go soft and lose their point."},"106":{"n":"Sweet and sour pork","p":["Cut the pork into bite-sized pieces, dip in beaten egg and then in flour.","Fry in hot oil until golden and drain on kitchen paper.","Make the sauce by dissolving sugar, vinegar, passata and half a glass of water.","Stir-fry the peppers and onion over a high heat for two minutes, then add the cubed pineapple.","Pour in the sauce, let it thicken and add the pork only at the very last moment."],"o":"The pork goes in at the end, or the coating softens and disappears."},"107":{"n":"Stir-fried noodles with vegetables","p":["Boil the noodles for a minute less than the packet says, drain and rinse under cold water.","Toss them with a drizzle of oil so they don't stick.","Heat a wok or wide pan over a very high heat with a little oil.","Stir-fry garlic and ginger for ten seconds, then the hard vegetables, then the tender ones.","Add the noodles and soy sauce and stir-fry for two minutes, keeping everything moving."],"o":"The secret is a very high heat and an uncrowded pan: if you pack it, the vegetables boil."},"108":{"n":"Beef with broccoli","p":["Slice the beef very thinly across the grain and mix with soy sauce and flour for 15 minutes.","Blanch the broccoli florets for three minutes and run them under cold water.","Stir-fry the meat in a very hot pan for two minutes, then remove it.","Stir-fry garlic and ginger, add the broccoli and the oyster sauce with a little water.","Return the meat and let it thicken for a minute."],"o":"Cutting across the grain and marinating with starch is what makes the meat tender in Chinese restaurants."},"109":{"n":"Steamed dumplings","p":["Mix the flour with 150 ml of boiling water, knead for five minutes and rest for 30 minutes, covered.","Finely chop the cabbage, salt it and squeeze it well.","Mix it with the minced pork, spring onion, ginger and soy sauce.","Roll the dough very thinly and cut out 8 cm discs.","Fill them and close into half-moons, pleating the edge.","Steam for 12 minutes on perforated baking paper."],"o":"Boiling water in the dough is what makes it elastic and translucent."},"110":{"n":"Tonkatsu","p":["Pound the pork slices lightly and nick the fatty edge in several places so they don't curl.","Coat in flour, egg and breadcrumbs, pressing well.","Fry in oil at 170 °C for 6-7 minutes, turning only once.","Drain on a rack, never on paper: the underside would go soggy.","Shred the cabbage very finely and serve it raw alongside, with the sauce."],"o":"Finely shredded raw cabbage isn't a garnish: it cuts the richness between mouthfuls."},"111":{"n":"Teriyaki salmon","p":["Make the sauce by dissolving sugar and soy sauce with two tablespoons of water and the grated ginger.","Cook the rice and keep it warm.","Heat a pan with a little oil and cook the salmon skin side down for five minutes.","Turn it, pour in the sauce and let it reduce for three minutes, basting the fish with a spoon.","Serve on the rice with sliced spring onion."],"o":"The sauce reduces quickly: if it dries out too much it turns bitter."},"112":{"n":"Onigiri","p":["Cook short-grain rice and let it cool slightly, covered with a damp cloth.","Mix the drained tuna with the soy sauce.","Wet your hands with salted water and take a handful of rice.","Make a hollow, add the filling and close it up, shaping it into a triangle.","Wrap a strip of nori around the base and serve at room temperature."],"o":"Wet, salted hands are everything: dry, the rice sticks and won't hold its shape."},"113":{"n":"Yakitori","p":["Cut the chicken into bite-sized pieces and thread them onto skewers, alternating with chunks of spring onion.","Make the sauce by heating soy sauce, sugar, ginger and a glass of water and reducing it by half.","Cook the skewers on a very hot griddle or grill for five minutes, turning often.","In the last two minutes brush with the sauce several times, letting it caramelise.","Serve at once with the remaining sauce."],"o":"Only brush on the sauce at the end: added earlier, the sugar makes it burn."},"114":{"n":"Simple ramen","p":["Heat the stock with sliced ginger, crushed garlic and soy sauce and let it simmer for 20 minutes.","Cook the eggs for exactly 6½ minutes, then cool them in iced water.","Poach the chicken breast in the stock for 12 minutes, then slice it.","Cook the noodles separately and drain them.","Assemble the bowl: noodles, boiling strained broth, chicken, half an egg, spring onion and seaweed."],"o":"A 6½-minute egg has a set white and a creamy yolk: it's the heart of the dish."},"115":{"n":"Falafel","p":["Use dried chickpeas soaked for 24 hours, never boiled: this is the rule that decides everything.","Blend the drained raw chickpeas with onion, garlic, herbs, cumin, salt and pepper into a grainy mixture.","Add the flour and leave to rest in the fridge for 30 minutes.","Shape into flattened balls with wet hands.","Fry in oil at 170 °C for 4 minutes, until dark outside and green inside."],"o":"With tinned chickpeas, falafel fall apart in the oil. No trick will save them."},"116":{"n":"Tabbouleh","p":["Cover the bulgur with cold water and leave it to swell for 30 minutes, then squeeze it out.","Chop the parsley very finely with a knife: in real tabbouleh it's the main ingredient, not the bulgur.","Cut the tomatoes into very small dice, removing the seeds.","Mix everything with chopped spring onion and mint.","Dress with plenty of lemon juice, oil and salt, and leave to rest for half an hour."],"o":"The right ratio is three parts parsley to one of bulgur, not the other way round."},"117":{"n":"Baba ganoush","p":["Cook the whole aubergines over an open flame or under the grill until the skin is black and the flesh collapses.","Let them cool slightly in a closed bag, then peel them and leave them to drain for 20 minutes.","Mash the flesh with a fork, without blending.","Add tahini, lemon juice, crushed garlic, cumin and salt.","Serve with plenty of oil and a few parsley leaves."],"o":"The charred skin is the dish: in a closed oven you get just another aubergine purée."},"118":{"n":"Shakshuka","p":["Stew the onion and pepper strips for 15 minutes.","Add garlic, cumin and paprika and toast them for a minute.","Add the chopped tomatoes and cook for 15 minutes until the sauce is thick.","Make six hollows and crack the eggs into them.","Cover and cook for 6 minutes: whites set, yolks runny.","Finish with coriander and bread for scooping."],"o":"It comes to the table in the pan and is shared: that's half the point."},"119":{"n":"Chicken tagine with olives and lemon","p":["Rub the chicken with grated ginger, cumin, saffron dissolved in a little water, salt and pepper.","Leave it to take on the flavours for at least an hour.","Brown the chicken in oil, add the sliced onions and let them soften.","Add a glass of water, cover and cook over a very low heat for an hour.","In the last 15 minutes add the olives and sliced lemon.","Finish with fresh coriander and serve with bread."],"o":"The traditional lemon is preserved in salt: if you can't find it, sliced fresh lemon is fine."},"120":{"n":"Harira","p":["Brown the meat with chopped onion and celery.","Add passata, cinnamon, salt and pepper, and cook for ten minutes.","Add the lentils, chickpeas and two litres of water, and cook for 45 minutes.","Mix the flour into a cup of cold water and pour it in slowly, stirring: it's what thickens the soup.","Continue for ten minutes and finish with plenty of chopped coriander and parsley."],"o":"It's the soup that breaks the Ramadan fast: it should be thick and comforting, not watery."},"121":{"n":"Lamb kofta","p":["Chop the onion very finely and squeeze it in a cloth to remove the water.","Mix the minced lamb with the onion, garlic, parsley, spices, salt and pepper.","Knead the mixture for five minutes: here you want it to become sticky, unlike meatballs.","Shape it into cylinders around the skewers, wetting your hands.","Cook on the grill or in a very hot pan, three minutes per side."],"o":"Water from the onion is why kofta fall apart on the grill."},"122":{"n":"Ceviche","p":["Cut the fish into 2 cm cubes and keep it in the fridge.","Slice the onion very thinly and leave it in cold water for ten minutes.","Squeeze the limes and mix the juice with chopped chilli and salt: this is the leche de tigre.","Pour it over the fish and leave to marinate for ten minutes, no longer.","Add the onion and coriander and serve at once with sliced boiled potato."],"o":"Ten minutes is enough. Any longer and the fish 'cooks' too much and turns rubbery. And it must be frozen first: 24 hours at -20 °C."},"123":{"n":"Papa a la huancaína","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins and let them cool slightly, then peel and slice them thickly.","Hard-boil the eggs for 9 minutes.","Sauté the onion and deseeded chillies in a little oil for five minutes.","Blend with the cheese and milk into a smooth, thick cream.","Coat the potatoes with the sauce and garnish with egg wedges and olives."],"o":"The sauce should coat, not run: adjust it with milk a spoonful at a time."},"124":{"n":"Beef empanadas","p":["Knead flour, cold butter, an egg, 150 ml of water and salt; rest for an hour in the fridge.","Stew the chopped onions for 15 minutes, then add the meat and brown it.","Season with cumin and paprika and leave to cool completely.","Add two chopped hard-boiled eggs and the olives to the cold filling.","Cut out discs, fill them, fold into half-moons and seal with a braided edge or a fork.","Brush with egg and bake at 200 °C for 20 minutes."],"o":"The filling must be cold: hot, it melts the butter in the pastry and the empanadas burst open."},"125":{"n":"Steak with chimichurri","p":["Chop the parsley, garlic and chilli with a knife: chimichurri isn't blended.","Mix with oregano, vinegar, salt and plenty of oil, and leave to rest for at least an hour.","Bring the meat to room temperature and dry it well.","Cook it on a very hot grill or griddle without moving it, four minutes per side.","Let it rest for five minutes before slicing, then serve with the chimichurri."],"o":"Chimichurri improves after a few hours: make it before the meat, not after."},"126":{"n":"Easy feijoada","p":["Brown the cubed pork and the sausage pieces in a large pot.","Add chopped onion and garlic and soften them in the fat.","Add the beans, the bay leaf and water to cover by three fingers.","Cook covered over a very low heat for an hour and a half, crushing a few beans to thicken.","Serve with white rice and orange segments, which cut the richness."],"o":"The orange isn't decoration: it cuts the fattiness, and in Brazil it's never missing."},"127":{"n":"Paella valenciana","p":["Heat the oil in the paella pan and brown the chicken pieces until golden on all sides.","Push the meat to the edge and fry the green beans, then add the grated tomatoes and paprika.","Pour in the boiling stock with the saffron and the beans and simmer for 20 minutes.","Scatter the rice evenly over the pan, and from now on never stir again.","Cook for 10 minutes over a high heat and 8 over a gentle heat, adding the rosemary.","Turn up the heat for 40 seconds to form the socarrat, then cover and rest for 5 minutes."],"o":"Paella isn't stirred or covered while it cooks: it isn't a risotto."},"128":{"n":"Patatas bravas","p":["Cut the potatoes into irregular 3 cm cubes.","Fry them a first time at 140 °C for 8 minutes without colouring, and drain.","Make the sauce by frying garlic and paprika, adding passata, vinegar and chilli and cooking for 15 minutes.","Fry the potatoes again at 190 °C until golden and crisp.","Salt straight away and serve with the sauce on the side or poured over at the last moment."],"o":"Double-frying is essential: the first cooks the inside, the second makes the crust."},"129":{"n":"Pulpo a la gallega","p":["Bring a pot of water to the boil with onion and bay leaf.","Dip the octopus in three times for a few seconds before leaving it in: this curls the tentacles.","Cook for 45 minutes over a gentle heat, then turn off the heat and leave it in the water for another 15.","Boil the potatoes in the same water and slice them thickly.","Cut the octopus into rounds with scissors and lay it on the potatoes.","Season with coarse salt, paprika and plenty of oil."],"o":"Let the octopus cool in its own water: taken out straight away, it turns rubbery."},"130":{"n":"Pisto manchego","p":["Cut all the vegetables into small, even cubes.","Stew the onion in plenty of oil over a gentle heat for 15 minutes.","Add the peppers, then the aubergines, then the courgettes, giving each ten minutes.","Add the peeled chopped tomatoes and cook for half an hour until everything is very soft.","Serve with a fried egg on top."],"o":"Unlike ratatouille, here the vegetables break down: it should become a savoury jam."},"131":{"n":"Pollo al ajillo","p":["Separate all the garlic cloves and crush them without peeling.","Heat plenty of oil and fry them gently until golden, then set aside.","In the same oil brown the chicken over a high heat until well coloured.","Return the garlic, deglaze with the wine and add the bay leaf.","Cover and cook for 20 minutes over a gentle heat, then uncover and reduce the sauce.","Finish with chopped parsley."],"o":"Two heads of garlic sound like too much. They aren't: cooked in their skins they turn sweet."},"132":{"n":"Crema catalana","p":["Heat the milk with the lemon zest and cinnamon and leave to infuse for 15 minutes.","Beat the yolks with the sugar and flour.","Pour in the strained milk in a thin stream, stirring.","Return to a gentle heat and cook, stirring, until it thickens, without letting it boil.","Pour into ramekins and chill for at least three hours.","Sprinkle with sugar and caramelise with a blowtorch."],"o":"Unlike crème brûlée, this uses milk and flour, not cream: it's lighter and less delicate."},"133":{"n":"Bacalhau à Brás","p":["Cut the potatoes into very thin matchsticks and fry until golden. Drain on kitchen paper.","Flake the salt cod by hand, removing bones and skin.","Stew the thinly sliced onions with the garlic for 15 minutes.","Add the cod and cook for five minutes, then add the potato straws.","Pour in the beaten eggs and stir over a low heat until they set but stay creamy.","Finish with olives and parsley."],"o":"The eggs shouldn't cook through: the dish is served soft, almost runny."},"134":{"n":"Seafood cataplana","p":["Purge the clams in salted water for two hours.","In the cataplana make a bed of sliced onion, garlic, pepper and tomato slices.","Add the sliced chorizo, a drizzle of oil and the wine.","Lay the shellfish and prawns on top, seal tightly and cook for 15 minutes over a medium heat.","Open it only at the table, so as not to lose the fragrant steam.","Finish with fresh coriander."],"o":"A cataplana steams under pressure: opening it early means throwing away half the dish."},"135":{"n":"Caldo verde","p":["Stew onion and garlic, add the potatoes in chunks and the stock, and cook for 25 minutes.","Blend everything into a smooth, not too thick cream.","Slice the cavolo nero into very thin strips, almost like hair.","Drop it into the soup and cook for just five minutes: it should stay green and crisp.","Brown the sliced chorizo and divide it among the bowls with plenty of oil."],"o":"Coarsely cut cabbage ruins everything: it should be almost a thread."},"136":{"n":"Frango piri piri","p":["Blend the chillies, garlic, paprika, vinegar, lemon juice, salt and oil into a runny marinade.","Score the chicken down to the bone and rub it with half the marinade.","Leave in the fridge for at least three hours, ideally overnight.","Roast at 200 °C for 40 minutes, or cook on the grill.","Brush with the remaining marinade every ten minutes."],"o":"Scoring to the bone lets the marinade in where the meat is thickest."},"137":{"n":"Pastéis de nata","p":["Heat the milk with lemon zest and cinnamon and leave to infuse.","Make a syrup with the sugar and 100 ml of water, bringing it to 100 °C.","Mix the flour into a little cold milk, then add the strained hot milk and cook until thick.","Pour in the syrup in a thin stream, let it cool slightly and stir in the yolks.","Roll up the puff pastry, cut 1 cm discs and press them into the moulds with your thumb.","Fill three-quarters full and bake at 250 °C for 12 minutes: dark spots on the surface are right."],"o":"The oven must be scorching. At a low temperature the custard dries out instead of scorching."},"138":{"n":"Moussaka","p":["Slice the aubergines, salt them and leave to drain for an hour, then grill or bake them.","Slice the potatoes and bake them for ten minutes.","Make the meat sauce with onion, meat, passata and a pinch of cinnamon, cooking for 30 minutes.","Make the béchamel and, off the heat, stir in two yolks and the cheese.","Layer potatoes, aubergines, meat sauce and more aubergines in a dish, finishing with the béchamel.","Bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes and rest for half an hour before cutting."],"o":"Cinnamon in the meat sauce is what sets moussaka apart from a parmigiana with meat."},"139":{"n":"Pork souvlaki","p":["Cut the pork into 3 cm cubes.","Marinate it with oil, lemon juice, oregano, crushed garlic, salt and pepper for at least two hours.","Thread the cubes onto skewers, not too tightly.","Cook on a very hot grill for 12 minutes, turning often.","Serve with warmed bread and yoghurt dressed with oil and oregano."],"o":"Cubes packed too tightly don't cook at the sides: give them room to breathe."},"140":{"n":"Tzatziki","p":["Grate the cucumbers, salt them and leave to drain in a sieve for 30 minutes.","Squeeze them with your hands: they should be almost dry.","Mix the yoghurt with crushed garlic, vinegar, oil and salt.","Add the cucumbers and the chopped mint.","Leave in the fridge for at least an hour before serving."],"o":"If you don't squeeze the cucumbers, after half an hour you've got soup."},"141":{"n":"Spanakopita","p":["Blanch the spinach for a minute, drain and squeeze hard.","Chop it and mix with crumbled feta, sliced spring onion, eggs and dill.","Don't add salt: feta is already very salty.","Line the tin with one sheet of pastry, pour in the filling and cover with the second.","Score the top into diamonds and brush with oil.","Bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes."],"o":"Squeeze the spinach until it seems too dry: it still releases water in the oven."},"142":{"n":"İmam bayıldı","p":["Peel the aubergines in alternating stripes and slit them lengthways without cutting all the way through.","Salt them and leave to drain for half an hour, then brown them in oil on all sides.","Stew the sliced onions with the garlic for 20 minutes, until very sweet.","Add the diced tomatoes, parsley and sugar and cook for ten minutes.","Open the slits and fill the aubergines, then lay them in a dish with a little water and oil.","Bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes and serve warm or cold."],"o":"The name means 'the imam fainted' and, legend has it, it was over the amount of oil. Don't skimp."},"143":{"n":"Lahmacun","p":["Knead flour, yeast, 250 ml of lukewarm water and salt, and leave to rise for an hour.","Blend the meat, onion, pepper, tomatoes, paprika, salt and pepper into a smooth paste.","Divide the dough into eight balls and roll them out very thinly.","Spread a very thin layer of topping right up to the edge.","Bake at 250 °C for 6-8 minutes, until the edges are crisp.","Serve with parsley and lemon, rolled up."],"o":"The topping should be a thin veil: spread it thick and it stays raw and the base goes soggy."},"144":{"n":"Cheese and egg pide","p":["Make the dough as for pizza and leave it to rise for an hour and a half.","Divide into four and roll out into long ovals.","Spread the cheeses, leaving a 3 cm border free.","Fold the long edges inwards and pinch both ends into a boat shape.","Bake at 250 °C for 10 minutes, then crack an egg into the centre and bake for 4 more minutes.","Brush the edges with melted butter as soon as it comes out of the oven."],"o":"The egg goes in halfway through, otherwise it's hard-cooked before the dough is done."},"145":{"n":"Baklava","p":["Roughly chop the walnuts and mix with cinnamon and two tablespoons of sugar.","In a tin, alternate layers of thin pastry brushed with melted butter and layers of walnuts.","Cut into diamonds before baking, right through to the bottom.","Bake at 170 °C for 45 minutes until golden.","Make the syrup by boiling sugar, water, honey and lemon for ten minutes.","Pour cold syrup over hot baklava, or hot syrup over cold: never both hot."],"o":"Syrup and pastry at the same temperature make a mush. One of the two must be cold."},"146":{"n":"Potato foam (siphon)","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel them while still hot and push them through a sieve.","Heat the milk, cream and butter without letting it boil.","Add to the sieved potato, stirring into a smooth, fairly runny cream.","Season with salt and nutmeg, then strain through a fine sieve: not a single lump must get into the siphon.","Fill the siphon no more than two-thirds full, add one charge and shake well.","Keep in a bain-marie at 60 °C and serve straight away."],"o":"A single lump blocks the nozzle: straining isn't optional."},"147":{"n":"Agnolotti del plin","p":["Knead flour and eggs for ten minutes and rest for half an hour, covered.","Brown the meats in a little butter, deglaze with the wine and cook covered for an hour, then add the stewed cabbage.","Chop everything very finely and bind with eggs and parmesan.","Roll the pasta very thin and place small nuggets of filling 1 cm apart.","Fold the pasta over and pinch between each filling: the plin is exactly that pinch.","Cook for two minutes and dress with melted butter and sage."],"o":"The plin is made with thumb and forefinger, not a pastry wheel: that's what gives the shape."},"148":{"n":"Tajarin with butter and sage","p":["Knead the flour with the yolks: real tajarin use only yolks, thirty per kilo.","Work until smooth and rest for an hour wrapped in cling film.","Roll the pasta thin, flour it and roll it up, then cut with a knife into 2 mm strips.","Cook for just under a minute in salted water.","Dress with butter melted with sage, without letting it fry."],"o":"With all those yolks the pasta is yellow and rich: keep the sauce to a minimum."},"149":{"n":"Brasato al Barolo","p":["Marinate the meat in the wine with the vegetables and spices for at least twelve hours.","Drain the joint and dry it well, then brown it in oil and butter on all sides.","Add the vegetables from the marinade and let them take on flavour.","Pour in the strained wine, cover and cook over a very low heat for three hours.","Take out the meat, blend the sauce and return the meat, sliced, to the sauce for ten minutes."],"o":"Dry the meat after marinating: wet, it won't brown and the braise stays pale."},"150":{"n":"Panissa vercellese","p":["Cook the beans with the pork rind and keep them in their broth.","Fry the chopped onion and lardo, add the crumbled sausage and brown it.","Toast the rice, deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate.","Add the bean broth a ladle at a time for sixteen minutes.","Halfway through add the beans and finish: it should stay loose and wavy, not dry."],"o":"It's a risotto, but it isn't finished with butter and cheese: the creaminess comes from the beans."},"151":{"n":"Bonet","p":["Make a caramel with 80 g of sugar and a little water and pour it into the mould.","Beat the eggs with the remaining sugar, then add cocoa and crumbled amaretti.","Pour in the hot milk in a thin stream with the rum, stirring.","Pour into the caramel-lined mould and bake in a bain-marie at 160 °C for 50 minutes.","Chill for at least four hours before turning out."],"o":"The bain-marie isn't optional: with direct heat the egg splits and the bonet turns grainy."},"152":{"n":"Russian salad","p":["Boil the finely diced potatoes and carrots separately, keeping them firm, and the peas.","Cool everything by spreading it out on a tray: it mustn't stay warm and damp.","Make the mayonnaise by whisking a yolk with oil in a thin stream and the vinegar.","Dress the vegetables with the mayonnaise, keeping some back for the top.","Chill for at least three hours and decorate with hard-boiled eggs and capers."],"o":"The vegetables must be cool and dry: if they're damp, the mayonnaise splits."},"153":{"n":"Carbonade valdostana","p":["Cut the meat into strips and dust lightly with flour.","Brown it in butter over a high heat, a little at a time, and set aside.","Stew the sliced onions in the same fat for twenty minutes.","Return the meat, pour in the wine and add bay leaf and cinnamon.","Cook covered over a very low heat for two hours: the sauce should turn dark and thick.","Serve with polenta."],"o":"By weight, the onions should be almost half the meat: they are what makes the sauce."},"154":{"n":"Polenta concia","p":["Bring a litre and a half of salted water to the boil and pour in the cornmeal in a stream, stirring.","Cook for 45 minutes over a low heat, stirring often.","In the last ten minutes add the diced fontina and half the butter.","Stir until stringy, then finish with parmesan and the rest of the butter, melted until nutty brown."],"o":"Brown butter poured over at the last moment is the signature of the dish."},"155":{"n":"Ossobuco alla milanese","p":["Nick the edges of the ossobuco in several places so they don't curl.","Flour them and brown them in butter until golden on both sides.","Add the chopped onion and soften it, then deglaze with the wine.","Add the stock, cover and cook over a gentle heat for an hour and a half.","Make the gremolata with chopped lemon zest, garlic and parsley, and add it for the last two minutes."],"o":"The gremolata goes in off the heat: cooked, it loses all its fragrance."},"156":{"n":"Casoncelli bergamaschi","p":["Make the pasta dough with flour and eggs and let it rest.","Brown the meat and sausage, then add the soaked bread, parmesan, and chopped amaretti and raisins.","Cut out discs, fill them and close like a sweet wrapper, pressing the ends.","Cook for four minutes in salted water.","Dress with melted butter, crispy pancetta and sage."],"o":"Amaretti and raisins in a meat filling are Bergamo's signature: they seem out of place and they aren't."},"157":{"n":"Pizzoccheri valtellinesi","p":["Knead the two flours with lukewarm water, roll out 3 mm thick and cut into short, wide ribbons.","Boil the potatoes in chunks and the savoy cabbage in strips for ten minutes, then add the pizzoccheri.","Drain everything together and layer in a serving bowl with the diced cheese and parmesan.","Cook the butter with garlic and sage until golden and pour it over sizzling hot.","Mix only at the end, at the table."],"o":"The butter must be very hot: that's what melts the cheese between the layers."},"158":{"n":"Cassoeula","p":["Brown the ribs and sausage in a large pan, then set aside.","Make a soffritto with onion, carrot and celery.","Return the meats, deglaze with the wine and add the passata.","Cook covered for an hour, then add the savoy cabbage cut into strips, a little at a time.","Continue for another hour over a very low heat: the cabbage should break down completely.","Skim the fat off the surface before serving."],"o":"It's made the day before and skimmed when cold: that's how it has always been eaten."},"159":{"n":"Mondeghili","p":["Soak the bread in the milk and squeeze it out.","Mix with the meat, chopped mortadella, eggs, parmesan, parsley and lemon zest.","Shape into flattened meatballs and coat them in breadcrumbs.","Fry them in butter over a medium heat, three minutes per side.","Serve hot, without sauce."],"o":"Born to use up leftover boiled meat: if you have some, use it instead of raw mince."},"160":{"n":"Spinach spätzle","p":["Blanch the spinach, squeeze it well and blend it with the eggs and milk.","Add the flour and work into a thick batter that falls in ribbons.","Push the batter through a spätzle maker or a colander with large holes straight into boiling water.","Lift out the spätzle as soon as they float.","Toss in a pan with browned speck, onion and cream, and finish with parmesan."],"o":"If you don't have a spätzle press, a coarse grater set over the pot works fine."},"161":{"n":"Apple strudel","p":["Knead flour, egg, melted butter and lukewarm water into an elastic dough, then rest for an hour.","Slice the apples thinly and mix with sugar, cinnamon, soaked raisins and pine nuts.","Toast the breadcrumbs in butter: they soak up the apple juice.","Stretch the dough very thinly on a cloth, sprinkle with the breadcrumbs and spread the apples.","Roll it up with the help of the cloth, seal the ends and brush with butter.","Bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes."],"o":"Stretch the dough until you can read a newspaper through it: that's the real test."},"162":{"n":"Trentino goulash","p":["Stew the sliced onions over a gentle heat for twenty minutes.","Take off the heat and stir in the paprika: if it burns it turns bitter.","Add the cubed meat, the wine, the passata and the spices.","Cook covered over a very low heat for two hours.","Finish with grated lemon zest and serve with polenta."],"o":"Compared with the Hungarian one, this has lemon zest, which lightens the dish."},"163":{"n":"Baccalà mantecato","p":["Simmer the stockfish in unsalted water for twenty minutes, then drain and bone it while hot.","Put it in a bowl and start working it with a wooden spoon.","Pour in the oil in a thin stream while beating, as for mayonnaise.","Keep going until it turns white and fluffy and triples in volume.","Season with pepper and serve on crostini or polenta."],"o":"Beat it by hand while warm: in a blender it heats up and separates."},"164":{"n":"Baccalà alla vicentina","p":["Stew the sliced onions in plenty of oil for half an hour, then add the melted anchovies.","Flour the pieces of stockfish and arrange them in a casserole, alternating with the onions.","Sprinkle with parmesan and parsley, and cover with milk and more oil.","Cook over a very low heat for three and a half hours without ever stirring.","Every now and then gently swirl the pot with a circular motion."],"o":"Swirling without stirring is called 'pipare': if you stir, the fish falls apart."},"165":{"n":"Venetian-style liver","p":["Slice the onions very thinly and stew them in oil and butter for thirty minutes with a little water.","They should become almost a cream, without colouring.","Turn up the heat and add the liver in thin strips.","Cook for two minutes at most, turning: any longer and it gets tough.","Deglaze with the wine, salt only now and finish with parsley."],"o":"Salt at the start toughens liver: it always goes in at the end."},"166":{"n":"Sarde in saor","p":["Clean the sardines, flour them and fry them in hot oil. Drain on kitchen paper and salt them.","Slice the onions and stew them in oil for twenty minutes without colouring.","Pour in the vinegar and let it bubble for five minutes.","In a container layer sardines, onions, soaked raisins and pine nuts.","Cover and leave in the fridge for at least twenty-four hours."],"o":"On the same day it tastes of nothing: saor needs two or three days."},"167":{"n":"Radicchio risotto","p":["Slice the radicchio and keep a handful aside for the end.","Stew the onion in butter, add the radicchio and let it wilt.","Toast the rice, deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate.","Add the stock a ladle at a time for sixteen minutes.","Beat in cold butter and parmesan, and finish with the remaining raw radicchio."],"o":"Raw radicchio at the end keeps the bitterness lively: all cooked, it turns sweetish."},"168":{"n":"Jota triestina","p":["Cook the beans with the ribs, bay leaf and plenty of water for an hour.","Separately, stew the sauerkraut with caraway and a little water for twenty minutes.","Add the potatoes in chunks to the beans and cook for another twenty minutes.","Crush half the beans and potatoes against the side to thicken.","Make a roux with flour and garlic in a little oil and stir it into the soup.","Add the sauerkraut and let the flavours come together for ten minutes."],"o":"The roux is what binds it and sets it apart from any ordinary bean soup."},"169":{"n":"Cjarsons","p":["Boil the potatoes, mash them and knead with the flour into a soft dough.","Make the filling with ricotta, soaked raisins, mint, cinnamon and sugar.","Roll the dough thinly, cut out discs, fill them and fold into half-moons.","Cook for three minutes in salted water.","Dress with melted butter and grated smoked ricotta."],"o":"Sweet and savoury together: it's the cooking of Carnia, not a measuring mistake."},"170":{"n":"Farinata (chickpea flatbread)","p":["Whisk the chickpea flour into the water until there are no lumps.","Leave to rest for at least four hours, ideally overnight.","Skim off the foam that forms on top: it's what makes it hard to digest.","Add salt and half the oil, then pour into a tin greased with the rest.","Bake at 250 °C for 15 minutes, until golden and speckled."],"o":"The layer should be half a centimetre thick: any thicker and it turns into a pudding."},"171":{"n":"Torta pasqualina","p":["Knead flour, water, oil and salt and divide into six balls. Rest for an hour.","Blanch the chard, squeeze it and sauté with the onion, then mix with ricotta, parmesan and marjoram.","Roll three very thin sheets into the tin, brushing oil between each.","Pour in the filling and make four hollows, cracking an egg into each.","Cover with the other three sheets, seal and prick only the top one.","Bake at 180 °C for 50 minutes."],"o":"The original had thirty-three sheets, one for each year of Christ's life. Six will do."},"172":{"n":"Pansoti with walnut sauce","p":["Knead flour, eggs, white wine and water, and let it rest.","Blanch the chard, squeeze it, chop it and mix with ricotta, parmesan and marjoram.","Cut out squares, fill them and fold into plump triangles.","For the sauce, pound walnuts, garlic, bread soaked in milk and oil into a cream.","Cook the pansoti for three minutes and dress with the sauce loosened with cooking water."],"o":"Walnut sauce is never heated: just loosen it with the cooking water."},"173":{"n":"Ligurian-style rabbit","p":["Brown the rabbit pieces in oil with garlic and rosemary until well golden.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Add cherry tomatoes, Taggiasca olives and pine nuts.","Cover and cook over a gentle heat for an hour, adding a little water if it dries out.","Uncover for the last ten minutes to reduce the sauce."],"o":"The rabbit's liver, chopped and added halfway through, thickens the sauce: that's the Ligurian trick."},"174":{"n":"Lasagne alla bolognese","p":["Make the ragù with soffritto, pancetta, meat, wine and passata, cooking for at least two hours.","Make the béchamel by melting butter and flour and adding hot milk in a thin stream.","Blanch the pasta sheets if they aren't fresh and lay them out on a cloth.","Layer béchamel, pasta, ragù and parmesan in a dish, six layers.","Finish with béchamel and plenty of parmesan.","Bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes and rest for twenty minutes before cutting."],"o":"Little ragù per layer and lots of layers: the opposite of what almost everyone does."},"175":{"n":"Tortellini in brodo","p":["Brown the pork loin in butter and let it cool.","Very finely chop the meat, prosciutto and mortadella, then bind with egg, parmesan and nutmeg.","Rest the filling in the fridge for at least twelve hours: that's what gives it its flavour.","Roll the pasta thinly, cut 3 cm squares and fill each with a hazelnut-sized piece.","Fold into a triangle, then wrap around your finger, joining the tips.","Cook for three minutes in boiling capon broth."],"o":"Resting the filling is the step nobody mentions, and it changes everything."},"176":{"n":"Passatelli in brodo","p":["Mix breadcrumbs, parmesan, eggs, nutmeg and lemon zest.","Knead into a firm, compact mixture and leave to rest for half an hour.","Press it through a passatelli iron or a large-holed potato ricer straight over the boiling broth.","Cut the passatelli into 4 cm lengths with a knife.","Cook for two minutes: they're ready when they float."],"o":"The right proportion is equal weights of breadcrumbs and parmesan: less cheese and they fall apart."},"177":{"n":"Gramigna with sausage","p":["Crumble the sausage out of its casing and brown it in a pan with no fat.","Add the chopped onion and soften it in the sausage fat.","Deglaze with the wine, then add the passata and cook for twenty minutes.","Stir in the cream and turn off the heat.","Toss the gramigna in the sauce and finish with parmesan."],"o":"Cream is traditional here, not some foreign shortcut: it binds the sausage fat."},"178":{"n":"Erbazzone reggiano","p":["Knead flour, lard, water and salt into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Blanch the spinach, squeeze it very hard and chop it.","Sauté spring onion and garlic, then add the spinach and parmesan.","Roll out two thin sheets, fill and close, sealing the edges.","Prick the top, scatter over the diced pancetta and bake at 200 °C for 30 minutes."],"o":"Squeeze the spinach until it seems dry, or the bottom sheet stays raw."},"179":{"n":"Cotoletta alla petroniana","p":["Coat the cutlets in egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry them in butter until golden, then arrange them in a baking dish.","Cover each with a slice of prosciutto and plenty of parmesan.","Pour stock into the bottom of the dish, without touching the coating.","Bake at 200 °C for ten minutes, until the cheese has melted."],"o":"It's the Milanese cutlet brought to Bologna: same base, but finished in the oven with prosciutto and parmesan."},"180":{"n":"Zuppa inglese","p":["Make pastry cream with yolks, sugar, flour and lemon-scented milk.","Divide it in two and stir sifted cocoa into one half.","Dip the savoiardi in alchermes and layer them with the two creams.","Finish with a layer of cream and chill for at least six hours."],"o":"Only the name is English: it was born at the Este court as an imitation of trifle."},"181":{"n":"Panzanella","p":["Soak the stale bread in cold water for ten minutes, then squeeze it well and crumble it.","Slice the onion thinly and leave it in cold water for half an hour to mellow it.","Cut the tomatoes and cucumber into pieces and add them to the bread.","Dress with oil, vinegar, salt and hand-torn basil.","Chill for an hour before serving."],"o":"Really squeeze the bread: if water is left in, the panzanella turns to mush."},"182":{"n":"Peposo alla fornacina","p":["Put the cubed meat in a terracotta pot with unpeeled garlic, bay leaves and peppercorns.","Cover with red wine and bring to a simmer.","Cook uncovered over a very low heat for three hours, until the liquid reduces to a thick sauce.","Add salt only in the last half hour.","Serve with toasted Tuscan bread."],"o":"No browning and no soffritto: everything goes in raw and you wait. It was born in the brick kilns of Impruneta."},"183":{"n":"Cacciucco","p":["Fry garlic and chilli in plenty of oil.","Start with the octopus and cuttlefish in pieces, deglaze with red wine and cook for forty minutes.","Add the passata and continue for twenty minutes.","Add the fish steaks, then the shellfish, and finally the mussels: each has its own timing.","Serve on toasted bread rubbed with garlic."],"o":"The Livorno rule: five kinds of fish, as many as the c's in 'cacciucco'."},"184":{"n":"Fagioli all'uccelletto","p":["Heat plenty of oil with unpeeled garlic and sage.","As soon as the garlic is fragrant, add the passata and cook for ten minutes.","Add the cooked beans and let them take on flavour for twenty minutes over a gentle heat.","Season with salt and pepper and let it rest before serving."],"o":"The name ('little bird style') comes from the sage, the herb small birds used to be cooked with."},"185":{"n":"Tuscan chicken liver crostini","p":["Clean the chicken livers, removing the bile and sinews.","Stew the chopped onion, add the livers and brown for ten minutes.","Deglaze with the wine and let it evaporate.","Chop with a knife or blitz briefly, then return to the pan with anchovies and capers.","Stir in the butter and loosen with stock into a soft spread.","Spread on bread lightly moistened in the stock."],"o":"Dipping the bread briefly in stock before spreading is the detail that makes them special."},"186":{"n":"Castagnaccio","p":["Sift the chestnut flour and whisk in the water to a smooth, runny batter.","Add a pinch of salt, half the oil and the soaked raisins.","Pour into an oiled tin, just under 1 cm deep.","Scatter with pine nuts and rosemary needles and drizzle with the remaining oil.","Bake at 200 °C for 35 minutes: the surface should crack."],"o":"No sugar: the sweetness all comes from the chestnut flour. If you add some, it's no longer castagnaccio."},"187":{"n":"Torta al testo","p":["Knead flour, water, oil, bicarbonate of soda and salt into a smooth dough.","Leave to rest, covered, for thirty minutes.","Roll out discs 1 cm thick.","Cook on the testo or a very hot cast-iron griddle, eight minutes per side, pricking it as soon as it puffs up.","Cut into wedges, split them and fill with cured meats or sautéed greens."],"o":"Heat the testo for a long time over a medium flame: if it's too hot it burns outside and stays raw inside."},"188":{"n":"Umbricelli all'aglione","p":["Knead semolina and water for ten minutes and rest for half an hour.","Roll into strands as thick as a pencil and 20 cm long.","Slice the garlic and stew it in plenty of oil over a very low heat for ten minutes, without colouring.","Add the passata and chilli and cook for twenty-five minutes.","Toss the umbricelli in the sauce with a little cooking water."],"o":"Aglione from the Valdichiana is mild and digestible: eight cloves isn't a mistake."},"189":{"n":"Olive all'ascolana","p":["Pit the olives with a spiral cut, keeping the flesh in one piece.","Brown the three meats, then chop them very finely and bind with egg, parmesan, nutmeg and lemon zest.","Wrap each olive spiral around a hazelnut-sized ball of filling, reforming its shape.","Coat in flour, egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in oil at 170 °C until golden."],"o":"The spiral cut is the whole skill: done well, the olive closes up as if it were whole."},"190":{"n":"Brodetto all'anconetana","p":["Open the mussels and clams and strain the liquid.","Fry garlic in plenty of oil, add the cuttlefish and cook for twenty minutes.","Deglaze with vinegar: it's the hallmark of the Ancona brodetto.","Add the passata and the shellfish liquid, then the fish steaks, starting with the firmest.","Never stir: shake the pot.","Serve with toasted bread."],"o":"Thirteen kinds of fish in the orthodox version. With four it's still delicious."},"191":{"n":"Coniglio in porchetta","p":["Bone the rabbit, or have it boned, keeping it in one opened-out piece.","Chop wild fennel, garlic, rosemary and pancetta and spread them over the inside.","Roll it up and tie it tightly with string.","Brown in a pan on all sides, then deglaze with the wine.","Roast at 180 °C for an hour, basting with the juices every twenty minutes."],"o":"Wild fennel is what makes it 'in porchetta': cultivated fennel isn't the same."},"192":{"n":"Pasta alla gricia","p":["Cut the guanciale into 5 mm thick strips.","Put it in a cold pan with no fat and brown over a medium heat until crisp and the fat has melted.","Lift it out and set aside, leaving the fat in the pan.","Drain the pasta very al dente and toss it in the fat with a ladle of cooking water.","Off the heat stir in grated pecorino and plenty of pepper, adding water until creamy.","Return the crispy guanciale only at the very end."],"o":"It's carbonara without egg and amatriciana without tomato: the mother of both."},"193":{"n":"Carciofi alla romana","p":["Clean the artichokes by removing the tough leaves, trim and peel the stems, then keep them in water and lemon.","Chop wild mint, garlic and parsley with salt and pepper and push it between the leaves.","Stand them upside down in a narrow casserole so they stay upright.","Half-cover with water, wine and plenty of oil.","Cook covered over a gentle heat for thirty to forty minutes, until a knife slides in with no resistance.","Serve warm with their cooking juices."],"o":"Roman mentuccia isn't ordinary mint: it's smaller and peppery, and it changes the dish."},"194":{"n":"Carciofi alla giudia","p":["Use Roman artichokes, round and without thorns.","Clean them by turning the artichoke against the knife in a spiral until you reach the heart.","Tap the artichoke on the board to open the leaves like a flower.","Fry in oil at 130 °C for ten minutes to cook them through.","Raise the oil to 180 °C and plunge them back in for a minute: the second frying makes them crisp.","Salt and serve at once."],"o":"Two fryings at different temperatures: the first cooks, the second opens the leaves and makes them glassy."},"195":{"n":"Vignarola","p":["Clean the artichokes and cut them into wedges, keeping them in acidulated water.","Brown the guanciale and sliced spring onion.","Add the artichokes and cook for ten minutes with a little water.","Add the shelled broad beans and peas and continue for fifteen minutes.","In the last five minutes add the lettuce in strips and the wild mint."],"o":"It's the dish of the Roman spring: artichokes, broad beans and peas overlap for only a few weeks a year."},"196":{"n":"Coda alla vaccinara","p":["Blanch the oxtail in boiling water for ten minutes and drain.","Brown it in oil with a soffritto of onion, carrot and celery.","Deglaze with the wine, add the passata and water to cover.","Cook covered over a very low heat for three and a half hours: the meat should fall off the bone.","In the last half hour add celery in chunks, pine nuts, raisins and cocoa."],"o":"Pine nuts, raisins and cocoa are the historic version of this offal-cut dish: they seem odd and they complete it."},"197":{"n":"Saltimbocca alla romana","p":["Pound the slices of veal thin and lay a slice of prosciutto and a sage leaf on each.","Secure with a cocktail stick without rolling: Roman saltimbocca are flat.","Flour only the meat side.","Cook in butter meat side down for two minutes, then turn for one minute.","Deglaze with the wine, take out the meat and let the juices reduce."],"o":"They aren't rolled and aren't salted: the prosciutto already brings all the salt needed."},"198":{"n":"Abbacchio a scottadito","p":["Marinate the lamb cutlets with oil, crushed garlic and rosemary for at least an hour.","Lightly pound the meaty part to thin it.","Heat the grill or griddle until scorching.","Cook for three minutes per side without moving them.","Salt only at the end and serve at once, to be eaten with your fingers."],"o":"The name says how they're eaten: straight off the grill, hot enough to burn your fingers."},"199":{"n":"Puntarelle alla romana","p":["Pull off the inner shoots of the catalogna chicory and cut them lengthways into thin strips.","Put them in iced water for an hour: they curl by themselves.","Pound the anchovies with the garlic into a paste.","Beat in the vinegar and oil in a thin stream.","Drain and dry the puntarelle well and dress only just before serving."],"o":"The curl comes from the iced water, not the cut: without that step they stay straight and bitter."},"200":{"n":"Trippa alla romana","p":["Simmer the cleaned tripe for an hour in water with bay leaf, then cut it into strips.","Make a soffritto with onion, carrot and celery.","Add the tripe, deglaze with the wine and let it take on flavour.","Add the passata and cook for an hour over a gentle heat.","Finish with plenty of wild mint and grated pecorino."],"o":"Without wild mint and pecorino it's just any tripe: they're what make it Roman."},"201":{"n":"Supplì al telefono","p":["Make a ragù with onion, meat and passata, cooking for forty minutes.","Cook the rice in the ragù loosened with water, like a risotto, until dry.","Stir in parmesan and an egg, then spread it out and leave to cool completely.","Shape into croquettes with a stick of mozzarella in the middle.","Coat in egg and breadcrumbs and fry at 175 °C."],"o":"The 'telephone' is the string of mozzarella that stretches when you pull them apart: if it doesn't, the supplì is wrong."},"202":{"n":"Roman pasta e ceci","p":["Warm the oil with unpeeled garlic and rosemary, then melt the anchovies.","Add the chickpeas and the passata and cover with their cooking water.","Blend a third of the chickpeas and return them to the pot.","Cook the pasta straight in it, stirring often.","Turn off the heat, remove the rosemary and leave to rest for ten minutes."],"o":"You can't taste the anchovies but they give a deep savouriness: it's the trick of Roman peasant cooking."},"203":{"n":"Artichoke frittata","p":["Clean the artichokes, slice them thinly and keep them in water and lemon.","Sauté them with garlic and oil for ten minutes, season with salt and let them cool slightly.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, parsley, salt and pepper.","Add the artichokes and pour into a non-stick pan.","Cook over a low heat for seven minutes, then flip with a lid and cook for four more."],"o":"Slice the artichokes really thinly: in thick wedges the frittata won't hold together."},"204":{"n":"Maccheroni alla chitarra","p":["Knead semolina, flour and eggs and leave to rest for half an hour.","Roll out sheets as thick as the gap between the chitarra's strings, about 2 mm.","Lay the sheet on the strings and press with the rolling pin: square-section spaghetti drop through.","Make a ragù with onion, meat and passata, cooking for an hour.","Cook the pasta for two minutes and dress with the ragù and pecorino."],"o":"The square section holds sauce far better than a round one: that's the whole point of the chitarra."},"205":{"n":"Pallotte cacio e ova","p":["Soak the bread in water and squeeze it out well.","Mix with grated pecorino, eggs, and chopped garlic and parsley.","Shape into walnut-sized balls and fry them in hot oil.","Make a simple sauce with oil, passata and basil.","Drop the balls into the sauce and cook for twenty minutes over a gentle heat."],"o":"Meatless 'meatballs' born of poverty: the pecorino must be plentiful and well aged."},"206":{"n":"Pampanella molisana","p":["Cut the pork into even pieces, keeping some fat.","Make the marinade with sweet paprika, chilli, chopped garlic, salt and vinegar.","Rub it well into the meat and leave to rest for at least two hours.","Put it in a covered dish and roast at 180 °C for an hour.","Uncover and raise to 200 °C for the last fifteen minutes."],"o":"The vinegar poured over at the end is what gives it character: don't skip it."},"207":{"n":"Pasta alla Genovese","p":["Slice the onions very thinly: there should be at least twice as much onion as meat.","Put the meat, onions and chopped carrot and celery in a pot with a little oil and no water.","Cook covered over a very low heat for three hours: the onions release all their water.","Uncover, deglaze with the wine and continue until the sauce is creamy and dark golden.","Dress the hand-broken ziti with the sauce and plenty of cheese."],"o":"There's nothing Genoese about it: the name comes from Ligurian cooks who worked in the port of Naples."},"208":{"n":"Pasta alla scarpariello","p":["Fry the unpeeled garlic with the chilli, then remove it.","Add the halved cherry tomatoes and cook for ten minutes over a high heat, crushing a few.","Cook the pasta very al dente and drain it, keeping the water.","Toss it in the sauce and take off the heat.","Stir in both cheeses and cooking water, mixing until creamy."],"o":"The name comes from the cobblers, who made it on Mondays with cheese left over from Sunday."},"209":{"n":"Polpo alla luciana","p":["Put the octopus in a terracotta casserole with oil, garlic, chilli and cherry tomatoes.","Don't add water: the octopus must cook in its own.","Seal with baking paper and a heavy lid, and cook over a very low heat for an hour.","Only check at the end: every time you lift the lid you lose steam.","Add olives and capers and finish with parsley."],"o":"The Neapolitan saying goes that octopus cooks in its own water. Heavy lid and patience."},"210":{"n":"Ragù napoletano","p":["Brown the whole pieces of meat and the sausage in oil over a high heat.","Add the sliced onions and let them soften slowly.","Deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate completely.","Pour in the passata and bring it barely to a simmer, the famous 'pippiare'.","Cook uncovered for four or five hours, stirring every half hour with a wooden spoon.","The meat is served as a main course and the sauce dresses the pasta."],"o":"It must never boil: it should just blip every so often, gently. That's what Neapolitans call 'pippiare'."},"211":{"n":"Impepata di cozze","p":["Clean the mussels, scraping the shells.","Put them in a wide pot with plenty of coarsely ground pepper and nothing else.","Cover and cook over a high heat for five minutes, shaking the pot.","Turn off the heat as soon as they open and add parsley and lemon juice.","Serve with toasted bread to mop up the juices."],"o":"No garlic, no wine, no tomato: just mussels and pepper. It's as essential as a dish gets."},"212":{"n":"Golden fried anchovies","p":["Clean the anchovies, removing head and backbone, and open them out like a book without separating the halves.","Coat them in flour and then in beaten salted egg.","Fry in hot oil a few at a time, one minute per side.","Drain on kitchen paper and salt only now.","Serve at once with lemon wedges."],"o":"'Indorate' means dipped in egg, not breadcrumbed: breadcrumbs have nothing to do with it."},"213":{"n":"Gattò di patate","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel them while hot and pass them through a ricer.","Mix in butter, milk, eggs, parmesan, salt and pepper.","Spread half the mixture in a buttered tin sprinkled with breadcrumbs.","Fill with drained mozzarella and diced ham, then cover with the rest.","Sprinkle with breadcrumbs and flakes of butter and bake at 200 °C for 40 minutes."],"o":"Cut the mozzarella and let it drain for an hour: otherwise the gattò floods."},"214":{"n":"Stuffed peppers","p":["Cut the tops off the peppers and remove the seeds and membranes.","Crumble the bread and toast it in a pan with oil.","Add olives, capers, melted anchovies, garlic, parsley and diced cherry tomatoes.","Fill the peppers without packing too tightly and put the tops back on.","Bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes, drizzling with oil halfway through."],"o":"Don't pack the filling: it should stay soft and soak up the pepper's juices."},"215":{"n":"Stuffed escarole","p":["Wash the escarole well and blanch it for two minutes, then squeeze it.","Fry garlic and anchovies, then add olives, capers, pine nuts and soaked raisins.","Add the escarole and let it take on flavour for ten minutes over a medium heat.","Serve warm, on its own or as the filling for escarole pie."],"o":"The play is between the bitterness of the escarole, the saltiness of the anchovies and the sweetness of the raisins."},"216":{"n":"Babà","p":["Knead flour, yeast and eggs, one at a time, working for a long time until the dough is elastic.","Add the soft butter in three additions and finally a pinch of salt.","Let it rise until doubled, then fill the moulds a third full.","Let them rise to the rim and bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes.","Make the syrup with water, sugar, lemon zest and rum, and let it cool to lukewarm.","Soak the cold babàs in the warm syrup until swollen and glossy."],"o":"Cold babà, warm syrup: the other way round they fall apart. And the syrup must never be boiling."},"217":{"n":"Pastiera napoletana","p":["Make the shortcrust with flour, cold butter, sugar and eggs and chill for an hour.","Cook the wheat with milk, a knob of butter and lemon zest until creamy. Leave to cool.","Sieve the ricotta and beat it with the sugar, then add the eggs, candied peel, cinnamon and orange-blossom water.","Mix with the wheat and pour into the pastry-lined tin.","Decorate with a lattice of strips and bake at 170 °C for an hour and twenty minutes.","Leave to rest for at least two days before serving."],"o":"It's made on Holy Thursday and eaten on Sunday: the three days' rest do half the work."},"218":{"n":"Zeppole di San Giuseppe","p":["Make choux pastry by bringing water and butter to the boil, adding the flour all at once and drying out the dough.","Off the heat beat in the eggs one at a time, waiting until each is absorbed.","Pipe rings onto baking paper.","Fry in oil at 170 °C or bake at 200 °C for 25 minutes.","Fill with pastry cream and top with a cherry in syrup."],"o":"Fried is the original; baked is lighter. Pipe in the cream only once the zeppole are cold."},"219":{"n":"Ciceri e tria","p":["Knead semolina and lukewarm water, rest for half an hour and roll out 2 mm thick.","Cut short ribbons 1 cm wide: these are the tria.","Set aside a third of the pasta and fry it in hot oil until golden and crisp.","Cook the chickpeas with bay leaf and celery, then season them with fried garlic.","Boil the remaining pasta straight in the chickpeas and serve topped with the fried pasta."],"o":"Half boiled and half fried pasta in the same dish: that's the Salento signature."},"220":{"n":"Fave e cicoria","p":["Soak the split dried broad beans overnight.","Cook them with a potato in pieces and water to cover, without salt, for an hour.","Stir often towards the end: they should break down into a cream.","Beat in oil in a thin stream, as for mash, and salt only now.","Boil the chicory separately and serve it alongside, dressed with raw oil."],"o":"You can't taste the potato but it makes the purée velvety: every Apulian grandmother's trick."},"221":{"n":"Tiella barese","p":["Open the mussels raw with a knife, keeping the half-shell with the mussel and straining the liquid.","Oil the dish and make a layer of sliced onion, then thinly sliced potatoes.","Arrange the mussels in their shells and sprinkle with raw rice, pecorino, parsley and garlic.","Cover with more potatoes and cherry tomatoes, and finish with pecorino and breadcrumbs.","Pour in the mussel liquid and water almost to cover.","Bake at 200 °C for 50 minutes without ever stirring."],"o":"The rice goes in raw and the water must come level with the top layer: it all cooks together, in the oven."},"222":{"n":"Apulian braciole in sauce","p":["Pound the slices of meat thin and top each with pecorino, parsley, garlic and a small piece of pancetta.","Roll up tightly and secure with string or a cocktail stick.","Brown the rolls in oil until golden on all sides.","Deglaze with red wine, then add the passata.","Cook over a very low heat for two and a half hours: the sauce dresses the pasta, the braciole are the main course."],"o":"Like Neapolitan ragù, it's a sauce that makes two courses: it isn't all served together."},"223":{"n":"Pasticciotto leccese","p":["Make the shortcrust with flour, lard, sugar and two eggs, working it as little as possible. Rest for an hour.","Make pastry cream with yolks, sugar, flour and lemon-scented milk.","Line the oval moulds with the pastry, fill with cold cream and cover with a disc of pastry.","Seal the edges well and brush with egg.","Bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes, until golden."],"o":"Lard instead of butter is what makes the pastry as crumbly as it should be."},"224":{"n":"Lagane e ceci","p":["Knead semolina and water, rest for half an hour and roll out 2 mm thick.","Cut strips 2 cm wide and 10 cm long: these are the lagane.","Fry garlic, chilli and rosemary in plenty of oil.","Add the chickpeas with their water and the passata, and cook for twenty minutes.","Add the lagane straight into the soup and cook, stirring."],"o":"It's one of the oldest dishes in Italy: lagane already appear in Horace."},"225":{"n":"Lucanian salt cod","p":["Fry the peperoni cruschi in hot oil for a few seconds and drain them immediately.","In the same oil brown the potato chunks with the garlic.","Flour the pieces of salt cod and fry until golden.","Combine everything in the pan and let it take on flavour for five minutes.","Crumble the cruschi over the top only just before serving."],"o":"Crumble the cruschi at the last moment: on a hot plate they lose their crunch within a minute."},"226":{"n":"Calabrian stuffed aubergines","p":["Halve the aubergines and scoop out the flesh, leaving a 5 mm border.","Salt the shells and leave them to drain for half an hour.","Sauté the diced flesh with garlic, then mix with soaked bread, eggs, pecorino and basil.","Fill the shells and arrange them in a dish on top of tomato sauce.","Bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes, spooning the sauce over them."],"o":"The scooped-out flesh is half the filling: it's never thrown away."},"227":{"n":"Pesce spada alla ghiotta","p":["Stew the onion and chunks of celery in oil for fifteen minutes.","Add cherry tomatoes, olives, capers, pine nuts and raisins and cook for ten minutes.","Lay the swordfish slices on the sauce without stirring.","Cover and cook for eight minutes, turning the fish only once.","Leave to rest off the heat for five minutes before serving."],"o":"Swordfish cooked for more than ten minutes turns dry and stringy: watch the clock."},"228":{"n":"Sagne chine","p":["Make a ragù with onion, meat and sausage, cooking for an hour with the passata.","Hard-boil two eggs and slice them; sauté the artichoke wedges and blanch the peas.","Make tiny meatballs with the leftover meat and fry them.","Layer lasagne sheets, ragù, eggs, scamorza, peas, artichokes and meatballs in a dish.","Finish with ragù and pecorino and bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes."],"o":"It's the lasagne of Calabrian feast days: everything goes in, and that's the point."},"229":{"n":"Arancini","p":["Cook the rice in salted water with saffron until dry, and spread it out to cool.","Make the ragù with onion, meat, peas and passata, cooking for forty minutes. It must be dry.","Bind the cold rice with parmesan and an egg.","Make a hollow in your palm, add ragù and mozzarella, and close into a cone or ball.","Dip in a batter of flour and water, then in breadcrumbs.","Fry at 175 °C until golden."],"o":"Cold rice and dry ragù: those are the two conditions that stop them bursting open."},"230":{"n":"Pasta alla trapanese","p":["Blanch and peel the tomatoes and remove the seeds.","Pound garlic, almonds and basil in a mortar with a pinch of salt.","Add the tomatoes and keep pounding into a coarse cream.","Pour in the oil in a thin stream, still pounding.","Toss the pasta with the raw sauce and pecorino."],"o":"It's Sicilian pesto, brought by Genoese sailors stopping in Trapani: almonds instead of pine nuts."},"231":{"n":"Sarde a beccafico","p":["Clean the sardines, opening them out like a book and removing the backbone but leaving the tail.","Toast the crumbled bread in a pan with oil until golden.","Mix it with soaked raisins, pine nuts, orange juice and sugar.","Fill each sardine and roll it up from head to tail, leaving the tail pointing upwards.","Arrange in a dish with a bay leaf between each one.","Drizzle with orange juice and oil and bake at 180 °C for 20 minutes."],"o":"The upturned tail imitates the beccafico, a small bird: hence the name."},"232":{"n":"Swordfish rolls","p":["Have the swordfish cut into very thin slices and pound them lightly.","Toast the crumbled bread and mix it with diced mozzarella, olives, capers and parsley.","Fill the slices and roll them up.","Thread onto skewers, alternating with bay leaves and onion wedges.","Cook on the grill or in the oven at 200 °C for 15 minutes, drizzling with oil and lemon."],"o":"The slices must be very thin: thick ones won't roll and stay raw inside."},"233":{"n":"Falsomagro","p":["Have the slice of beef butterflied into a thin rectangle.","Make the filling with minced meat, breadcrumbs, an egg, pecorino and salami.","Spread it over the meat and lay whole hard-boiled eggs in a row down the middle.","Roll up tightly and tie with string.","Brown, deglaze with the wine, add passata and peas and cook for two hours over a gentle heat.","Slice only when lukewarm, so you can see the eggs in the centre."],"o":"The Sicilian name is 'farsumagru', fake lean: from the outside it looks like a plain piece of meat."},"234":{"n":"Pasta con i broccoli arriminati","p":["Blanch the broccoli florets and keep the water for cooking the pasta.","Stew the onion, then add the anchovies and melt them.","Add the broccoli, soaked raisins, pine nuts and saffron dissolved in a little water.","Stir for a long time, crushing the broccoli: 'arriminare' means exactly that, stirring at length.","Cook the pasta in the broccoli water and toss it in the sauce."],"o":"'Arriminare' is the key verb: the broccoli must break down into a cream."},"235":{"n":"Panelle","p":["Whisk the chickpea flour into the cold water until there are no lumps.","Cook over a medium heat, stirring constantly, for twenty minutes, until it comes away from the sides.","Add chopped parsley, salt and pepper.","Spread it immediately on a wet surface in a 3 mm layer and leave to cool.","Cut into rectangles and fry in very hot oil until they puff up."],"o":"They're served in a bread roll with a squeeze of lemon. In Palermo they eat bread with bread, and that's fine."},"236":{"n":"Sicilian cannoli","p":["Knead flour, lard, sugar, cocoa and wine into a firm dough. Rest for two hours.","Sieve the ricotta and beat it with the sugar, then leave it to drain in the fridge overnight.","Roll the dough very thinly, cut out ovals and wrap them around the tubes, sealing with egg white.","Fry in oil at 180 °C until golden and blistered, then slide off the tubes.","Fill with the ricotta only when serving, and finish with chocolate chips."],"o":"A cannolo is filled at the last second: filled in advance it goes soggy and becomes something else."},"237":{"n":"Cassata siciliana","p":["Sieve the ricotta with the sugar and leave it to drain overnight, then add the chocolate chips.","Make marzipan (pasta reale) by kneading ground almonds, sugar and a little water, colouring it green.","Line the mould with alternating strips of marzipan and sponge cake.","Fill with the ricotta cream and close with more sponge.","Chill for six hours, then turn out and cover with sugar icing.","Decorate with whole candied fruit."],"o":"The ricotta must be sheep's milk and really well drained: it's the only way the cassata will stand up."},"238":{"n":"Culurgiones","p":["Boil the potatoes, mash them and mix with pecorino, mint and garlic fried in oil.","Make the dough with semolina and lukewarm water and let it rest.","Cut out discs, add the filling and close by pinching alternate sides to form an ear of wheat.","Cook for five minutes in salted water.","Dress with a simple tomato and basil sauce."],"o":"The wheat-ear closure is called 'impuntura' and takes practice: the first ones will come out badly, that's normal."},"239":{"n":"Fregola with clams","p":["Purge the clams in salted water for two hours.","Open them in a covered pan and strain the liquid, shelling two-thirds of them.","Fry garlic and chilli, add the cherry tomatoes and deglaze with the wine.","Pour in the strained liquid and water, bring to the boil and add the fregola.","Cook for fifteen minutes like a risotto, adding liquid a little at a time.","Add the clams and parsley off the heat."],"o":"Fregola is already toasted and releases little starch, so the creaminess comes entirely from the clam liquid."},"240":{"n":"Pane frattau","p":["Make a simple sauce with garlic, passata and basil, and keep it hot and fluid.","Bring salted water to a simmer for moistening the bread.","Dip a sheet of carasau for two seconds and lay it on the plate.","Alternate bread, sauce and pecorino for three or four layers.","Finish with a poached egg on top and more pecorino."],"o":"Two seconds in the water, no more: the carasau should soften without falling apart."},"241":{"n":"Zuppa gallurese","p":["Slice the stale bread and arrange it in layers in an ovenproof dish.","Alternate with sliced fresh cheese, grated pecorino and chopped herbs.","Continue until everything is used, pressing down lightly.","Pour in boiling stock to cover and let it soak in for ten minutes.","Bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes, until the top is golden."],"o":"Despite its name, it isn't a soup: it's cut into slices like a savoury pie."},"242":{"n":"Stocco alla ghiotta","p":["Use pre-soaked stockfish: starting from dry, it takes three or four days with the water changed twice a day.","Stew the onion and chunks of celery in plenty of oil for twenty minutes, without colouring.","Add cherry tomatoes, olives, rinsed capers, pine nuts and raisins and cook for ten minutes.","Lay the pieces of stockfish on the sauce and cover with large chunks of potato.","Add water just to cover, put the lid on and cook over a very low heat for an hour, shaking the pot instead of stirring.","Finish with basil and leave to rest for half an hour before serving."],"o":"Stockfish is air-dried cod; baccalà is salted cod: they aren't the same and can't be swapped without changing the dish."},"243":{"n":"Spaghetti allo scoglio","p":["Purge the clams in salted water for two hours and clean the mussels.","Open the shellfish in a covered pan over a high heat and strain the liquid.","Fry garlic and chilli, add the squid rings and cook for ten minutes.","Add the cherry tomatoes, deglaze with the wine, then pour in the strained liquid.","Cook the pasta very al dente and finish it in the pan with the sauce.","Add the prawns and shellfish only for the last two minutes, with the parsley."],"o":"Seafood has very different cooking times: throw it all in together and either the squid is rubbery or the mussels have vanished."},"244":{"n":"Seafood risotto","p":["Open the mussels and clams and strain the liquid: it will be your stock, topped up with water.","Shell most of the shellfish and set aside.","Stew the chopped onion in oil and toast the rice for two minutes.","Deglaze with the wine, then add the hot stock a ladle at a time.","Halfway through add the cherry tomatoes, and in the last three minutes the seafood.","Finish with raw oil and parsley: no butter or cheese."],"o":"A seafood risotto is never finished with butter and parmesan: raw olive oil is used instead."},"245":{"n":"Cuttlefish with peas","p":["Clean the cuttlefish and cut into 1 cm strips.","Fry onion and garlic, add the cuttlefish and brown for five minutes.","Deglaze with the wine and let it evaporate, then add the passata.","Cover and cook for forty minutes over a gentle heat: the cuttlefish should turn tender.","Add the peas for the last fifteen minutes and finish with parsley."],"o":"Cuttlefish goes from tender to rubbery and back to tender: if it's tough at twenty minutes, keep going."},"246":{"n":"Stuffed squid","p":["Clean the squid, setting aside the tentacles to chop for the filling.","Mix soaked and squeezed bread, chopped tentacles, egg, parmesan, garlic and parsley.","Fill the bodies two-thirds full and close with a cocktail stick: they shrink as they cook.","Brown them in a pan, deglaze with the wine and add the cherry tomatoes.","Cover and cook for thirty minutes over a gentle heat."],"o":"Overfilling is the classic mistake: they burst and the filling ends up in the sauce."},"247":{"n":"Mussel soup","p":["Clean the mussels and set aside.","Fry garlic and chilli in plenty of oil, add the passata and cook for fifteen minutes.","Add the mussels, cover and cook over a high heat for five minutes, shaking the pot.","Discard any that stay closed and finish with parsley.","Serve on toasted bread with plenty of sauce."],"o":"Put the bread in the bowl, not in the pot: in the pot it turns to mush."},"248":{"n":"Seafood salad","p":["Boil the octopus for forty minutes and let it cool in its water.","Cook the squid for eight minutes and the prawns for two, in boiling salted water.","Open the mussels and shell them.","Cut everything into even pieces and add thinly sliced celery.","Dress with oil, plenty of lemon, very finely chopped garlic and parsley.","Chill for at least two hours before serving."],"o":"Each seafood has its own timing and is cooked separately: the only way to keep any from being rubbery."},"249":{"n":"Fritto misto di paranza","p":["Clean the fish and dry it very thoroughly: water is the enemy of frying.","Dredge in fine durum wheat semolina, which gives more crunch than flour.","Shake off the excess in a sieve.","Fry in oil at 180 °C, a little fish at a time, for two or three minutes.","Drain on kitchen paper, salt only now and serve at once with lemon."],"o":"Semolina, not flour: that's the difference between a crisp fry and a soggy one."},"250":{"n":"Baked sea bream with potatoes","p":["Slice the potatoes 3 mm thick, dress with oil, salt and rosemary and spread them in a tin.","Bake at 200 °C for twenty minutes, until they start to colour.","Lay the cleaned bream on top, with garlic and rosemary in the cavity.","Scatter cherry tomatoes and olives around and pour in the wine.","Continue for twenty-five minutes: the fish is ready when the dorsal fin pulls out without resistance."],"o":"The fin test is more reliable than the clock: it depends on thickness, not weight."},"251":{"n":"Sea bass baked in salt","p":["Mix the coarse salt with the egg whites and a little water until it feels like wet sand.","Spread a bed of salt in a tin and lay the unscaled fish on it, with lemon and rosemary in the cavity.","Cover it completely with the rest of the salt, pressing down well.","Bake at 200 °C for thirty minutes.","Crack the crust with the back of a knife and fillet at once, peeling away the skin with the salt stuck to it."],"o":"Don't scale the fish: the scales are the barrier that keeps the salt out of the flesh."},"252":{"n":"Prawns in white wine","p":["Clean the prawns, leaving the shells on for flavour.","Heat oil, sliced garlic and chilli.","Add the prawns over a high heat and sauté for two minutes.","Deglaze with the wine and let it reduce for three minutes.","Finish with parsley and serve with bread to mop up the juices."],"o":"Shells on doubles the flavour: they're peeled at the table, with your hands."},"253":{"n":"Fried salt cod","p":["Cut the salt cod into even pieces and dry it well.","Make a batter with flour and cold water, as thick as custard.","Leave it to rest in the fridge for half an hour.","Dip the pieces in and fry in oil at 175 °C until golden.","Drain on kitchen paper and serve with lemon."],"o":"Cold batter hitting hot oil is what creates the crisp shell."},"254":{"n":"Chicken cacciatore","p":["Brown the chicken pieces in oil with rosemary, skin side down, without moving them.","Take them out and make a soffritto with chopped onion, carrot and celery.","Return the chicken, deglaze with the wine and let it evaporate.","Add the passata and olives, cover and cook for forty minutes.","Uncover for the last ten to reduce the sauce."],"o":"Brown the skin for a long time without moving it: turn it too soon and it stays flabby throughout."},"255":{"n":"Rabbit cacciatore","p":["Soak the rabbit in water and vinegar for an hour to remove the gamey taste.","Dry it and brown it in oil with garlic and rosemary.","Deglaze with the wine, then add the passata, bay leaf and olives.","Cover and cook for fifty minutes over a gentle heat, adding liquid if needed.","Leave to rest for ten minutes before serving."],"o":"Soaking in water and vinegar is the step that wins over even people who say they don't like rabbit."},"256":{"n":"Baked meatloaf","p":["Soak the bread in the milk and squeeze it out.","Mix with the meat, eggs, parmesan, salt and pepper, working it as little as possible.","Spread it on baking paper, fill with ham and mozzarella and roll it up using the paper.","Place in a tin with seasoned potato chunks.","Bake at 180 °C for an hour, turning the potatoes halfway.","Slice only after ten minutes' rest."],"o":"Cut it straight out of the oven and it crumbles: resting lets the juices be reabsorbed."},"257":{"n":"Rare roast beef","p":["Take the meat out of the fridge two hours ahead: it must be at room temperature.","Rub it with mustard, garlic, rosemary, salt and pepper, and tie it.","Sear it in a very hot pan on all sides, three minutes per side.","Roast at 200 °C for twenty minutes: the core should reach 52 °C for rare.","Wrap in foil and rest for fifteen minutes before slicing very thinly."],"o":"Without a thermometer you're guessing and you'll get it wrong: twenty minutes is for a kilo of even shape."},"258":{"n":"Tagliata di manzo","p":["Bring the meat to room temperature and dry it well.","Heat the grill or griddle until smoking.","Cook for four minutes per side without moving or piercing it.","Let it rest for five minutes wrapped in foil.","Slice across the grain, salt now and serve on a bed of rocket with parmesan shavings."],"o":"Salting before cooking draws out the juices: always salt after slicing."},"259":{"n":"Fillet steak with green peppercorns","p":["Brown the medallions in butter, three minutes per side, and keep them warm.","Add the coarsely crushed green peppercorns to the pan juices.","Deglaze with brandy and flambé, or simply let it evaporate.","Pour in stock and cream and let it reduce until it coats the spoon.","Return the meat for a minute and serve with the sauce spooned over."],"o":"Crush the green peppercorns but don't grind them: some whole grains should remain."},"260":{"n":"Straccetti with rocket","p":["Cut the meat across the grain into very thin strips.","Heat the pan with oil and garlic until scorching.","Add the meat and sauté for two minutes, no more.","Deglaze with the wine, season with salt and turn off the heat.","Add the rocket off the heat (it should only wilt) and finish with parmesan and lemon."],"o":"Two minutes is the maximum: straccetti dry out in an instant."},"261":{"n":"Meat rolls in tomato sauce","p":["Pound the slices of meat and fill them with ham, crumbled bread, parmesan, garlic and parsley.","Roll up and secure with a cocktail stick.","Brown them in oil on all sides, then set aside.","Soften the onion, add the passata and return the rolls.","Cook covered for an hour over a gentle heat."],"o":"The sauce that results is perfect for pasta: it's a dish that makes two."},"262":{"n":"Arista (roast pork loin)","p":["Make cuts in the meat and fill them with chopped garlic, rosemary and wild fennel.","Tie it and season generously with salt and pepper.","Brown in a pan on all sides, then transfer to a tin with the potatoes.","Pour in the wine and roast at 180 °C for an hour and ten minutes.","Rest for fifteen minutes before slicing."],"o":"The name comes from the Greek 'aristos', the best: Byzantine bishops in Florence are said to have called it that."},"263":{"n":"Sausages and beans","p":["Prick the sausages and brown them in a pan with no fat until golden.","Take them out and soften onion and garlic with sage in their fat.","Add the passata and cook for ten minutes.","Return the sausages and add the cooked beans.","Cook for twenty minutes over a gentle heat, until the sauce thickens."],"o":"Pricking the sausages makes them lose fat: if you want them juicy, don't, and brown them more gently."},"264":{"n":"Chicken breast pizzaiola","p":["Cut the breast into 1 cm thick slices and brown them for two minutes per side.","Take them out and in the same pan fry the garlic with the passata and oregano.","Cook the sauce for ten minutes, then return the chicken.","Lay sliced mozzarella and capers on top.","Cover and let it melt for five minutes over a low heat."],"o":"Brown the chicken and take it out straight away: if it cooks in the sauce from the start it dries out."},"265":{"n":"Mixed meat skewers","p":["Cut the meat and vegetables into cubes of the same size, about 3 cm.","Alternate meat, sausage, pepper, onion and bay leaves on the skewers.","Season with oil, chopped rosemary, salt and pepper.","Cook on a very hot grill for twelve minutes, turning every three.","Leave to rest for two minutes before serving."],"o":"Same-sized cubes, or some burn while others stay raw: that's all there is to it."},"266":{"n":"Bollito misto","p":["Put the beef into already boiling water with the vegetables: that way the meat stays flavourful.","Cook for two hours over a gentle heat, skimming now and then.","Add the chicken for the last hour.","For the green sauce, blend parsley, bread soaked in vinegar, anchovies, capers and oil.","Serve the meats hot, thickly sliced, with the sauce on the side."],"o":"Meat into boiling water if you want to eat the meat, into cold water if you want the broth. You can't have both."},"267":{"n":"Leftover boiled beef with onions","p":["Slice the onions thinly and stew them in oil for twenty minutes.","Add the leftover boiled beef, cut into thick slices.","Deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate.","Add the passata and bay leaf and cook for fifteen minutes.","Serve with polenta or toasted bread."],"o":"Created so as not to waste yesterday's boiled meat, and it's better than the original."},"268":{"n":"Baked pasta","p":["Make a ragù with onion, meat and passata, cooking for forty minutes.","Hard-boil two eggs and slice them.","Boil the pasta very al dente, draining it four minutes early.","Toss it with the ragù and layer it in a dish with drained mozzarella, eggs and parmesan.","Sprinkle with breadcrumbs and bake at 200 °C for thirty minutes.","Rest for ten minutes before cutting."],"o":"Drain the pasta well ahead of time: in the oven it keeps cooking, absorbing the sauce."},"269":{"n":"Anelletti al forno","p":["Fry the cubed aubergines and drain on kitchen paper.","Make the ragù with onion, meat, peas and passata, cooking for forty minutes.","Boil the anelletti very al dente and toss with the ragù.","Oil the tin and coat it with breadcrumbs: that's what creates the crust around the sides.","Layer pasta, diced caciocavallo and aubergines.","Bake at 200 °C for thirty-five minutes and turn out when lukewarm."],"o":"The breadcrumbs in the tin aren't a whim: they let you turn it out whole, like a cake."},"270":{"n":"Pasta 'ncasciata","p":["Fry the sliced aubergines and use them to line an oiled mould.","Make the ragù with meat and passata, cooking for forty minutes.","Hard-boil the eggs and slice them.","Toss the pasta with the ragù and layer it in the mould with salami, eggs, caciocavallo and basil.","Close with aubergines and bake at 200 °C for forty minutes.","Turn out onto a plate after ten minutes' rest."],"o":"It's Inspector Montalbano's dish: you turn it out upside down and the aubergines become the crust."},"271":{"n":"Gnocchi alla sorrentina","p":["Make a simple sauce with garlic, passata and basil, cooking for twenty minutes.","Boil the gnocchi and drain them as soon as they float.","Toss them in the sauce and transfer to individual ovenproof dishes.","Add well-drained diced mozzarella and parmesan.","Bake at 220 °C for ten minutes, until a crust forms."],"o":"Drain the mozzarella for at least half an hour: its water ruins the dish."},"272":{"n":"Ricotta and spinach cannelloni","p":["Blanch the spinach, squeeze it hard and chop it.","Mix with sieved ricotta, egg, parmesan, salt, pepper and nutmeg.","Make the béchamel and spread a thin layer over the base of the dish.","Pipe the filling onto the pasta sheets, roll them up and lay them side by side.","Cover with béchamel and parmesan and bake at 180 °C for thirty-five minutes."],"o":"Badly squeezed spinach is the number one cause of watery cannelloni."},"273":{"n":"Baked crespelle","p":["Make the batter with flour, eggs and 400 ml of milk and let it rest for half an hour.","Cook thin crespelle in a greased non-stick pan.","Fill with ricotta and blanched, squeezed spinach.","Roll up or fold into quarters and arrange in a dish.","Cover with béchamel made with the remaining milk and bake at 180 °C for twenty-five minutes."],"o":"The first crespella never works: it's there to set the pan temperature."},"274":{"n":"Pasta and broccoli","p":["Blanch the broccoli florets for eight minutes and keep the water.","Fry garlic and chilli, add the anchovies and melt them.","Add the broccoli and crush it with a fork.","Cook the pasta in the broccoli water and drain it al dente.","Toss in the pan with a ladle of water and finish with pecorino."],"o":"Cooking the pasta in the broccoli water is what brings the dish together: never throw it away."},"275":{"n":"Pasta with cauliflower","p":["Blanch the cauliflower florets for ten minutes, keeping the water.","Stew the onion, then add passata, soaked raisins, pine nuts and saffron.","Add the cauliflower and partly crush it.","Cook the pasta in its water and add it to the sauce.","Finish with pecorino and pepper."],"o":"It's the Palermo version of pasta with broccoli: there cauliflower is called 'broccolo', hence the confusion."},"276":{"n":"Spaghetti alla Nerano","p":["Slice the courgettes into thin rounds and fry them in plenty of oil, a few at a time.","Drain on kitchen paper and salt them, then leave half to rest with the basil for an hour.","Cook the pasta very al dente.","In a pan heat a little of the frying oil with garlic, add the courgettes and a ladle of water.","Toss the pasta and, off the heat, beat in grated provolone and butter, stirring vigorously."],"o":"Courgettes fried in advance and left to rest with basil are the real secret of the dish."},"277":{"n":"Pasta e piselli","p":["Stew the onion and diced pancetta in oil.","Add the peas and passata, cover with hot water and cook for fifteen minutes.","Add the pasta straight in, adding water a little at a time.","Stir often: it should stay creamy.","Turn off the heat, stir in parmesan and leave to rest for five minutes."],"o":"Like all risotto-style pastas it needs frequent stirring: the starch makes it creamy."},"278":{"n":"Pasta with lentils","p":["Make a soffritto with chopped onion, carrot and celery.","Add the rinsed lentils, the passata and water to cover.","Cook for thirty-five minutes, salting only halfway through.","Add the pasta to the soup and cook, stirring.","Serve with a drizzle of raw oil and chopped rosemary."],"o":"Lentils salted at the start stay hard: salt toughens the skins."},"279":{"n":"Minestra maritata","p":["Make a broth with the meats, cooking for two hours and skimming.","Strain the broth and cut the meat into pieces.","Boil the greens separately, then squeeze and roughly chop them.","Add the greens and meat to the broth and cook together for half an hour.","Serve with parmesan and chilli."],"o":"The 'marriage' in the name is between meat and greens, not two people."},"280":{"n":"Stracciatella alla romana","p":["Beat the eggs with parmesan, nutmeg and lemon zest.","Bring the stock to a gentle boil.","Pour in the mixture in a thin stream, whisking.","Turn off the heat after thirty seconds: rags of egg will form.","Serve at once with more parmesan."],"o":"If the stock boils hard the egg sets into lumps: you want the barest simmer."},"281":{"n":"Farro soup","p":["Make a soffritto with onion, carrot and celery.","Add the beans, passata and plenty of water, and cook for twenty minutes.","Blend half the beans and return them to the pot.","Add the rinsed farro and cook for forty minutes.","Serve with a drizzle of raw oil and chopped garlic and rosemary."],"o":"Pearled farro doesn't need soaking; hulled farro does, for at least eight hours."},"282":{"n":"Barley 'risotto' with mushrooms","p":["Sauté the sliced mushrooms over a high heat and set aside, salting them at the end.","Stew the onion in butter and toast the barley for two minutes.","Deglaze with the wine and add hot stock a ladle at a time.","Barley takes longer than rice: allow thirty-five minutes.","Add the mushrooms halfway through and finish with butter and parmesan."],"o":"Barley doesn't release starch like rice: the creaminess has to be built with the final mantecatura."},"283":{"n":"Polenta taragna","p":["Bring a litre and a half of salted water to the boil.","Pour in the two mixed flours in a stream, whisking.","Cook for fifty minutes over a low heat, stirring often.","In the last ten minutes add the diced cheese and half the butter.","Finish with the rest of the butter, cooked until golden."],"o":"The 'tarai' was the stick used to stir it: that's where the name comes from, not from the buckwheat."},"284":{"n":"Pumpkin and sausage risotto","p":["Brown the crumbled sausage and set it aside.","Stew the onion in its fat and add the cubed pumpkin.","Cook for ten minutes, crushing half of it into a cream.","Toast the rice, deglaze with the wine and add the stock.","Return the sausage halfway through and finish with butter and parmesan."],"o":"Crushing half the pumpkin and leaving the rest in cubes gives two textures in the same dish."},"285":{"n":"Lemon tagliolini","p":["Grate the zest of two lemons, avoiding the white pith.","Warm the butter with the zest for a minute, without frying it.","Add the cream and the juice of half a lemon and let it thicken slightly.","Cook the tagliolini for two minutes and toss them in the sauce with cooking water.","Stir in parmesan and finish with basil and more raw zest."],"o":"Half the zest cooked and half raw: the first gives depth, the second the fragrance."},"286":{"n":"Pasta with pumpkin and provola","p":["Stew the onion, then add the cubed pumpkin and rosemary.","Cover with stock and cook for twenty minutes, until it breaks down.","Blend half the mixture to get a cream with some pieces left.","Add the pasta straight in, adding stock a little at a time.","Off the heat stir in the diced provola, which should just melt."],"o":"Provola goes in off the heat: cooked, it releases fat and turns rubbery."},"287":{"n":"Courgette parmigiana","p":["Slice the courgettes lengthways 5 mm thick, flour them and fry them.","Drain on kitchen paper and salt them.","Make a simple sauce with oil, passata and basil.","Layer sauce, courgettes, drained mozzarella and parmesan in a dish, three layers.","Bake at 180 °C for thirty-five minutes and rest for twenty minutes."],"o":"More delicate than the aubergine version, so it needs more careful salting."},"288":{"n":"Aubergine cutlets","p":["Slice the aubergines 1 cm thick, salt them and leave to drain for half an hour.","Pat them dry with paper.","Mix breadcrumbs, parmesan, and chopped garlic and parsley.","Dip the slices in beaten egg and then in the crumb mixture, pressing hard.","Fry in oil at 175 °C, or bake at 200 °C for twenty-five minutes."],"o":"Pressing the coating on well is what stops it coming away as it cooks."},"289":{"n":"Sweet and sour peppers","p":["Stew the sliced onions in oil for fifteen minutes.","Add the peppers in strips and cook for twenty minutes over a medium heat.","Add soaked raisins and pine nuts.","Dissolve the sugar in the vinegar and pour it in, turning up the heat to cook it off.","Turn off the heat, add the basil and serve at room temperature."],"o":"The sweet and sour must be cooked off over a high heat: otherwise it stays sharp and harsh."},"290":{"n":"Zucchine alla scapece","p":["Cut the courgettes into 5 mm rounds.","Fry them in hot oil a few at a time and drain on kitchen paper.","Layer them in a container with sliced garlic and mint.","Heat the vinegar and pour it over the layers while boiling.","Leave to rest for at least four hours, ideally overnight."],"o":"Pour the vinegar on boiling: that's what truly marinates them rather than just wetting them."},"291":{"n":"Cauliflower gratin","p":["Divide the cauliflower into florets and boil them for eight minutes, keeping them firm.","Make the béchamel with butter, flour and hot milk, flavoured with nutmeg.","Arrange the florets in an ovenproof dish and cover with the béchamel.","Sprinkle with parmesan and breadcrumbs.","Bake at 200 °C for twenty-five minutes."],"o":"Overcooked cauliflower falls apart under the béchamel: eight minutes and stop."},"292":{"n":"Vegetable sformato","p":["Boil and mash the potatoes; blanch, squeeze and chop the spinach.","Mix with eggs, parmesan, milk, salt, pepper and nutmeg.","Pour into a buttered mould sprinkled with breadcrumbs.","Bake at 180 °C for forty minutes.","Leave to cool for ten minutes before turning out."],"o":"It's the most effective way to get vegetables into people who won't eat them."},"293":{"n":"Pumpkin flan","p":["Roast the cubed pumpkin at 200 °C for thirty minutes: it's drier than boiled.","Blend it with cream, eggs and parmesan.","Season with salt, pepper and nutmeg.","Pour into buttered ramekins and bake in a bain-marie at 170 °C for thirty-five minutes.","Turn out when lukewarm."],"o":"Roasting the pumpkin instead of boiling it is what prevents a watery flan."},"294":{"n":"Country-style potatoes","p":["Cut the potatoes into chunks and brown them in a wide pan with oil and garlic.","After ten minutes add the onion and the pepper in strips.","Add the cherry tomatoes and half a glass of water.","Cover and cook for twenty-five minutes, shaking the pan now and then.","Uncover for the last five minutes and finish with oregano."],"o":"Shake the pan instead of stirring: the potatoes break up at the first spoon."},"295":{"n":"Sweet and sour baby onions","p":["Peel the baby onions, leaving them whole.","Brown them in oil over a gentle heat for ten minutes.","Add sugar, vinegar, wine and bay leaf.","Cover and cook for thirty minutes over a very low heat.","Uncover and let them caramelise until the liquid is syrupy."],"o":"They're eaten at room temperature and improve after a day."},"296":{"n":"Insalata di rinforzo","p":["Boil the cauliflower florets for ten minutes, keeping them firm, and drain well.","Leave to cool completely spread out on a tray.","Add olives, rinsed capers, anchovy pieces and pickled peppers.","Dress with oil, vinegar, salt and pepper.","Leave to rest for at least three hours before serving."],"o":"It's called 'reinforcement salad' because at Christmas it was topped up every day with whatever was left over."},"297":{"n":"Broccoli strascinati","p":["Blanch the broccoli florets for five minutes and drain.","Heat plenty of oil with garlic and chilli.","Add the broccoli and drag it across the bottom of the pan with a wooden spoon.","Deglaze with the wine and continue for ten minutes over a high heat.","They should just catch on the bottom: that's where they get their flavour."],"o":"'Strascinare' means exactly that: crushing and dragging them along the pan, not tossing them."},"298":{"n":"Stewed green beans","p":["Top and tail the green beans and blanch them for five minutes.","Stew onion and garlic in oil, add the passata and cook for ten minutes.","Add the green beans and half a glass of water.","Cover and cook for twenty minutes over a gentle heat.","Finish with basil and a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Cook them long, until soft: bite isn't the point here."},"299":{"n":"Pasta frittata","p":["Use leftover dressed pasta, or boil some and let it cool.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, parsley, salt and pepper.","Add the pasta, diced mozzarella and salami.","Pour into a non-stick pan with a little oil and press down well.","Cook over a low heat for ten minutes per side, turning with a lid."],"o":"Born to use up yesterday's pasta, which is exactly why people make it on purpose."},"300":{"n":"Uova in trippa","p":["Make thin omelettes with the beaten eggs, one at a time.","Roll them up and cut into thin strips, like tripe.","Make a simple sauce with onion and passata, cooking for twenty minutes.","Add the strips and let them take on flavour for ten minutes.","Finish with pecorino and wild mint."],"o":"The name comes from the shape: it was the poor man's tripe, for those who couldn't afford tripe."},"301":{"n":"Fonduta valdostana","p":["Dice the fontina and leave it in the milk for at least four hours.","Heat over a bain-marie with the butter, stirring until it melts.","Add the yolks one at a time, still over the bain-marie and never letting it boil.","Keep stirring until it becomes a smooth, velvety cream.","Serve with toasted bread or polenta."],"o":"If it boils it splits and separates: the bain-marie isn't a refinement, it's essential."},"302":{"n":"Cheese and potato bake","p":["Boil the potatoes, peel them while hot and mash them.","Mix in eggs, milk, parmesan, salt and pepper.","Spread half in a buttered tin sprinkled with breadcrumbs.","Cover with sliced scamorza and top with the rest.","Sprinkle with breadcrumbs and butter and bake at 200 °C for thirty-five minutes."],"o":"Mash the potatoes while hot: cold, they stay lumpy."},"303":{"n":"Uova alla monachina","p":["Hard-boil the eggs for nine minutes, peel them and halve them.","Take out the yolks and mash them.","Make a thick béchamel and mix it with the yolks, parmesan and nutmeg.","Fill the whites, pair them up to close them and coat in egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in oil at 175 °C until golden."],"o":"The two halves are paired up to rebuild the egg: that's the part that takes patience."},"304":{"n":"Seupa à la Valpellinentze","p":["Blanch the savoy cabbage, cut into strips, for ten minutes and drain.","Toast the slices of bread in butter.","In an ovenproof dish layer bread, cabbage and sliced fontina, three layers.","Pour in boiling stock almost to cover and dust with cinnamon.","Bake at 180 °C for forty minutes, until the top is golden."],"o":"It's the valley's most famous soup, and the pinch of cinnamon is what makes it unmistakable."},"305":{"n":"Valdostana veal chops","p":["Cut into the side of the chops to make a pocket.","Fill with a slice of ham and a slice of fontina.","Close by pressing the edges firmly and coat in egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in butter over a medium heat, five minutes per side.","Serve at once, while the cheese is melting."],"o":"Press the pocket closed firmly: if it opens, the fontina escapes and burns in the butter."},"306":{"n":"Toc' in braide","p":["Make a soft polenta, cooking the cornmeal for forty minutes.","Separately, heat the cream and cheese over a bain-marie until melted.","Melt butter with flour: this becomes the dark sauce to pour over.","Pour the polenta onto the plate, make a hollow and fill it with the cheese cream.","Finish with the brown butter and toasted flour."],"o":"'Braide' is the meadow: the polenta is the field, the cheese the pond. The name describes the dish."},"307":{"n":"Muset e brovada","p":["Prick the cotechino and simmer it in water for two hours over a very low heat.","Stew the onion in oil, add the drained brovada and deglaze with the wine.","Cook for forty minutes, adding a little water.","Slice the cotechino and serve it on the brovada.","Let it take on the flavours for ten minutes off the heat."],"o":"Brovada is turnip fermented in grape pomace: sour and unmistakable, with no equivalent."},"308":{"n":"Stoccafisso all'anconetana","p":["Use pre-soaked stockfish, cut into large pieces.","Line the base of a casserole with chopped vegetables and sliced potatoes.","Lay the stockfish on top and alternate more layers of vegetables and potatoes.","Moisten with wine, plenty of oil and water just to cover, without fully submerging.","Cook covered over a very low heat for two and a half hours, never stirring.","Swirl the casserole every so often."],"o":"It's Ancona's Slow Food Presidium dish: it cooks slowly and is never touched with a spoon."},"309":{"n":"Crescia sfogliata","p":["Knead flour, eggs, water, salt and plenty of pepper, and leave to rest for half an hour.","Roll out very thin sheets and spread them with lard.","Roll each sheet up on itself and then into a snail, and rest for another thirty minutes.","Roll each snail out into a disc.","Cook on a very hot griddle for four minutes per side."],"o":"The lard between the layers and the double rest are what create the flaky layers."},"310":{"n":"Imbrecciata umbra","p":["Soak the chickpeas and beans overnight.","Make a soffritto with onion, celery and carrot.","Add the soaked pulses and the passata, cover with water and cook for an hour.","Add the lentils and farro and continue for thirty-five minutes.","Serve with a drizzle of raw oil and marjoram."],"o":"'Imbrecciata' because the mixed pulses look like 'breccia', road gravel."},"311":{"n":"Timballo teramano","p":["Make thin crespelle with flour, eggs and milk.","Make a ragù with onion, meat and passata, cooking for an hour.","Make tiny meatballs with some of the meat and fry them.","Blanch the spinach and squeeze it.","Layer crespelle, ragù, meatballs, spinach, scamorza and parmesan in a dish.","Bake at 180 °C for forty minutes."],"o":"Crespelle instead of pasta are Teramo's signature: there they're called 'scrippelle'."},"312":{"n":"Brodetto alla vastese","p":["Use a terracotta pot, as tradition demands.","Fry garlic and chilli, add the crushed cherry tomatoes and cook for fifteen minutes.","Layer in the fish, starting with the firmest: cuttlefish, then fish, then crustaceans and shellfish.","Never stir: just shake the pot.","Cook covered for twenty minutes and serve with toasted bread."],"o":"In Vasto no onion is used and the tomato is fresh: that's what sets it apart from other brodetti."},"313":{"n":"Cavatelli with ventricina","p":["Soften the chopped onion in oil.","Add the diced ventricina and let it partly melt.","Add the passata and cook for twenty minutes: the sauce will turn orange and spicy.","Cook the cavatelli and toss them in the sauce.","Finish with pecorino and basil."],"o":"Ventricina from Montenero is fatty and spicy: 150 grams is enough for four people."},"314":{"n":"Calcioni molisani","p":["Knead flour, eggs, oil and a pinch of salt, and leave to rest for half an hour.","Beat the ricotta with sugar, cinnamon and lemon zest.","Roll the dough thinly and cut out discs.","Fill, fold into half-moons and seal with a fork.","Fry in oil at 170 °C, or bake at 180 °C for twenty minutes."],"o":"There are savoury versions too, with cheese and eggs: the same dough, a different destiny."},"315":{"n":"Panna cotta","p":["Soak the gelatine in cold water for ten minutes.","Heat the cream, milk, sugar and vanilla without letting it boil.","Turn off the heat and add the squeezed gelatine, stirring until dissolved.","Pour into moulds and chill for at least four hours.","Turn out by dipping the mould briefly in hot water."],"o":"If it boils, the cream takes on a cooked taste: bring it just to the point of boiling."},"316":{"n":"Torta caprese","p":["Grind the almonds finely: there's no flour in a caprese, the almonds give it structure.","Melt the chocolate over a bain-marie and let it cool slightly.","Cream the butter and sugar, then add the yolks one at a time.","Fold in the chocolate and almonds, then the stiffly whisked egg whites.","Bake at 170 °C for forty minutes: it should stay moist in the centre.","Dust with icing sugar once cold."],"o":"Born by mistake, when the flour was forgotten. It's gluten-free by chance, not by design."},"317":{"n":"Torta della nonna","p":["Make the shortcrust with flour, cold butter, sugar, an egg and a yolk. Rest for an hour.","Make pastry cream with yolks, sugar, flour and lemon-scented milk.","Line the tin with two-thirds of the pastry and pour in the cold cream.","Cover with the remaining disc and seal the edges.","Scatter with pine nuts and bake at 180 °C for forty-five minutes.","Dust with icing sugar once cold."],"o":"The cream must be cold: hot, it soaks the pastry and the base stays raw."},"318":{"n":"Cantucci","p":["Mix flour, sugar, eggs, baking powder and lemon zest.","Add the whole unpeeled almonds.","Shape into logs 5 cm wide and place them on the baking tray.","Bake at 180 °C for twenty-five minutes.","Cut diagonally into 1 cm slices and return to the oven for ten minutes on each side."],"o":"Baking twice is what makes them biscuits: baked only once they stay soft."},"319":{"n":"Ciambellone","p":["Whisk the eggs and sugar until the mixture leaves a trail, at least five minutes.","Add the oil and milk in a thin stream while whisking.","Fold in the sifted flour and baking powder from the bottom up.","Add the lemon zest.","Pour into a ring tin and bake at 180 °C for forty-five minutes."],"o":"Whisking the eggs and sugar for a long time is the only raising agent that really counts."},"320":{"n":"Torta paradiso","p":["Beat the soft butter with the sugar for ten minutes, until white and fluffy.","Add the yolks one at a time, waiting until each is absorbed.","Fold in the sifted flour, potato starch and baking powder, and the lemon zest.","Gently fold in the stiffly whisked egg whites.","Bake at 170 °C for forty minutes and dust with icing sugar."],"o":"Potato starch in place of half the flour is what gives that melt-in-the-mouth crumbliness."},"321":{"n":"Torrone semifreddo","p":["Whisk the yolks with half the sugar until pale.","Whisk the egg whites to stiff peaks with the remaining sugar.","Whip the cream until firm.","Combine the three mixtures, folding from the bottom up.","Fold in the roughly chopped torrone and pour into the mould.","Freeze for at least six hours and serve with melted chocolate."],"o":"Take it out of the freezer ten minutes before serving: straight out, it's too hard."},"322":{"n":"Sicilian lemon granita","p":["Dissolve the sugar in the cold water, stirring until it disappears.","Add the strained lemon juice and the grated zest of two lemons.","Pour into a shallow container and put it in the freezer.","Every thirty minutes break up the crystals with a fork, for at least three hours.","Serve just set, with a brioche col tuppo."],"o":"Real granita isn't made in a blender: it's broken up with a fork, and that's the difference from sorbet."},"323":{"n":"Struffoli","p":["Knead flour, eggs, butter, sugar, rum and zest, and rest for half an hour.","Roll into thin ropes and cut into 1 cm pieces.","Fry in oil at 170 °C, a few handfuls at a time.","Warm the honey with a little water until runny.","Tip the struffoli into the honey and mix off the heat.","Arrange in a ring and decorate with candied fruit and sprinkles."],"o":"The honey mustn't be boiling when you add them, or they turn rock hard."},"324":{"n":"Mostaccioli","p":["Knead flour, sugar, warm honey, cocoa, cinnamon, baking powder and orange juice.","Roll out 1 cm thick and cut into diamonds.","Bake at 180 °C for fifteen minutes: they should stay soft.","Melt the chocolate over a bain-marie.","Dip the cooled biscuits and leave them to set on a rack."],"o":"If you bake them until firm, they turn into bricks once cold: take them out while still soft."},"325":{"n":"Pardulas","p":["Knead semolina, lard and lukewarm water into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Beat the ricotta with sugar, eggs, saffron and lemon zest.","Roll the dough thinly and cut out discs.","Place a spoonful of filling in the middle and pinch the edges upwards to form a little basket.","Bake at 180 °C for twenty-five minutes."],"o":"Saffron in the filling is as Sardinian as the ricotta: it gives colour and a surprising fragrance."},"326":{"n":"Papassini","p":["Knead flour, lard, sugar, eggs and baking powder.","Add soaked raisins, roughly chopped walnuts and almonds, and the zest.","Roll out 1 cm thick and cut into diamonds.","Bake at 180 °C for twenty minutes.","Glaze with whisked egg white and icing sugar, and decorate with sprinkles."],"o":"'Papassa' means raisin: it's what gives the biscuit its name."},"327":{"n":"Panforte","p":["Toast the nuts and chop them roughly.","Mix with the candied peel, flour and spices.","Heat the honey and sugar to 115 °C, then pour over the mixture.","Mix quickly and spread 2 cm thick in a tin lined with wafer paper.","Bake at 150 °C for thirty minutes and dust with icing sugar once cold."],"o":"The syrup must reach 115 °C: below that the panforte won't hold together; above, it turns hard."},"328":{"n":"Ricciarelli","p":["Grind the blanched almonds very finely with half the sugar.","Whisk the egg whites to stiff peaks and fold them gently into the mixture.","Add the sugar, lemon zest and baking powder to make a soft paste.","Leave the mixture to rest overnight in the fridge.","Shape into diamonds, roll them in icing sugar and bake at 150 °C for fifteen minutes."],"o":"The overnight rest is what creates the cracked crust on top."},"329":{"n":"Zuccotto","p":["Line a dome mould with cling film and with slices of sponge soaked in alchermes.","Whip the cream and fold it into the sieved ricotta with the sugar.","Divide the cream in two: add chopped chocolate and almonds to one half.","Fill the mould, alternating the two creams.","Close with more sponge and chill for six hours, then turn it out."],"o":"It's said to have been born in Florence as a dessert shaped like Brunelleschi's dome."},"330":{"n":"Gelo di melone","p":["Blend and strain the watermelon flesh: you need about a litre of juice.","Mix the starch into the cold juice with no lumps and add the sugar.","Bring to the heat, stirring, until it thickens like custard.","Pour into glasses and chill for at least four hours.","Decorate with chocolate chips and a pinch of cinnamon."],"o":"In Sicily 'melone' means watermelon: anyone making gelo with yellow melon is making a different dessert."},"331":{"n":"Biancomangiare","p":["Mix the starch into a little cold milk.","Heat the rest of the milk with the lemon zest and sugar.","Add the starch and cook, stirring, until it thickens.","Pour into moulds and chill for three hours.","Turn out and finish with chopped almonds and cinnamon."],"o":"Medieval and found across half of Europe: in Sicily it's made with almond milk, and it's even better."},"332":{"n":"Chocolate pudding","p":["Mix the starch and sugar into a little cold milk.","Heat the remaining milk with the cream.","Add the starch mixture and cook, stirring, until it thickens.","Turn off the heat and add the chopped chocolate, stirring until melted.","Pour into moulds and chill for four hours."],"o":"The chocolate goes in off the heat: cooked, it turns grainy."},"333":{"n":"Rice cake","p":["Cook the rice in the milk with the lemon zest for thirty minutes, until creamy.","Leave to cool completely.","Add eggs, sugar, chopped almonds and rum.","Pour into a buttered tin sprinkled with breadcrumbs.","Bake at 180 °C for forty-five minutes."],"o":"It's the cake of the Bolognese 'addobbi' festivals: cut into diamonds, never slices."},"334":{"n":"Pasta with butter and parmesan","p":["Cook the pasta in salted water and drain it, keeping two ladles of water.","Put the cold butter in pieces in a large bowl, not a pan.","Tip the boiling pasta onto the butter and stir vigorously.","Add the parmesan in three goes, alternating with cooking water, until creamy."],"o":"Cold butter, and a bowl instead of a pan: that's how it emulsifies instead of separating."},"335":{"n":"Pasta with toasted breadcrumbs","p":["Crumble the stale bread coarsely, with a knife or your hands.","Toast it in a pan with plenty of oil, garlic and chilli, stirring constantly.","Take it off the heat as soon as it's golden: from there to burnt takes ten seconds.","Drain the pasta and toss it with half the crumbs and a little cooking water.","Serve with the rest of the crumbs on top, so they stay crunchy."],"o":"The poor man's bread in place of cheese. Half in, half on top is the rule: inside it binds, on top it crunches."},"336":{"n":"Spaghetti with anchovies and breadcrumbs","p":["Toast the crumbled bread in a little oil and set aside.","Heat plenty of oil with the garlic and melt the anchovies, crushing them with a spoon.","Toss the pasta in the sauce with a ladle of cooking water.","Finish with the crunchy crumbs and pepper."],"o":"No salt needed: the anchovies bring plenty."},"337":{"n":"Pasta cacio e uova","p":["Beat the whole eggs with the pecorino and lots of pepper into a thick cream.","Cook the pasta and drain it, keeping the water.","Tip it into the bowl with the egg, off the heat, stirring quickly.","Loosen with cooking water to the right consistency."],"o":"It's carbonara before carbonara: without guanciale, as it was made in the mountains."},"338":{"n":"Pasta with ricotta","p":["Sieve the ricotta into a large bowl.","Loosen it with two ladles of hot cooking water into a smooth cream.","Drain the pasta and tip it into the bowl.","Stir in the pecorino and plenty of pepper."],"o":"Loosen the ricotta with water first: thrown straight onto the pasta it stays lumpy."},"339":{"n":"Pasta with olive oil and lemon","p":["Grate the zest of two lemons and squeeze the juice of all three.","Whisk oil, juice, zest, salt and pepper together in a bowl.","Drain the pasta and toss it in the dressing with a little cooking water.","Finish with chopped parsley and more raw zest."],"o":"One for when there's nothing in the house: it costs next to nothing and always surprises."},"340":{"n":"Pasta with walnuts","p":["Chop the walnuts with a knife, keeping a few whole.","Toast them for a minute in a dry pan, until fragrant.","Drain the pasta and toss it with the walnuts, oil and two ladles of cooking water.","Stir in parmesan off the heat."],"o":"A minute of toasting completely changes the taste of walnuts."},"341":{"n":"Pasta with slow-cooked onions","p":["Slice the onions very thinly.","Stew them in plenty of oil over a very low heat for forty minutes with two tablespoons of water.","They should melt down and turn sweet, without ever colouring.","Toss the pasta in the onion cream with a little cooking water and finish with pecorino."],"o":"It's a genovese without the meat: forty minutes of onions and nothing else."},"342":{"n":"Spaghetti with raw tomatoes","p":["Cut the cherry tomatoes into wedges and dress them with oil, salt, crushed garlic and basil.","Leave them at room temperature for at least an hour: they need to release their juice.","Remove the garlic.","Drain the pasta and dress it raw with the tomatoes and all their liquid."],"o":"The hour's wait is the dish: without it, it's just tomatoes on pasta."},"343":{"n":"Pasta with courgettes","p":["Cut the courgettes into thin rounds.","Brown them in a wide pan with oil and garlic over a high heat, without crowding.","When golden, crush half of them with a fork to make a cream.","Toss the pasta with a ladle of cooking water, stirring well."],"o":"Half whole, half crushed: that gives creaminess without adding anything."},"344":{"n":"Pasta with parsley pesto","p":["Blend parsley, garlic, almonds and salt, adding oil in a thin stream.","Loosen with a tablespoon of cold water to keep the colour bright.","Drain the pasta and dress it raw with the pesto loosened with cooking water.","Never heat the pesto."],"o":"When there's no basil, parsley does the job at a third of the price."},"345":{"n":"Pasta with tuna, no tomato","p":["Drain the tuna, keeping a little of its oil.","Drain the pasta and tip it into a bowl with the flaked tuna.","Dress with oil, lemon juice and zest, salt and pepper.","Finish with chopped parsley."],"o":"Without tomato you really taste the tuna: the store-cupboard dish par excellence."},"346":{"n":"Pasta with garlic, oil and breadcrumbs","p":["Toast the breadcrumbs in a dry pan until golden, then set aside.","Warm the oil with sliced garlic and chilli over a very low heat.","Toss the pasta with a ladle of cooking water.","Stir well and finish with the toasted breadcrumbs."],"o":"A variation on the stale-bread version for when there's none: same principle, less rustic."},"347":{"n":"Pasta with beaten egg","p":["Beat the eggs with the parmesan and a pinch of salt.","Drain the boiling pasta and tip it straight into the bowl.","Stir quickly: the heat of the pasta cooks the egg.","Loosen with cooking water if needed."],"o":"The sick-child dish of half of Italy, and as adults we discover it's delicious."},"348":{"n":"Pasta with pumpkin","p":["Cut the pumpkin into small cubes.","Stew it in a pan with oil, rosemary and a little water for twenty minutes.","Crush half of it with a fork.","Toss the pasta in the cream with cooking water, stirring well."],"o":"Three ingredients and it looks elaborate: the pumpkin does all the work."},"349":{"n":"Plain buttered rice","p":["Cook the rice in salted water and drain it al dente.","Dress it straight away with pieces of butter, stirring.","Add the parmesan and a tablespoon of cooking water.","Serve immediately."],"o":"Dress the rice while piping hot, never lukewarm: the butter must melt on contact."},"350":{"n":"Parmesan risotto","p":["Stew the very finely chopped onion in half the butter.","Toast the rice for two minutes, until the grains feel hot to the touch.","Add the boiling stock a ladle at a time for sixteen minutes.","Turn off the heat, wait a minute and beat in cold butter and parmesan vigorously."],"o":"With nothing on it, a plain risotto is the best way to judge your mantecatura."},"351":{"n":"Lemon rice","p":["Cook the rice in salted water.","Drain it and dress it with butter, grated zest and the juice of one lemon.","Stir in the parmesan.","Finish with more raw zest."],"o":"Add the juice a little at a time, tasting: the line between fresh and sour is fine."},"352":{"n":"Rice with parsley","p":["Chop the parsley very finely with the garlic.","Heat the oil and flavour it with the mixture for thirty seconds, without frying.","Drain the rice and dress it with the flavoured oil.","Add plenty of pepper."],"o":"Thirty seconds and that's it: fried parsley turns bitter."},"353":{"n":"Rice with a poached egg","p":["Cook the rice in salted water and dress it with oil and parmesan.","Bring water with vinegar to a simmer, without boiling hard.","Swirl the water with a spoon and crack the egg into the centre.","Cook for three minutes and lift out with a slotted spoon.","Lay the egg on the rice and break the yolk at the table."],"o":"Vinegar makes the white set quickly: without it, it frays in the water."},"354":{"n":"Scrambled eggs","p":["Beat the eggs lightly, leaving streaks of yolk visible.","Melt the butter in a pan over a very low heat.","Pour in the eggs and stir slowly with a spatula, taking the pan off the heat now and then.","Stop while they're still moist: they finish cooking on the plate.","Season with salt and finish with parmesan."],"o":"Very low heat and taking the pan off are everything: scrambled eggs are ruined in thirty seconds."},"355":{"n":"Uovo in cereghin","p":["Melt plenty of butter in a pan over a medium heat until it starts to foam.","Gently crack in the eggs side by side.","Cook without turning, spooning the hot butter over the yolks.","The white should be set with golden edges and the yolk runny.","Season with salt and finish with parmesan."],"o":"'Cereghin' is Milanese for altar boy: the yellow yolk recalls a tonsure."},"356":{"n":"Onion frittata","p":["Slice the onions thinly and stew them in oil over a gentle heat for twenty-five minutes.","Let them cool slightly: piping hot, they'd cook the egg in the bowl.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, salt and pepper and add them to the onions.","Cook in a non-stick pan for seven minutes per side, flipping with a lid."],"o":"Long-stewed onions turn sweet: that's what makes this frittata memorable."},"357":{"n":"Frittata with breadcrumbs","p":["Soak the bread in a little water and squeeze it out.","Beat it with the eggs, pecorino and parsley.","Pour into a pan with hot oil.","Cook for eight minutes per side over a low heat."],"o":"The bread doubles the size of the frittata with the same eggs: that's why it exists."},"358":{"n":"Hard-boiled eggs with mayonnaise","p":["Hard-boil four eggs for nine minutes and cool them in cold water.","Whisk the mayonnaise with a raw yolk, oil in a thin stream, lemon and mustard.","Halve the eggs and arrange them on the plate.","Coat with the mayonnaise and finish with pepper."],"o":"Everything at room temperature, or the mayonnaise splits: it's the only rule that matters."},"359":{"n":"Bread and tomato","p":["Toast the bread.","Rub the still-hot surface with garlic.","Halve the tomato and rub it over the bread, squashing it.","Dress with oil and salt and eat at once."],"o":"The tomato is rubbed, not sliced: the pulp goes into the bread and the rest is thrown away."},"360":{"n":"Bruschetta","p":["Grill the slices of bread on both sides.","Rub with garlic until fragrant.","Dress the diced tomatoes with oil, salt and basil.","Spoon them onto the bread only when serving."],"o":"'Bruscare' means to toast over the fire: without the grill it's dressed bread, not bruschetta."},"361":{"n":"Pancotto (bread soup)","p":["Bring a litre of salted water to the boil with garlic and bay leaf.","Break up the stale bread and drop it in.","Cook for fifteen minutes, stirring, until it breaks down into a cream.","Serve with plenty of raw oil and pepper."],"o":"The humblest dish there is, and one of the most comforting. With a potato in it, it's even better."},"362":{"n":"Acquacotta","p":["Stew the sliced onions and celery in plenty of oil for twenty minutes.","Add the chopped tomatoes and a litre of water, and cook for twenty minutes.","Crack the eggs into the broth and let them set for three minutes.","Put the bread in the bowls and pour the broth and egg over it.","Finish with pecorino."],"o":"The name says it all: 'cooked water'. Then you enrich it with whatever there is."},"363":{"n":"Fried breadcrumbs","p":["Crumble the stale bread coarsely.","Heat plenty of oil with garlic and chilli.","Add the crumbs and stir constantly for five minutes.","Turn off the heat as soon as they're golden and add the parsley.","Use them on pasta or vegetables, or eat them as they are."],"o":"They keep for a week in a sealed jar and rescue any dull dish."},"364":{"n":"Bread with oil and oregano","p":["Cut the bread into thick slices.","Sprinkle it with a little water if it's stale.","Dress with plenty of oil, salt and oregano.","Let it rest for five minutes before eating."],"o":"The afternoon snack of half the South. Water on stale bread makes it soft again."},"365":{"n":"Dressed boiled potatoes","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins, starting from cold salted water.","Peel them while still hot and cut them into chunks.","Dress them at once with oil, very finely chopped garlic, parsley, salt and pepper.","Toss while hot: only then do they absorb the dressing."],"o":"Cold, the dressing just sits on the surface: it's all a matter of temperature."},"366":{"n":"Mashed potatoes","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins and peel them while hot.","Pass them through a ricer straight into the pan.","Add the butter in pieces and stir.","Pour in the hot milk a little at a time, beating with a whisk.","Flavour with nutmeg and adjust the salt."],"o":"The milk must be hot and so must the potatoes: if either is cold, the mash turns gluey."},"367":{"n":"Pan-cooked potatoes","p":["Slice the potatoes 5 mm thick and the onion thinly.","Heat plenty of oil in a wide pan.","Add everything together, season with salt and cover.","Cook for thirty minutes over a medium heat, shaking the pan now and then, without stirring.","Uncover for the last five minutes to brown them."],"o":"Shake instead of stirring: the potatoes stay whole and crispy bits form."},"368":{"n":"Fennel and orange salad","p":["Slice the fennel very thinly and keep it in cold water for ten minutes.","Peel the oranges, removing all the pith, and slice into rounds.","Drain and dry the fennel and arrange it on the plate, alternating with the oranges.","Dress with oil, salt, black pepper and olives."],"o":"Cold water makes fennel crisp and tones down its aniseed: ten minutes is enough."},"369":{"n":"Beans with oil and onion","p":["Drain and rinse the cooked beans.","Slice the onion very thinly and leave it in cold water for ten minutes.","Dress the beans with plenty of oil, salt and pepper.","Add the drained onion and parsley."],"o":"Soaking the onion takes away its harshness without taking away its flavour."},"370":{"n":"Chickpeas with rosemary","p":["Warm the oil with unpeeled garlic and rosemary over a very low heat for five minutes.","Remove the garlic and rosemary.","Add the drained chickpeas and let them take on flavour for ten minutes.","Crush a few to bind and serve with pepper."],"o":"Flavour the oil first, then add the chickpeas: the order changes the result."},"371":{"n":"Boiled cauliflower with lemon","p":["Divide the cauliflower into florets.","Boil them in salted water for ten minutes, keeping them firm.","Drain and dress them while hot with oil, lemon and salt.","Finish with parsley and pepper."],"o":"A piece of stale bread in the cooking water absorbs the cabbage smell."},"372":{"n":"Steamed courgettes with lemon","p":["Cut the courgettes into sticks.","Steam them for eight minutes: they should stay firm.","Dress while hot with oil, lemon juice and zest.","Finish with hand-torn mint."],"o":"Mint with courgettes is an old pairing that hardly anyone makes any more."},"373":{"n":"Roasted onions","p":["Leave the onions whole in their skins, just washed.","Put them in a tin with bay leaves and a finger of water.","Roast at 190 °C for an hour.","Cut them in half and dress with oil, salt, pepper and a drizzle of vinegar."],"o":"Cooked in their skins, they steam in their own moisture and turn very sweet."},"374":{"n":"Vegetable gratin","p":["Slice the vegetables 5 mm thick and arrange them in an oiled tin.","Mix breadcrumbs, parmesan, chopped garlic and oregano.","Sprinkle the mixture over the vegetables and drizzle with oil.","Bake at 200 °C for thirty minutes, until the top is golden."],"o":"Salt the vegetables before adding the crumbs, or they stay bland under the crust."},"375":{"n":"Pan-seared mozzarella","p":["Cut the mozzarella into thick slices and let it drain well on paper.","Heat a non-stick pan with no fat.","Cook the slices for two minutes per side, until a crust forms.","Finish with raw cherry tomatoes, oregano, oil and salt."],"o":"No fat and a scorching pan: the mozzarella forms its own crust."},"376":{"n":"Baked tomini","p":["Arrange the tomini in an ovenproof dish.","Bake at 200 °C for twelve minutes, until they puff up.","Take them out and immediately top with drizzled honey and chopped walnuts.","Serve piping hot with toasted bread."],"o":"Twelve minutes and no more: if they melt completely, you've lost the dish."},"377":{"n":"Ricotta with honey and walnuts","p":["Sieve the ricotta and divide it among small bowls.","Drizzle the honey over it.","Finish with toasted, roughly chopped walnuts.","Serve at room temperature, not straight from the fridge."],"o":"Cold ricotta tastes of nothing: half an hour out of the fridge changes everything."},"378":{"n":"Melted cheese with bread","p":["Slice the cheese thickly and arrange it in an ovenproof dish.","Bake at 220 °C for ten minutes, until bubbling and golden.","Toast the bread.","Serve at once, scooping up the cheese with the bread."],"o":"Three ingredients and it's dinner. It works with any cheese that melts."},"379":{"n":"Baked apples","p":["Wash the apples and remove the cores without piercing the bottom.","Stand them in a tin with a finger of water.","Fill the hollows with sugar and cinnamon.","Bake at 180 °C for forty minutes, basting with the juices now and then."],"o":"With a knob of butter in the hollow they become something else, but they're excellent without too."},"380":{"n":"Pears in red wine","p":["Peel the pears, leaving the stalks on.","Stand them upright in a narrow pan with wine, sugar and cinnamon.","Cook covered for forty minutes over a gentle heat, turning them now and then.","Take out the pears and reduce the wine to a syrup.","Serve the pears coated with the syrup."],"o":"The pan must be narrow: if the pears can't stand covered by the wine, they colour unevenly."},"381":{"n":"Mascarpone cream","p":["Whisk the yolks with the sugar until pale and foamy.","Add the mascarpone a little at a time, folding from the bottom.","Whisk the egg whites to stiff peaks and fold them in gently.","Chill for at least two hours before serving."],"o":"It's tiramisù without the savoiardi and coffee: on its own, with a few plain biscuits."},"382":{"n":"Zabaione","p":["Whisk the yolks with the sugar until pale.","Add the marsala in a thin stream.","Cook over a bain-marie, whisking without ever stopping.","In ten minutes the mixture triples in volume and turns foamy: it's ready when it coats the whisk.","Serve warm or cold, with plain biscuits."],"o":"The old rule is one yolk, one spoonful of sugar and one eggshell of marsala per person."},"383":{"n":"Thick hot chocolate","p":["Mix the starch and sugar into a little cold milk.","Heat the rest of the milk and add the chopped chocolate, stirring.","Pour in the starch mixture and cook, stirring, until it thickens.","Serve piping hot in small cups."],"o":"The starch is what makes it thick enough for a spoon rather than a drink."},"384":{"n":"Fruit salad","p":["Squeeze the juice of the oranges and lemon into a bowl.","Add the sugar and stir until it dissolves.","Cut the fruit into even cubes and add it to the juice straight away.","Chill for at least two hours."],"o":"Lemon juice on the cut fruit straight away is what stops it browning."},"385":{"n":"Yoghurt-pot cake","p":["Use the yoghurt pot as a measure: two of flour, two of sugar, one of oil.","Beat the eggs with the sugar, then add the yoghurt and oil.","Fold in the sifted flour and baking powder.","Pour into the tin and bake at 180 °C for forty minutes."],"o":"It's made without scales, which is why everyone knows how to make it."},"386":{"n":"Butter biscuits","p":["Beat the soft butter with the sugar into a cream.","Add the flour and knead quickly, without warming it.","Shape into a log, wrap in cling film and chill for an hour.","Slice 5 mm thick and bake at 170 °C for fifteen minutes."],"o":"Three ingredients in a 3:2:1 ratio: flour, butter, sugar. Nothing else needed."},"387":{"n":"Coffee cream","p":["Make the coffee and leave it to cool completely.","Whip the well-chilled cream with the sugar.","Add the cold coffee a little at a time, folding from the bottom.","Chill in the freezer for half an hour and serve in small glasses."],"o":"The coffee must be cold: lukewarm, it collapses the cream instantly."},"388":{"n":"Bread, butter and sugar","p":["Cut the bread into not-too-thin slices.","Spread generously with room-temperature butter.","Sprinkle with granulated sugar, which should stay grainy.","Eat at once."],"o":"The snack of three generations. The butter must be soft and the sugar mustn't dissolve."},"389":{"n":"Caesar salad","p":["Cut the bread into cubes and toast in a pan with oil and garlic until golden: these are the croutons.","For the dressing, blend a yolk, the anchovies, garlic, mustard and lemon juice.","Pour in the oil in a thin stream while blending, until thick and creamy.","Add half the grated parmesan and adjust with water if needed.","Dress the lettuce torn into large pieces, add the croutons and finish with parmesan shavings."],"o":"Born in Tijuana in 1924 from what was left in the kitchen at the end of the night: old bread, an egg and a few anchovies."},"390":{"n":"Chicken salad","p":["Poach the chicken breast for twenty minutes in water with celery and bay leaf, and let it cool in the stock.","Shred it by hand, not with a knife: it absorbs the dressing better.","Dice the celery and apple, sprinkling the apple with lemon.","Dress with yoghurt, oil, salt and pepper.","Finish with toasted chopped walnuts."],"o":"Cooling the chicken in its stock is what stops it drying out."},"391":{"n":"Chickpea and tuna salad","p":["Drain the chickpeas and rinse them under running water.","Slice the onion very thinly and leave it in cold water for ten minutes.","Add the drained, flaked tuna.","Dress with oil, lemon, salt, pepper and parsley.","Leave to rest for half an hour before serving."],"o":"Rinsing tinned chickpeas removes the liquid they come in, which is salty and tastes of the tin."},"392":{"n":"Farro salad","p":["Cook the farro in salted water for thirty-five minutes and drain.","Run it under cold water and dress straight away with a drizzle of oil.","Sauté the diced courgettes and leave to cool.","Add cherry tomatoes, well-drained diced mozzarella and olives.","Dress with oil, salt and basil, and chill for an hour."],"o":"Farro dressed with oil while warm doesn't stick: the same rule as for rice."},"393":{"n":"Cold pasta salad","p":["Cook short pasta al dente and cool it under running water.","Dress it straight away with oil so it doesn't stick.","Add cherry tomato wedges, drained mozzarella, olives and rinsed capers.","Dress with oil, salt and hand-torn basil.","Chill for an hour, but don't serve it ice-cold."],"o":"Take it out of the fridge twenty minutes ahead: ice-cold, it tastes of nothing."},"394":{"n":"Lentil salad","p":["Cook the lentils in unsalted water with a bay leaf for thirty-five minutes.","Drain and leave to cool spread out on a tray.","Cut the carrot, celery and onion into tiny dice.","Dress everything with oil, vinegar, salt and pepper.","Finish with parsley and leave to rest for an hour."],"o":"Lentils salted while cooking stay hard: always salt at the end or in the dressing."},"395":{"n":"Potato salad","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins, starting from cold water.","Peel them and cut into chunks while still hot.","Whisk together oil, vinegar, mustard, salt and pepper.","Dress the potatoes while hot so they absorb everything.","Add spring onion and parsley and serve warm."],"o":"Dressing hot potatoes is the difference between a tasty salad and a bland one."},"396":{"n":"Coleslaw","p":["Slice the cabbage very thinly and grate the carrots into julienne.","Salt the cabbage and leave it to drain for twenty minutes, then squeeze: it loses water and stays crunchy.","Mix yoghurt, mustard, vinegar, sugar, salt and pepper.","Dress and leave to rest in the fridge for an hour."],"o":"Pre-salting the cabbage is what stops the salad turning into a puddle the next day."},"397":{"n":"Cucumber and yoghurt salad","p":["Slice the cucumbers into thin rounds, salt them and leave to drain for twenty minutes.","Squeeze them lightly.","Mix yoghurt, crushed garlic, oil and chopped mint.","Add the cucumbers and serve cold."],"o":"It's Turkish cacık, cousin of tzatziki: here the cucumbers stay in rounds instead of being grated."},"398":{"n":"Orange and olive salad","p":["Peel the oranges, removing all the pith, and slice into rounds.","Slice the onion very thinly and leave it in cold water for ten minutes.","Arrange the plate, alternating oranges, onion and olives.","Dress with oil, salt, coarsely ground black pepper and oregano."],"o":"Plenty of black pepper on orange seems odd, but it's the point of the dish."},"399":{"n":"Octopus and potato salad","p":["Boil the octopus for forty-five minutes and let it cool in its water.","Boil the potatoes separately and cut into chunks.","Cut the octopus into pieces with scissors.","Dress everything with oil, lemon, very finely chopped garlic, salt and pepper.","Add parsley and olives and leave to rest for half an hour."],"o":"Dress the potatoes warm and the octopus cold: combining them at different temperatures is the trick."},"400":{"n":"Quick rice salad","p":["Cook the rice, drain it al dente and cool it under running water.","Dress it straight away with a drizzle of oil.","Hard-boil the eggs and cut them into wedges.","Drain and rinse the tinned sweetcorn and peas, and drain the tuna.","Mix everything, dress with oil and salt and leave to rest for an hour."],"o":"Almost every ingredient comes from the cupboard: it's the salad for when you haven't been shopping."},"401":{"n":"Farro panzanella","p":["Cook the farro and cool it under the tap.","Dice the tomatoes and cucumber and slice the onion thinly.","Dress the vegetables with oil, vinegar and salt and leave them for twenty minutes.","Add the farro and basil.","Leave to rest for an hour before serving."],"o":"Dressing the vegetables first makes them release their juice, which is the real dressing for the farro."},"402":{"n":"Waldorf salad","p":["Dice the apples with their skins and sprinkle them with lemon straight away.","Slice the celery thinly, removing the strings.","Soak the raisins in warm water for ten minutes.","Dress with yoghurt, oil, salt and pepper.","Finish with toasted walnuts and leave to rest for half an hour."],"o":"Born at the Waldorf in New York in 1896 with just apples, celery and mayonnaise: walnuts and raisins came later."},"403":{"n":"White cabbage and apple salad","p":["Slice the cabbage very thinly and massage it with salt for five minutes.","Rinse and squeeze it: it becomes tender without losing its crunch.","Cut the apples into matchsticks and sprinkle with lemon.","Dress with oil, vinegar and pepper.","Finish with toasted walnuts."],"o":"Massaging the cabbage with salt is a one-minute step that changes the texture."},"404":{"n":"Greek barley salad","p":["Cook the barley in salted water for thirty-five minutes and cool it.","Cut the cucumber and cherry tomatoes into pieces and slice the onion thinly.","Add the olives and cubed feta.","Dress with oil, salt, pepper and plenty of oregano.","Chill for an hour."],"o":"Add the feta last and don't mix it too much, or it crumbles away."},"405":{"n":"Bean and tuna salad","p":["Drain and rinse the beans.","Slice the onion very thinly and leave it in cold water for ten minutes.","Add the drained tuna in large chunks, without flaking it too much.","Dress with oil, vinegar, salt and pepper.","Leave for half an hour and finish with parsley."],"o":"The absolute classic of the Italian store cupboard: two tins and an onion."},"406":{"n":"Black rice salad","p":["Cook the black venere rice for forty minutes, then drain and cool it.","Sauté the diced courgettes and leave them to cool.","Add cherry tomatoes and the drained, rinsed sweetcorn.","Dress with oil, lemon, salt and pepper.","Finish with basil and leave to rest for half an hour."],"o":"Venere rice holds its texture better than white: it stays separate even the next day."},"407":{"n":"Tuna fishcakes","p":["Boil the potatoes, peel them and mash them while still hot.","Let cool, then add the drained tuna, an egg, parmesan and parsley.","Shape into flattened patties and coat in egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in oil at 175 °C, or bake at 200 °C for twenty minutes."],"o":"Work the mixture cold: when hot it's impossible to shape."},"408":{"n":"Tuna roll","p":["Boil the potatoes and mash them, then let them cool.","Add the drained tuna, eggs, parmesan, lemon zest and parsley.","Shape into a log and wrap it tightly in a clean cloth, tying the ends.","Simmer it in boiling water for twenty-five minutes.","Leave to cool completely before slicing."],"o":"It's boiled in the cloth, not baked: that's the Ligurian method and it stays much softer."},"409":{"n":"Mackerel with onions","p":["Slice the onions thinly and stew them in oil for twenty minutes.","Deglaze with the vinegar and add the bay leaf.","Drain the mackerel and add it in large pieces.","Let it take on the flavours for five minutes off the heat.","Serve warm or cold, with bread."],"o":"Tinned mackerel is cheap, has more omega-3 than tuna and hardly anyone uses it."},"410":{"n":"Pasta with tinned sardines","p":["Toast the breadcrumbs in a pan and set aside.","Stew the onion, add the drained sardines and let them break down.","Add soaked raisins, pine nuts and saffron dissolved in a little water.","Toss the pasta in the sauce.","Finish with the toasted breadcrumbs."],"o":"It's pasta con le sarde, store-cupboard version: less noble, delicious all the same."},"411":{"n":"Chickpea croquettes","p":["Drain and rinse the chickpeas, then dry them well.","Blend them with onion, parsley, cumin, salt and pepper, leaving a grainy mixture.","Add flour until you can shape the croquettes.","Coat in breadcrumbs and fry, or bake at 200 °C for twenty-five minutes."],"o":"With tinned chickpeas they won't come out like falafel — those need soaked raw chickpeas — but they're an excellent stand-in."},"412":{"n":"Bean burgers","p":["Drain, rinse and thoroughly dry the beans.","Mash them with a fork, leaving some pieces whole.","Add sautéed chopped onion and garlic, the spices and the breadcrumbs.","Shape into patties and chill for half an hour.","Cook in a pan with a little oil, five minutes per side."],"o":"Chilling is what holds them together while they cook."},"413":{"n":"Mixed bean soup","p":["Make a soffritto with chopped onion, carrot and celery.","Add the passata and cook for five minutes.","Add the drained and rinsed pulses and water to cover.","Cook for twenty minutes: they're already cooked, they just need flavouring.","Blend a third of the soup to thicken it and finish with rosemary and raw oil."],"o":"Tinned pulses are already cooked: boiling them for an hour just turns them to mush."},"414":{"n":"Tuna frittata","p":["Stew the sliced onion and let it cool slightly.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, parsley, salt and pepper.","Add the drained, flaked tuna and the onion.","Cook in a non-stick pan, seven minutes per side."],"o":"Cold and in a sandwich, it's one of the best things to take with you."},"415":{"n":"Pickled peppers with tuna","p":["Drain the pickled peppers and pat them dry.","Cut them into wide strips.","Blend half the tuna with the capers and a little oil into a cream.","Spread on the strips and roll them up.","Finish with the remaining tuna in pieces and parsley."],"o":"Two jars that sit at the back of every cupboard, and in a quarter of an hour they become a starter."},"416":{"n":"Baby artichokes with egg","p":["Drain the artichokes in oil and cut them into wedges.","Sauté them in a pan for a minute to dry them out.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, salt and pepper.","Pour into the pan and cook over a low heat like a frittata.","Finish with parsley."],"o":"Drying the artichokes for a minute removes the oiliness and concentrates the flavour."},"417":{"n":"Pasta with mushrooms in oil","p":["Drain the mushrooms in oil and pat them dry on paper.","Sauté them with garlic over a high heat for five minutes.","Add the cream and let it thicken for two minutes.","Toss the pasta with a ladle of cooking water.","Finish with parsley."],"o":"Mushrooms in oil are already seasoned: no extra salt and very little oil needed."},"418":{"n":"Rice with tuna and peas","p":["Stew the chopped onion in oil.","Add the drained peas and the passata, and cook for ten minutes.","Add the drained tuna and turn off the heat.","Cook the rice, drain it and dress it with the sauce.","Serve hot or warm."],"o":"A fifteen-minute dish made entirely from the store cupboard."},"419":{"n":"Cream of pea soup","p":["Stew the onion, then add the cubed potato and the stock.","After fifteen minutes add the peas and cook for five more.","Blend until smooth.","Stir in the cream and adjust the salt.","Finish with mint and a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"The peas go in at the end even when frozen or tinned: cooked for long, they lose their colour."},"420":{"n":"Rice-stuffed tomatoes","p":["Cut the tops off the tomatoes and hollow them out, keeping the pulp.","Blend the pulp with garlic, basil, oil and salt, and stir in the raw rice.","Leave the rice to soak in the juice for an hour: that's what makes it cook properly.","Fill the tomatoes three-quarters full and put the tops back on.","Arrange in a tin with potato chunks and bake at 180 °C for an hour."],"o":"Raw rice soaked in tomato juice is the secret of the Roman recipe."},"421":{"n":"Ten-minute tomato pasta","p":["Put the water on to boil and meanwhile warm the oil with an unpeeled garlic clove.","Pour in the passata and cook over a high heat for eight minutes, seasoning with salt.","Remove the garlic and add the basil off the heat.","Drain the pasta al dente and toss it in the sauce for a minute."],"o":"Over a high heat the tomato reduces in eight minutes: long cooking is only for meat sauces."},"422":{"n":"Tuna and potato bake","p":["Boil and mash the potatoes and mix with eggs, parmesan, salt and pepper.","Spread half in an oiled tin sprinkled with breadcrumbs.","Fill with drained tuna and drained mozzarella.","Cover with the rest of the potatoes and dust with breadcrumbs.","Bake at 200 °C for thirty-five minutes."],"o":"It's a meatless gattò di patate, at a third of the cost."},"423":{"n":"Potato frittata","p":["Slice the potatoes thinly and cook them covered in a pan with oil and onion for twenty minutes.","They should be soft but not falling apart. Let them cool slightly.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, parsley, salt and pepper.","Add the potatoes and pour into a non-stick pan.","Cook for eight minutes per side over a low heat, flipping with a lid."],"o":"If you have leftover boiled potatoes, skip the first step: that's what this frittata is for."},"424":{"n":"Spinach frittata","p":["Blanch the spinach for two minutes, drain and squeeze hard.","Sauté it with garlic for five minutes and let it cool.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, nutmeg, salt and pepper.","Add the roughly chopped spinach.","Cook in a non-stick pan, seven minutes per side."],"o":"Badly squeezed spinach releases water and the frittata stays soft in the middle."},"425":{"n":"Asparagus frittata","p":["Snap off the woody ends of the asparagus by bending them: they break where the tender part ends.","Cut into rounds, keeping the tips whole, and sauté with the spring onion for eight minutes.","Let it cool slightly.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, salt and pepper and add the asparagus.","Cook for seven minutes per side, arranging the tips on top."],"o":"The snapping trick stops you throwing away tender bits and keeping woody ones."},"426":{"n":"Chard frittata","p":["Separate the stalks from the leaves: the stalks need five minutes longer.","Boil everything, drain and squeeze hard, then chop.","Sauté in a pan with garlic and chilli and let it cool.","Beat the eggs with pecorino and add the chard.","Cook for seven minutes per side."],"o":"Stalks and leaves cooked together give the wrong result: the first stay hard, the second fall apart."},"427":{"n":"Pepper frittata","p":["Cut the peppers into thin strips and stew them with the onion for twenty minutes.","Let them cool: piping hot, they'd cook the egg in the bowl.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, salt and pepper.","Add the peppers and torn basil.","Cook for eight minutes per side over a low heat."],"o":"It's even better with leftover peperonata: just drain it of its sauce."},"428":{"n":"Aubergine frittata","p":["Dice the aubergines, salt them and leave them to drain for half an hour.","Pat them dry and brown them in a pan with garlic until golden.","Leave to cool.","Beat the eggs with pecorino and chopped mint.","Add the aubergines and cook for seven minutes per side."],"o":"Mint with aubergine is a Sicilian pairing worth rediscovering."},"429":{"n":"Courgette and mint frittata","p":["Slice the courgettes into thin rounds and sauté them over a high heat with the spring onion.","They should brown, not stew: wide pan and a few at a time.","Let cool slightly and beat the eggs with pecorino and mint.","Add the courgettes and cook for seven minutes per side."],"o":"A high heat and an uncrowded pan are the difference between golden courgettes and boiled ones."},"430":{"n":"Herb frittata","p":["Blanch the leafy greens, squeeze them well and chop them.","Sauté with the sliced spring onion for five minutes.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, parsley and marjoram.","Add the greens and cook over a low heat for eight minutes per side."],"o":"It's the spring frittata made with whatever grew in the fields: change the herbs as you like."},"431":{"n":"Mushroom frittata","p":["Clean the mushrooms with a damp cloth and slice them.","Sauté them with garlic over a high heat until the liquid has evaporated, then salt them.","Let them cool slightly.","Beat the eggs with parmesan and parsley and add the mushrooms.","Cook for seven minutes per side."],"o":"Salt the mushrooms at the end: salt makes them release water and the frittata gets soggy."},"432":{"n":"Spring onion and ricotta frittata","p":["Slice the spring onions, green part included, and stew them for ten minutes.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, salt and pepper.","Add the spring onions and pour half the mixture into the pan.","Dot with spoonfuls of ricotta and cover with the rest of the egg.","Cook over a low heat for eight minutes per side."],"o":"Spoonfuls of ricotta instead of mixing it in leaves soft pockets: a completely different result."},"433":{"n":"Frittata rognosa","p":["Brown the diced salami in a pan with no fat until it releases its own.","Add the chopped onion and let it soften.","Beat the eggs with parmesan and plenty of pepper.","Pour into the pan and cook over a low heat, turning halfway."],"o":"'Rognosa' (mangy) because the surface isn't smooth: the bits of salami make it bumpy."},"434":{"n":"Rice frittata","p":["Use leftover rice, dressed or plain.","Beat the eggs with parmesan and parsley.","Add the cold rice and diced mozzarella.","Press into a non-stick pan with a little oil.","Cook for ten minutes per side over a low heat, flipping with a lid."],"o":"Like pasta frittata, it only exists thanks to leftovers: fresh rice doesn't hold together the same way."},"435":{"n":"Scamorza and cherry tomato frittata","p":["Halve the cherry tomatoes and dry them out in a pan for two minutes.","Beat the eggs with salt, pepper and oregano.","Pour into the pan and scatter over the diced scamorza and the tomatoes.","Cook covered over a low heat, without turning, until the top has set.","Finish with raw basil."],"o":"Covered and not turned: the steam cooks the top and the cheese stays stringy."},"436":{"n":"Broccoli frittata","p":["Blanch the broccoli florets for six minutes and drain well.","Sauté them in a pan with garlic and chilli, crushing them a little.","Leave to cool.","Beat the eggs with pecorino and add the broccoli.","Cook for eight minutes per side."],"o":"It works perfectly with leftover sautéed broccoli from the night before too."},"437":{"n":"Salt cod frittata","p":["Simmer the salt cod for ten minutes, bone it and flake it.","Boil the potatoes and cut them into small cubes.","Beat the eggs with parsley, chopped garlic and pepper: no salt, the cod is enough.","Add the cod and potatoes.","Cook over a low heat for eight minutes per side."],"o":"A typical Friday dish, created to use up leftover salt cod from the day before."},"438":{"n":"Frittata in tomato sauce","p":["Make a plain frittata with eggs, parmesan, salt and pepper.","Cook it on both sides and cut it into wide wedges.","Make a sauce with garlic, passata and basil, cooking for fifteen minutes.","Lay the wedges in the sauce and cook for ten minutes over a gentle heat.","Serve with bread to mop up the sauce."],"o":"Frittata in sauce was the Sunday main course when there was no meat."},"439":{"n":"Courgette flower frittata","p":["Clean the flowers, removing the pistil and stem, and cut them into strips.","Stew the spring onion and add the flowers for just two minutes.","Beat the eggs with parmesan.","Add the flowers and cook over a low heat for six minutes per side."],"o":"The flowers cook in two minutes: any longer and they turn slightly bitter and lose their colour."},"440":{"n":"Leek frittata","p":["Slice the leeks into thin rounds, using only the white and tender green parts.","Wash them well: soil hides between the leaves.","Stew them in oil and butter for twenty minutes with two tablespoons of water.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, cream and nutmeg.","Add the leeks and cook over a low heat for eight minutes per side."],"o":"Long-stewed leeks turn almost as sweet as onions, but more delicate."},"441":{"n":"Ham and cheese frittata","p":["Cut the ham and cheese into small dice.","Beat the eggs with parmesan and plenty of pepper.","Add the ham and cheese.","Cook in a non-stick pan over a low heat, seven minutes per side.","Go easy on the salt: the ham already brings its own."],"o":"The classic fridge-clearer: it works with any cured meat and any cheese that melts."},"442":{"n":"Leftover vegetable frittata","p":["Gather the cooked vegetables you have in the fridge, draining them well of any dressing.","Cut them into even pieces if they're large.","Beat the eggs with parmesan, salt and pepper.","Add the vegetables and basil.","Cook over a low heat for eight minutes per side."],"o":"One egg per hundred grams of vegetables is the ratio that holds it all together."},"443":{"n":"Prawn and courgette frittata","p":["Sauté the diced courgettes with garlic and set aside.","Sear the prawns for two minutes in the same pan.","Beat the eggs with parsley, lemon zest, salt and pepper.","Add the courgettes and prawns and cook over a low heat for six minutes per side.","Don't add cheese: with seafood it drowns everything."],"o":"The no-cheese-with-fish rule really applies here: prawns are delicate."},"444":{"n":"Cauliflower frittata","p":["Boil the cauliflower florets for eight minutes and drain well.","Sauté them with onion and chilli, partly crushing them.","Leave to cool.","Beat the eggs with pecorino and add the cauliflower.","Cook for eight minutes per side over a low heat."],"o":"A piece of stale bread in the cooking water softens the cabbage smell in the house."},"445":{"n":"Leftover boiled meat patties","p":["Chop the leftover boiled meat with a knife or mincer.","Soak the bread in milk and squeeze it out.","Mix the meat, bread, eggs, parmesan and parsley, working it as little as possible.","Shape into patties and coat in breadcrumbs.","Fry in oil, or bake at 200 °C for twenty-five minutes."],"o":"Boiled meat on its own is dry: the bread and milk give it back its softness."},"446":{"n":"Bread balls","p":["Soak the bread in milk for fifteen minutes and squeeze it well.","Mix with eggs, parmesan, and chopped garlic and parsley.","If the mixture is soft add breadcrumbs; if it's stiff, a splash of milk.","Shape into balls and fry, or cook them straight in the sauce.","Cook for twenty minutes in the tomato sauce."],"o":"Poor man's meatballs: not a gram of meat and better than many that have it."},"447":{"n":"Ricotta balls","p":["Drain the ricotta for at least an hour.","Mix it with eggs, parmesan, parsley and half the breadcrumbs.","Shape into balls and roll them in the rest of the breadcrumbs.","Fry in hot oil until golden.","Serve as they are, or dropped into tomato sauce for five minutes."],"o":"Undrained ricotta makes the mixture impossible to shape: this step decides everything."},"448":{"n":"Leftover rice croquettes","p":["Use leftover rice, ideally dressed.","Bind it with an egg and parmesan and let it cool.","Shape into croquettes with a cube of mozzarella in the middle.","Coat in beaten egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in oil at 175 °C until golden."],"o":"They're arancini without the ragù: born the same way, to avoid throwing rice away."},"449":{"n":"Stale bread bake","p":["Cut the bread into cubes and put them in a buttered ovenproof dish.","Scatter over the diced ham.","Beat milk, eggs, parmesan, salt, pepper and nutmeg.","Pour over the bread and leave it to soak for ten minutes.","Bake at 180 °C for twenty-five minutes."],"o":"The quickest way to turn half a kilo of old bread into dinner."},"450":{"n":"Bread and cheese soup","p":["Stew the sliced onions in butter for twenty minutes.","Toast the sliced bread.","In an ovenproof dish layer bread, onions and sliced cheese.","Pour in boiling stock almost to cover.","Bake at 180 °C for twenty-five minutes, until golden."],"o":"Three leftovers — old bread, ends of cheese and an onion — become a mountain dish."},"451":{"n":"Leftovers savoury tart","p":["Gather the cooked vegetables and cheeses you have in the fridge.","Drain them well: moisture is the enemy of a savoury tart.","Beat the eggs, cream and parmesan.","Roll out the shortcrust, prick the base and spread the filling over it.","Pour in the mixture and bake at 180 °C for thirty-five minutes."],"o":"Rule of thumb: three eggs and 150 ml of cream hold about 400 grams of filling."},"452":{"n":"Leftover pasta bake","p":["Use leftover dressed pasta, or boil some very al dente.","If there's not much, stretch it with a little sauce and béchamel.","Layer it in an oiled dish with mozzarella and parmesan.","Sprinkle with breadcrumbs.","Bake at 200 °C for twenty-five minutes."],"o":"Leftover pasta baked comes out better than pasta made for the purpose: it has already absorbed the sauce."},"453":{"n":"Use-it-up tray pizza","p":["Turn the oven to maximum, 250 °C if it goes that high, and let it heat for at least ten minutes: the pizza depends on it.","Spread the base in the tray on its paper without stretching it: if you force it, it shrinks as it cooks.","Season the passata with salt, a drizzle of oil and oregano, then spread it, leaving two fingers' border.","Bake on the lowest shelf for 10 minutes: first the base dries out, then the cheese goes on.","Slice the mozzarella, squeeze it in kitchen paper, scatter it over and return to the oven for 5 minutes until melted."],"o":"The secret is dry mozzarella added later: if it goes in wet at the start, the pizza comes out soggy."},"454":{"n":"Pinsa with leftover vegetables","p":["Cut the leftover vegetables into strips, even if already cooked: anything left in the fridge works here.","If they're raw, sauté them for five minutes over a high heat with a little oil and salt, since they won't have time to cook on the pinsa.","Heat the oven to 240 °C and bake the base on its own first for 6 or 7 minutes, until rigid to the touch.","Scatter over the mozzarella and vegetables and return to the oven for 5 more minutes.","Out of the oven, add hand-torn basil and a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Pinsa wants two bakes: first the base on its own, then with the topping. That's what keeps it crisp underneath."},"455":{"n":"White pizza with potato and rosemary","p":["Slice the potatoes very thinly, ideally on a mandoline, and keep them in cold water for ten minutes to remove the starch.","Dry them well and toss in a bowl with oil, salt and chopped rosemary needles.","Spread the base in the tray and lay the slices like scales, barely overlapping, leaving no gaps.","Bake at 230 °C for 20 minutes, until the edges of the potatoes darken.","While still hot, add another drizzle of oil and coarse salt crushed between your fingers."],"o":"Without the cold-water soak, the potatoes stay rubbery instead of crisping at the edges."},"456":{"n":"Use-it-up baked calzone","p":["Drain the mozzarella well and cut it into small dice: inside a calzone the water has nowhere to go.","Roll out the base and top only half the disc, leaving a two-finger border all round.","Add the passata, pieces of ham and the mozzarella, without overdoing it: an overfilled calzone bursts in the oven.","Fold into a half-moon, seal the edge with the tines of a fork and make two cuts on top to let steam out.","Bake at 230 °C for 18 minutes, until puffed and golden."],"o":"The two cuts on top aren't for show: they let the steam out, and without them the calzone splits at the side."},"457":{"n":"Pinsa with mortadella and ricotta","p":["Work the ricotta with a fork, a pinch of salt and a tablespoon of oil until it's a smooth cream.","Bake the base on its own at 240 °C for 8 minutes, with nothing on it.","Let it cool for a couple of minutes: on a scorching base the ricotta heats up and turns grainy.","Spread the cream, lay the mortadella on top in curled ribbons and serve at once."],"o":"This one never goes back in the oven: cooked mortadella loses its fragrance and becomes something else."},"458":{"n":"Empty-fridge pizza","p":["Rinse the capers under running water and dry them, otherwise they bring all their salt with them.","Season the passata with oregano, oil and very little salt: anchovies, olives and capers bring plenty of savouriness.","Spread the base in the tray, cover with the tomato and scatter over the olives and capers.","Bake at 250 °C for 12 minutes on the low shelf.","The anchovies go on in the last two minutes, or even with the oven off: cooked for long, they turn bitter."],"o":"It's the pizza you make when there's nothing fresh left: everything keeps in the cupboard for months."},"459":{"n":"Seupa à la vapelenentse","p":["Cut the savoy cabbage into strips and blanch it for ten minutes in salted water, then drain.","Slice the stale rye bread fairly thinly.","Butter an ovenproof dish and layer bread, cabbage and sliced fontina, four or five layers.","Dust the top layer with cinnamon and pour in boiling stock almost to cover.","Dot the top with butter and bake at 200 °C for 40 minutes, until well browned."],"o":"It comes from Valpelline, born so as not to waste old bread: the staler the bread, the better it soaks up the stock."},"460":{"n":"Chnéfflene","p":["Beat flour, eggs, milk and a pinch of salt into a thick batter that falls from the spoon in ribbons.","Let the batter drip in small drops from a board straight into boiling salted water, helping it with a wet knife.","Lift out the dumplings as soon as they float, after a couple of minutes.","Toss them in melted butter until lightly golden.","Scatter with shavings of toma and chives, and let it melt for a minute over a low heat."],"o":"It's the Walser version of spätzle, brought by the German-speaking communities of Gressoney."},"461":{"n":"Chnolle","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel them while still hot and mash finely.","Knead with flour, egg and a pinch of salt into a soft but workable dough.","Roll into ropes and cut irregular dumplings with a knife, as for potato spätzle.","Cook in boiling salted water and drain as soon as they float.","Toss in melted butter with diced fontina until slightly stringy."],"o":"A direct relative of chnéfflene, but with potato instead of flour alone: softer and more rustic."},"462":{"n":"Gnocchi alla bava","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel them while hot and pass them through a ricer.","Knead with flour and egg into a soft dough, working it as little as possible.","Roll into 2 cm thick ropes, cut the gnocchi and ridge them with a fork.","Melt the fontina pieces in hot milk over a bain-marie, stirring into a stringy cream.","Boil the gnocchi, drain them as soon as they float and dress with the fontina fondue and melted butter."],"o":"The 'bava' is the thread of fontina that trails from the spoon: if the fondue doesn't stretch, the milk was too hot."},"463":{"n":"Fettuccine alla valdostana","p":["Cut the ham into strips and the fontina into small dice.","Melt the butter in a pan and briefly brown the ham.","Pour in the cream, heat without boiling and add the fontina, stirring until it starts to melt.","Cook the fettuccine al dente and drain them, keeping a ladle of cooking water.","Toss them in the sauce, loosening with the water if needed, and finish with nutmeg."],"o":"More a tavern recipe than a farmhouse one, but now a classic on every Aosta Valley menu."},"464":{"n":"Chamois civet","p":["Marinate the meat in pieces in the wine with chunks of vegetables, juniper and bay for at least twelve hours.","Drain the meat, dry it well and brown it in oil over a high heat, a little at a time.","Return the vegetables from the marinade and let them soften, then dust with flour and stir for two minutes.","Pour in the strained wine and water to cover, put the lid on and cook over a very low heat for two and a half hours.","If the sauce is still thin, take out the meat and reduce it over a high heat before serving."],"o":"The gaminess of chamois is softened by the long marinade: without it, the meat stays stringy."},"465":{"n":"Motsetta (cured mountain meat)","p":["Rub the pieces of leg with coarse salt, crushed juniper, and chopped bay and rosemary.","Leave in the fridge under a weight for a week, turning the meat every two days.","Rinse off the excess salt and dry the meat well.","Hang in a cool, airy, dark place for four to six weeks, until firm to the touch.","Slice very thinly and serve as a starter with alpine butter and dark bread."],"o":"It's a cured meat, not a cooked dish: the curing time can't be shortened in any way."},"466":{"n":"Costoletta alla valdostana","p":["Butterfly each chop without detaching the bone, making a pocket.","Fill with a slice of ham and sliced fontina, then close it, pressing the edges well.","Coat in flour, beaten egg and breadcrumbs, sealing the opening well.","Melt plenty of butter in a pan and fry over a medium heat, 5 minutes per side.","Drain on paper and serve at once, while the fontina inside is still stringy."],"o":"If the opening isn't sealed properly the fontina escapes and burns in the butter: better to coat it twice in breadcrumbs."},"467":{"n":"Lardo di Arnad with chestnuts and honey","p":["Slice the lardo very thinly, almost transparent, with a very sharp knife.","Gently warm the cooked chestnuts, cutting them in half.","Toast the slices of rye bread.","Lay the lardo on the still-hot bread so it just starts to melt.","Finish with the warm chestnuts and a drizzle of chestnut honey."],"o":"The warmth of the bread is what melts the lardo slightly: cold on cold misses half the point."},"468":{"n":"Tegole valdostane","p":["Roughly chop the hazelnuts and mix them with the flaked almonds, sugar and flour.","Add the lightly beaten egg whites and the lukewarm melted butter, to a sticky mixture.","Spread very thin spoonfuls onto baking paper, well apart: they spread a lot.","Bake at 180 °C for 8-10 minutes, until the edges are golden and the centre barely coloured.","Lift them off the tray at once and drape over a rolling pin to give them a curved tile shape."],"o":"Take them out a little before it seems right: out of the oven they harden in seconds."},"469":{"n":"Mecoulin","p":["Dissolve the yeast in lukewarm milk with a spoonful of sugar and leave to activate for ten minutes.","Knead the flour with the eggs, the yeasted milk and the remaining sugar into a smooth dough.","Add the soft butter a little at a time, working the dough well between additions.","Add the floured raisins and leave to rise, covered, for two hours until doubled.","Shape into a ball, put it in a round tin, let it rise for another hour and bake at 180 °C for 35 minutes."],"o":"It's the festive sweet bread of the Aosta Valley: the yellow, stringy crumb comes from butter, not colourings."},"470":{"n":"Fiadone valdostano","p":["Knead flour, cold butter in pieces, sugar, eggs and a pinch of salt into a smooth dough.","Wrap in cling film and chill for an hour.","Roll the dough thinly and cut out 10 cm discs.","Put a teaspoon of grappa-scented jam in the centre, fold into a half-moon and seal the edges.","Fry in oil at 170 °C until golden, or bake at 190 °C for 20 minutes."],"o":"They're the fried ravioli of the Aosta Valley carnival: bilberry jam is the most traditional filling there."},"471":{"n":"Carne cruda all'albese","p":["Chop the meat very finely with a knife, without blitzing it.","Dress immediately with oil, salt, pepper and lemon juice, mixing gently.","Spread in a thin layer on individual plates.","Finish with parmesan shavings and very thinly sliced raw mushrooms."],"o":"The knife is essential: a mixer heats the meat and makes it pasty instead of silky."},"472":{"n":"Battuta di Fassona","p":["Chop the meat with a knife to a fine texture, without mincing it.","Dress with oil, salt, pepper and a few drops of lemon just before serving.","Serve at once, without letting it stand: undressed raw meat loses its colour quickly."],"o":"It's the most essential version of carne all'albese, with nothing added: here only the quality of the meat speaks."},"473":{"n":"Finanziera","p":["Blanch the sweetbreads and cockscombs separately in boiling water, then peel off the membranes.","Lightly flour all the offal and brown it in butter over a high heat, one type at a time.","Add the sliced mushrooms and let them take on flavour for five minutes.","Deglaze with marsala and vinegar and reduce until the sauce is glossy.","Serve piping hot, traditionally as an intermezzo between the first and main course of an important meal."],"o":"The offal dish par excellence: born in noble kitchens so that nothing of the animal was wasted."},"474":{"n":"Piedmontese fritto misto","p":["Cook the semolina in milk to a firm pudding, pour into a shallow tray and leave to cool, then cut into diamonds.","Slice the meats and sweet items into small, even pieces.","Coat each piece, sweet and savoury, in beaten egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in oil or clarified butter at 170 °C, keeping the sweet items separate from the savoury.","Assemble the mixed plate with all the small fried pieces together."],"o":"Golden rule: at least seven different items, sweet and savoury together, in very small portions."},"475":{"n":"Piedmontese bollito misto","p":["Put the beef in cold water with the vegetables and bring slowly to the boil: it should stay juicy, not overcooked.","Cook over a low heat for two hours, skimming often.","Boil the hen and tongue separately in their own broth, peeling the tongue while still hot.","Cook the cotechino separately, pricking it, for at least an hour.","Slice all the meats and serve them together, hot, with bagnet verd and coarse salt on the side."],"o":"Each meat has its own cooking time and its own pot: cooking them all together ruins the flavours."},"476":{"n":"Tajarin al ragù","p":["Knead the flour with yolks only, work it for a long time and rest for an hour.","Roll the pasta thin and cut 2 mm tajarin.","Make a classic ragù with soffritto, meat, wine and passata, cooking for an hour and a half.","Cook the tajarin for just under a minute in salted water.","Dress with a not-too-generous amount of ragù and parmesan."],"o":"With so many yolks the pasta is already rich: use the ragù sparingly so as not to smother it."},"477":{"n":"Rabaton","p":["Blanch the chard, squeeze it very hard and chop it.","Mix with ricotta, parmesan, eggs and flour into a firm mixture.","Shape small oval quenelles with two spoons.","Cook them in boiling salted water: they're ready when they float.","Dress with sage butter and more parmesan."],"o":"They're the Piedmontese gnudi, more rustic than the Tuscan ones: the name comes from the French 'quenelle'."},"478":{"n":"Barolo risotto","p":["Soften the chopped onion in half the butter without colouring.","Toast the rice for two minutes, then add a third of the Barolo and let it absorb.","Add the hot stock a ladle at a time, adding the rest of the wine halfway through.","After about sixteen minutes the risotto should be a deep, rich purple.","Off the heat, beat in cold butter and parmesan."],"o":"Add the wine in two stages, not all at once: that way it colours without masking the taste of the rice."},"479":{"n":"Paniscia novarese","p":["Cook the beans with the pork rind in plenty of water, keeping their broth.","Brown the crumbled salami with the chopped onion.","Toast the rice, deglaze with red wine and add the savoy cabbage in strips.","Add the bean broth and vegetable stock a ladle at a time for sixteen minutes.","Halfway through add the beans and finish stirring: it should be nice and thick."],"o":"It's the risotto of the Novara lowlands: a close relative of the Vercelli panissa, with more vegetables and less lardo."},"480":{"n":"Bagnet verd","p":["Soak the bread in vinegar for five minutes, then squeeze it lightly.","Finely chop the parsley, garlic and anchovies, or pound them in a mortar.","Add the squeezed bread and keep working into a coarse mixture.","Loosen with oil in a thin stream to a creamy sauce.","Leave to rest for at least an hour before serving with boiled meats."],"o":"It's the standard sauce for Piedmontese bollito misto: make it with a knife or mortar, never blended too fine."},"481":{"n":"Caponet","p":["Half-cook the rice and drain it.","Mix the meat, rice, parmesan, egg, salt and pepper into a firm filling.","Clean the courgette flowers by removing the pistil and fill them gently.","Close by folding over the petal tips and arrange them in an oiled dish.","Spoon over thinned passata and cook covered in a pan, or bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes."],"o":"The flowers are very delicate: fill them by hand without pressing."},"482":{"n":"Friciulin","p":["Roughly chop the leftover vegetables, boiled or raw depending on the type.","Make a batter with flour, eggs, milk, parmesan and a pinch of salt.","Add the vegetables to the batter and mix well.","Drop spoonfuls into hot oil and fry until golden on both sides.","Drain on paper and serve hot, as a starter or side."],"o":"A use-it-up dish par excellence: leftover savoy cabbage, chard and courgettes all become fritters."},"483":{"n":"Baci di dama","p":["Grind the toasted hazelnuts very finely, almost to a flour.","Knead with flour, soft butter and sugar to a smooth mixture.","Shape into balls the size of a hazelnut and space them out on baking paper.","Bake at 160 °C for 15 minutes, until the base is just golden.","Melt the chocolate and use it to join two biscuits at the base, like a kiss."],"o":"Don't flatten the balls: they stay round and spread only slightly by themselves in the oven."},"484":{"n":"Gianduiotti","p":["Toast the hazelnuts and blend them for a long time into a smooth, oily paste.","Melt the milk chocolate over a bain-marie with the cocoa butter.","Add the hazelnut paste and icing sugar, stirring to a smooth mixture.","Spread into a 1 cm layer between two sheets of baking paper and chill for an hour.","Cut into wedges with a hot knife before it sets completely."],"o":"The triangular ingot shape is a historic trademark: it was made to be picked up with your fingers without getting messy."},"485":{"n":"Krumiri","p":["Beat the soft butter with the sugar until pale and fluffy.","Add the yolks one at a time, then the vanilla.","Fold in the two sifted flours, working as little as possible.","Pipe moustache-shaped sticks, slightly curved.","Bake at 170 °C for 15 minutes, until the edges are just golden."],"o":"The curved moustache shape is a tribute to Victor Emmanuel II's moustache: it isn't a detail, it's the biscuit's signature."},"486":{"n":"Paste di meliga","p":["Knead the two flours with soft butter, sugar, egg and lemon zest.","Work it only until combined, without warming the dough.","Shape 2 cm sticks by hand, or use a piping bag.","Space them out on baking paper.","Bake at 180 °C for 15 minutes, until golden at the edges but still pale in the centre."],"o":"Fine cornmeal gives the classic sandy texture: with coarse cornmeal the biscuits stay hard."},"487":{"n":"Hazelnut cake","p":["Chop the hazelnuts not too finely, leaving some pieces for texture.","Whisk the yolks with the sugar until pale.","Add the hazelnuts, flour, baking powder and lukewarm melted butter.","Whisk the egg whites to stiff peaks and fold them in gently from the bottom up.","Pour into a buttered tin and bake at 170 °C for 40 minutes."],"o":"It's meant to be low and moist in the centre: it shouldn't turn out like a dry sponge."},"488":{"n":"Moscato zabaione","p":["Put the yolks and sugar in a bowl and whisk until pale.","Add the Moscato in a thin stream, still whisking.","Cook over a gentle bain-marie, whisking without ever stopping, for eight to ten minutes.","The mixture is ready when it triples in volume and coats the spoon.","Serve at once, warm, in small glasses, perhaps with plain biscuits."],"o":"Never stop whisking: a moment's pause over the heat and the yolks scramble."},"489":{"n":"Pasta with tenerumi","p":["Trim the tenerumi, removing the toughest strings, and break them up with your hands.","Fry garlic in oil, add the crushed cherry tomatoes and cook for five minutes.","Add the tenerumi and the courgette in chunks, cover with water and cook for twenty minutes.","Add the pasta straight into the soup and cook, stirring, adding water if needed.","Finish with basil and a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Tenerumi are the tender leaves of the long Sicilian courgette: found only in summer, at southern greengrocers."},"490":{"n":"Busiate with Trapani pesto","p":["Scald and peel the tomatoes and remove the seeds.","Pound almonds, garlic and basil in a mortar with a pinch of salt.","Add the tomatoes and keep pounding into a coarse cream.","Add oil in a thin stream and the pecorino, still pounding.","Dress the busiate, drained al dente, with the raw sauce, without returning to the heat."],"o":"Busiate, rolled by hand around a thin rod, hold pesto far better than smooth pasta."},"491":{"n":"Pasta alla carrettiera","p":["Fry garlic and chilli in plenty of oil.","Add the halved cherry tomatoes and cook for ten minutes over a high heat.","Cook the pasta al dente and drain it, keeping the cooking water.","Toss it in the sauce with a ladle of water.","Finish with torn basil and grated pecorino."],"o":"It began as a quick dish for Sicilian cart drivers: few ingredients, all already in the cupboard."},"492":{"n":"Fish couscous","p":["Make a fish stock with the heads and bones, onion, tomato and bay leaf, straining it after half an hour.","Stew garlic and chopped almonds, then cook the pieces of firmer fish in their stock.","Steam the couscous in a couscoussier, moistening it with hot stock in several stages.","Rub the couscous apart with oiled hands between additions of stock.","Serve the couscous with the fish and the remaining stock poured on the side."],"o":"It's Trapani's signature dish, arrived from North Africa: the secret is repeated steaming, not a quick soak."},"493":{"n":"Stocco alla messinese","p":["Stew the onion and celery chunks in plenty of oil for fifteen minutes.","Add cherry tomatoes, olives and rinsed capers and cook for ten minutes.","Lay the stockfish pieces on the sauce and cover with large chunks of potato.","Add water just to cover, put the lid on and cook over a low heat for an hour, shaking the pot instead of stirring.","Leave to rest for ten minutes before serving."],"o":"Unlike the Calabrian ghiotta, celery plays a bigger role here: it's the Messina signature of the dish."},"494":{"n":"Sicilian meat pizzaiola","p":["Brown the slices of meat for two minutes per side in very hot oil and set aside.","In the same pan fry the garlic, then add the passata, season with salt and cook for ten minutes.","Return the meat to the sauce and sprinkle with oregano.","Cook for another five minutes over a medium heat, until the sauce thickens slightly.","Serve hot, with bread to mop up the sauce."],"o":"A very simple, very quick dish: the meat must never overcook in the sauce, or it turns tough."},"495":{"n":"Sfincione palermitano","p":["Knead flour, yeast, 300 ml of lukewarm water and salt, and leave to rise for two hours.","Stew the sliced onions in oil until sweet and translucent.","Spread the dough thickly in an oiled tin and let it rise for another thirty minutes.","Top with the onions, passata, broken-up anchovies and chunks of caciocavallo.","Sprinkle with breadcrumbs and a drizzle of oil and bake at 220 °C for 25 minutes."],"o":"The base is thick and spongy, very different from pizza: the breadcrumbs on top make it crisp without drying it out."},"496":{"n":"Scacce ragusane","p":["Knead flour, lukewarm water, oil and salt into a smooth, elastic dough and leave to rest for an hour.","Roll out a very thin rectangular sheet.","Spread a filling of your choice, for example passata, onion and caciocavallo, over half the sheet.","Fold it over several times like a book, as for puff pastry, sealing each fold.","Place in a tin and bake at 200 °C for 30 minutes until golden."],"o":"The book-folding is what creates the layers: it's closer to puff pastry than to a calzone."},"497":{"n":"Focaccia messinese","p":["Knead flour, yeast, water and salt and leave to rise for two hours.","Blanch the escarole, squeeze it and sauté with a drizzle of oil.","Roll out two discs of dough and fill with escarole, sliced tuma, cherry tomatoes and anchovies.","Lay the second disc on top and seal the edges well.","Bake at 220 °C for 25 minutes, until golden on both sides."],"o":"It's closed like a flat calzone: the fresh tuma inside stays soft and stringy."},"498":{"n":"Pane cunzato","p":["Cut the bread into thick slices and toast lightly.","Dress while still hot with plenty of oil, so the bread soaks it up.","Top with sliced tomato, anchovies and caciocavallo shavings.","Finish with oregano, salt and more raw oil.","Serve at once, while the cheese is just beginning to soften."],"o":"The bread must be dressed while hot: that's what makes all the difference from a simple bruschetta."},"499":{"n":"Crocchè or cazzilli","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel them while hot and mash them.","Season with an egg, chopped parsley and mint, salt and pepper.","Shape into long cylinders with damp hands.","Coat in the remaining beaten egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in hot oil until golden and crisp on the outside."],"o":"The mint is what sets them apart from ordinary potato croquettes: very little, but you must taste it."},"500":{"n":"Pani ca meusa","p":["Cut the spleen and lung into thin strips.","Melt the lard in a large pan and fry the offal over a high heat until well cooked.","Drain off the excess fat and sprinkle with lemon juice.","Open the warm semolina rolls and fill them with the meat.","If you like, add shavings of caciocavallo or ricotta salata before closing."],"o":"It's eaten 'schietto', with just lemon, or 'maritato' with cheese: the two classic versions."},"501":{"n":"Milza e stigghiola","p":["Wash the intestines thoroughly several times in water and lemon.","Wrap them around a spring onion or a sprig of parsley to form small rolls.","Thread the rolls onto cane or metal skewers.","Cook over hot embers, turning often, until well charred and crisp outside.","Season with coarse salt and serve at once with lemon wedges."],"o":"It's cooked in the street, over open embers: the smoke is part of the flavour and hard to reproduce at home."},"502":{"n":"Messina-style meat rolls","p":["Mix breadcrumbs, caciocavallo, pine nuts and soaked raisins with a drizzle of oil.","Spread the filling on the pounded slices of meat and roll them up tightly.","Thread the rolls onto skewers, alternating with bay leaves and onion wedges.","Cook in a pan or on the grill, turning often, for fifteen minutes.","Serve hot, perhaps with a squeeze of lemon."],"o":"They're close cousins of swordfish rolls, with the same sweet-and-sour filling but meat instead of fish."},"503":{"n":"Orange salad","p":["Peel the oranges, removing all the white pith.","Slice them into rounds, not too thin.","Slice the red onion very thinly and leave it in cold water for ten minutes.","Arrange the plate, alternating oranges, onion and olives.","Dress with oil, a pinch of salt, oregano and, if you like, a sprinkling of chilli."],"o":"The sweet-bitter contrast of orange with olive and onion is the whole dish: no vinegar, it would ruin the balance."},"504":{"n":"Anelletti timbale","p":["Make a ragù with meat, passata and peas, cooking for forty minutes.","Fry thinly sliced aubergines and drain on kitchen paper.","Cook the anelletti very al dente and dress with the ragù and diced caciocavallo.","Line a mould with the fried aubergines, fill with the dressed pasta and close by folding the aubergines over the top.","Sprinkle with breadcrumbs and bake at 180 °C for 30 minutes, then turn it out upside down."],"o":"Turned out of the mould it becomes a real pasta cake: let it rest for ten minutes before turning it over."},"505":{"n":"Granita with brioche","p":["Blend the almonds with the water until the liquid turns white and milky.","Strain through a cloth, squeezing well to extract all the almond milk.","Dissolve the sugar in the almond milk and pour into an ice-cream maker, or a shallow freezer container.","If using the freezer, stir with a fork every thirty minutes to break up the crystals, for about three hours.","Serve in a glass with a warm brioche alongside, for dunking."],"o":"The quintessential Sicilian summer breakfast: the brioche never goes into the granita, but alongside."},"506":{"n":"Brioche col tuppo","p":["Dissolve the yeast in lukewarm milk with a spoonful of sugar.","Knead the flour with the eggs, the yeasted milk and the remaining sugar into a smooth dough.","Work in the soft butter in small pieces, a little at a time.","Leave to rise for two hours, then divide the dough into balls with a smaller ball on top, the tuppo.","Leave to rise for another forty minutes and bake at 180 °C for 15 minutes."],"o":"The tuppo, a hair bun, is the shape that makes it recognisable: without it, it's just any brioche."},"507":{"n":"Frutta martorana","p":["Grind the almonds very finely, almost to a powder.","Knead with the icing sugar and water into a firm, smooth paste, the pasta reale.","Leave to rest wrapped in cling film for an hour.","Divide into portions, colour each with a few drops of food colouring and shape the fruits by hand.","Paint the details with a small brush dipped in colouring and leave to dry in the air for a day."],"o":"Pasta reale is worked by hand, like clay: that's why every piece is slightly different."},"508":{"n":"Almond paste sweets","p":["Grind the almonds finely with the sugar, almost to a flour.","Add the egg white and grated zest, kneading to a firm mixture.","Shape into balls or small ovals with slightly damp hands.","Arrange them on baking paper and leave to dry for a couple of hours.","Bake at 150 °C for ten minutes, just enough to colour the base slightly."],"o":"They mustn't really bake: just warm them enough to form a thin crust."},"509":{"n":"Sfinci di San Giuseppe","p":["Make choux pastry by bringing water and butter to the boil, adding the flour all at once and drying out the dough.","Off the heat beat in the eggs one at a time, waiting until each is absorbed.","Fry spoonfuls of dough in oil that isn't too hot, so they puff up well, for five minutes.","Sieve the ricotta with the sugar into a smooth cream, adding chocolate chips and candied peel.","Fill the warm sfinci with the ricotta cream and finish with more candied peel."],"o":"Unlike cannoli: the dough is fried choux, not shortcrust, and they're filled while warm, not cold."},"510":{"n":"Buccellato siciliano","p":["Knead flour, lard, sugar and water into a firm dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Roughly chop dried figs, soaked raisins and almonds, and mix with honey and cinnamon.","Roll the dough into a rectangle, spread the filling along a strip and roll it up into a log.","Bend it into a ring, joining the two ends.","Score the top and bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes."],"o":"The ring shape is linked to Christmas: the cuts let steam escape from the figs while baking."},"511":{"n":"Fried iris","p":["Knead flour, yeast, two eggs and lukewarm water into a soft dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Sieve the ricotta with sugar and chocolate chips for the cream.","Shape the dough into balls, flatten them, fill with the ricotta cream and close well.","Coat in the remaining beaten egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in oil at 170 °C until golden and puffed."],"o":"They're halfway between a brioche roll and a sfincia: eaten hot, with sugar melting just outside."},"512":{"n":"Genovesi ericine","p":["Knead flour, lard, sugar, an egg and water into a soft shortcrust. Rest for an hour.","Make a thick pastry cream with yolk, sugar, flour and lemon-scented milk.","Line round moulds with the pastry, fill with cold cream and close with a disc of pastry.","Seal the edges and make a small hole in the centre.","Bake at 190 °C for 25 minutes, until golden, and serve warm dusted with icing sugar."],"o":"They come from Erice, near Trapani, and are eaten warm: cold cream inside warm pastry is the point."},"513":{"n":"Cuccìa","p":["If the wheat isn't already cooked, soak it overnight and boil for two hours until soft and glossy.","Drain the cooked wheat well and let it cool slightly.","Sieve the ricotta with the sugar into a smooth cream.","Fold the wheat into the ricotta cream gently.","Finish with chocolate chips, candied peel and a pinch of cinnamon, and serve cold."],"o":"It's made for St Lucy's day, 13 December, when in Sicily neither bread nor pasta is eaten."},"514":{"n":"Mustazzoli","p":["Warm the honey slightly, without boiling, and pour it over the flour.","Add the roughly chopped almonds, the cinnamon and ground cloves.","Knead into a firm, dark mixture.","Roll out 1 cm thick and cut into diamonds.","Bake at 170 °C for 15 minutes: they stay soft in the centre and harden as they cool."],"o":"These are keeping biscuits: they improve after a few days in a tin."},"515":{"n":"Gelo di mellone","p":["Blend the deseeded watermelon flesh and pass it through a sieve for a clear juice.","Mix the cornflour into a little cold juice, then add it to the rest with the sugar.","Cook over a gentle heat, stirring, until it thickens, about ten minutes.","Pour into glasses and let it cool, then chill for at least three hours.","Decorate with chocolate shavings and, if available, jasmine petals."],"o":"It's a pudding, not an ice despite the name: 'gelo' refers to the jelly-like set it takes on as it cools."},"516":{"n":"Sfoglio delle Madonie","p":["Knead flour, lard and lukewarm water into a smooth, elastic dough. Rest for an hour.","Grate the fresh pecorino and mix it with sugar, chocolate chips and cinnamon.","Roll the dough out so thin you could read through it.","Spread the filling over one half, fold over and seal the edges well.","Fry in hot oil until golden, or bake at 190 °C for 25 minutes."],"o":"It's the mountain relative of the sfince, typical of the Madonie: sweet and savoury together, like much Sicilian filled pastry."},"517":{"n":"Culurgiones ogliastrini","p":["Knead semolina and water into a firm, elastic dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Boil the potatoes, mash them and mix with grated pecorino and chopped mint and garlic.","Roll the dough thinly and cut out 8 cm discs.","Fill each disc and close by pinching the edges into an ear of wheat, overlapping the folds.","Boil in salted water until they float and dress with tomato sauce or just butter and pecorino."],"o":"The wheat-ear closure is their hallmark: it takes practice, but without it they aren't culurgiones."},"518":{"n":"Fregula with clams","p":["Purge the clams in salted water for a couple of hours.","Open them in a pan with garlic, white wine and a drizzle of oil, then strain the cooking liquid.","In the same pan fry more garlic, add the cherry tomatoes and cook for five minutes.","Toast the dry fregula for a minute, then cook it in the clam liquid topped up with water, like a risotto.","At the end add the shelled clams and parsley."],"o":"Fregula, an oven-toasted Sardinian couscous, is cooked like a risotto: it absorbs the liquid a little at a time."},"519":{"n":"Fregula with sausage","p":["Crumble the sausage and brown it in oil with the chopped onion.","Add a splash of wine if you like, then the passata, and cook for fifteen minutes.","Toast the dry fregula in a pan for a minute.","Add it to the sauce with stock or hot water a ladle at a time, cooking like a risotto for twelve minutes.","Stir in grated pecorino off the heat."],"o":"Fregula absorbs more liquid than ordinary pasta: don't be afraid to add more if it seems to dry out too soon."},"520":{"n":"Lorighittas","p":["Knead the semolina with lukewarm water into a smooth, firm dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Roll into strands as thin as thick spaghetti.","Wind each strand in a spiral around two fingers, twisting the ends together into a small braided ring.","Boil in salted water for about ten minutes: they're fairly thick.","Dress with a simple sauce of tomato, garlic and basil, and pecorino."],"o":"The hand-braided shape is unique to Morgongiori: it takes practice, the first ones will be uneven."},"521":{"n":"Filindeu","p":["Knead the semolina with water and salt into a very elastic dough, working it for a long time.","Stretch the dough by hand into very fine threads, like hair.","Lay the threads criss-crossed in three layers on a cloth, forming a mesh sheet.","Let the sheet dry, then break it into portions.","Cook briefly in boiling mutton broth and serve with fresh pecorino shavings."],"o":"The name means 'threads of God': it's considered the most difficult pasta in Italy, handed down only by word of mouth."},"522":{"n":"Pane carasau","p":["Knead semolina, yeast, lukewarm water and salt into a firm dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Roll out very thin discs, as wide as a plate.","Bake in a very hot oven, 250 °C, until the disc puffs up like a balloon, a couple of minutes.","As soon as it comes out, split the puffed disc into two thin sheets with a knife.","Return the sheets to the oven for a few minutes until dry and crisp, almost golden."],"o":"It's also called 'music paper' for its thinness: the balloon puffing up is the sign it's working."},"523":{"n":"Suppa cuata","p":["Soften the sheets of carasau by dipping them quickly in hot stock.","Line the base of a dish with a layer of bread, then sprinkle with grated pecorino and mint.","Repeat the layers until everything is used, finishing with cheese.","Pour in hot stock almost to cover and dot with butter.","Bake at 200 °C for 30 minutes, until a golden crust forms on top."],"o":"It isn't a soup for spoons: after the oven it becomes almost a firm savoury bake, to be cut into slices."},"524":{"n":"Fregula soup","p":["Fry the chopped onion in oil.","Add chopped tomatoes and diced potatoes and let them take on flavour for five minutes.","Cover with stock or water and cook for twenty minutes until the potatoes are almost soft.","Add the fregula and cook for another ten minutes, stirring.","Serve with grated pecorino and a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"It's the most homely, frugal version of fregula, meant as a hearty soup rather than a dry dish."},"525":{"n":"Cocciula e cozzas","p":["Purge the clams in salted water and clean the mussels by scraping the shells.","Fry the garlic in plenty of oil.","Add the shellfish and deglaze with white wine.","Cover and cook over a high heat until they all open, five minutes.","Sprinkle with chopped parsley and serve at once with their cooking liquid."],"o":"Cook them to order, just until open: left on the heat too long they turn rubbery."},"526":{"n":"Burrida","p":["Clean the fish, cut it into steaks and fry it lightly in oil.","Finely chop walnuts, garlic and parsley into a coarse paste.","Thin the paste with vinegar and a little water into a thick sauce.","Lay the fried fish in a container and cover it with the sauce.","Leave to marinate in the fridge for at least a day before serving, cold."],"o":"A dish to make the day before: kept cool, the fish takes on flavour and keeps longer."},"527":{"n":"Clams marinara","p":["Purge the clams in salted water for a couple of hours, changing the water.","Fry garlic and chilli in oil.","Add the clams and deglaze straight away with white wine.","Cover and cook over a high heat until they open, four or five minutes.","Sprinkle with fresh parsley and serve with their juices."],"o":"Discard any that stay closed after cooking: it means they were already dead before cooking."},"528":{"n":"Lobster alla catalana","p":["Boil the lobsters in salted water for ten minutes, then let them cool.","Cut the tomatoes into small dice and slice the onion very thinly.","Halve the lobsters lengthways and partly shell them.","Arrange the plate with the lobster on top of the tomato and onion.","Dress with oil, salt and lemon juice, serving warm or cold."],"o":"The Catalan heritage comes from Alghero: here lobster is eaten cold, dressed like a salad, not hot off the grill."},"529":{"n":"Porceddu","p":["Clean the suckling pig and rub it inside and out with coarse salt.","Fill the cavity with sprigs of myrtle and bay.","Put it on the spit and secure it well.","Cook at a distance from the embers, turning slowly and steadily, for about two and a half hours.","Towards the end, move it slightly closer to the embers to crisp the skin."],"o":"Slow cooking at a distance is what makes the meat meltingly tender under a crisp skin: hurrying ruins it."},"530":{"n":"Sa cordula with peas","p":["Wash the intestines at length and plait them around the offal, forming tight braids.","Fry the onion in oil, then brown the braided cordula over a high heat.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Add chopped tomato and a little water, cover and cook for forty minutes.","Add the peas for the last ten minutes."],"o":"The braid must be tight: if it loosens while cooking, the offal escapes into the sauce."},"531":{"n":"Pecora in cappotto","p":["Brown the pieces of mutton in oil with the chopped onion until well coloured.","Add the chopped tomato and bay leaf, cover with water and cook over a low heat for an hour and a half.","Add the potatoes in large chunks, almost covering the meat like a coat.","Cook for another forty minutes, until the potatoes are soft and the sauce has reduced.","Leave to rest for ten minutes before serving."],"o":"The potatoes almost completely cover the meat as it cooks, hence the name: 'mutton in an overcoat'."},"532":{"n":"Lamb with artichokes","p":["Clean the artichokes and keep them in water and lemon until needed.","Brown the lamb in oil with garlic over a high heat until well coloured.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Add the artichokes cut into wedges and a little water, cover and cook for thirty minutes.","Adjust the salt and let the sauce reduce in the last few minutes."],"o":"Sardinian lamb is small and lean: it cooks faster than mainland lamb, so check it often."},"533":{"n":"Roast kid","p":["Rub the pieces of kid with crushed garlic, rosemary, salt and oil.","Arrange them in a tin alternating with potato chunks.","Pour over the white wine and a drizzle of oil.","Roast at 200 °C for an hour, turning the pieces halfway.","Raise the temperature to 220 °C for the last ten minutes to brown the skin well."],"o":"Kid is leaner than lamb: baste it often with the juices, or it dries out in the oven."},"534":{"n":"Favata sarda","p":["Soak the dried broad beans overnight, then drain.","Brown the ribs and sausage pieces in oil with the chopped onion.","Add the beans and cover generously with water.","Add the wild fennel and cook over a low heat for two hours, until the beans start to break up.","Adjust the salt only at the end, since the meats already salt the dish."],"o":"It's the winter pork dish par excellence: born to use all the humbler cuts along with the garden's broad beans."},"535":{"n":"Sardinian sausage","p":["Finely chop the dried wild fennel.","Mix the meat with the fennel, chilli, salt and a little cold water.","Leave to take on flavour in the fridge for at least an hour.","Stuff into natural casing, tying the sausages every ten centimetres.","Grill over embers for twenty minutes, turning often, or hang to cure."],"o":"Dried rather than fresh wild fennel gives Sardinian sausage its recognisable flavour."},"536":{"n":"Griddled casizolu","p":["Cut the casizolu into 1 cm thick slices.","Heat a griddle or non-stick pan well, without adding fat.","Cook the slices for two minutes per side, until a golden crust forms.","Sprinkle with oregano as soon as it comes off the heat.","Serve immediately, while the cheese is still stringy inside."],"o":"Casizolu, a stretched-curd cheese similar to provola, is cooked very briefly: crisp outside, stringy inside."},"537":{"n":"Sebadas","p":["Knead semolina, lard, lukewarm water and a pinch of salt into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Slice the pecorino thinly and warm it just enough to soften it.","Roll the dough very thinly and cut out discs, placing the cheese between two discs and sealing the edges well.","Fry in very hot oil until golden and puffed, turning once.","Serve hot, drizzled with warm honey."],"o":"The pecorino should be young but slightly sour: that contrast with the honey makes the dish."},"538":{"n":"Sardinian amaretti","p":["Grind the almonds finely with half the sugar.","Whisk the egg whites with the remaining sugar to soft peaks.","Fold in the ground almonds and lemon zest gently.","Shape balls with two spoons and place them on baking paper, flattening them slightly.","Bake at 160 °C for 18 minutes, until golden outside and soft inside."],"o":"They stay soft in the centre even when cold: if they dry out completely, they were in the oven too long."},"539":{"n":"Pabassinas","p":["Soak the raisins in warm water for ten minutes, then squeeze them.","Roughly chop the walnuts and almonds.","Knead flour, lard, sugar, baking powder, raisins and nuts into a firm mixture.","Shape into flattened diamonds by hand, a couple of centimetres thick.","Bake at 180 °C for 20 minutes, until golden."],"o":"The name comes from 'pabassa', grapes dried in the sun: the one ingredient that can't be left out."},"540":{"n":"Gueffus","p":["Grind the almonds very finely, almost to a flour.","Knead with the sugar, a little water and the lemon zest into a firm, workable paste.","Shape into small balls the size of a walnut.","Roll them in icing sugar until completely coated.","Leave to dry for a couple of hours before serving."],"o":"They're the traditional Sardinian wedding sweets: the icing sugar coating makes them pure white."},"541":{"n":"Acciuleddi","p":["Knead flour and eggs into a firm, elastic dough. Rest for twenty minutes.","Roll out thin sheets and cut long strips.","Plait the strips into small braids or spirals.","Fry in hot oil until golden and crisp.","Drain and dip them while still hot in warm honey scented with orange zest."],"o":"Frying makes them crisp, the warm honey makes them shine: dip them in the honey straight out of the oil."},"542":{"n":"Aranzada","p":["Peel the oranges, taking the zest without the bitter white pith.","Cut the zest into very thin strips and blanch it three times in fresh water each time, to remove the bitterness.","Warm the honey in a pan until runny.","Add the strips of zest and the toasted almonds, mixing well.","Spread the mixture on wet baking paper, flatten it and let it cool before cutting into diamonds."],"o":"The three blanchings of the zest are essential: without them, the bitterness of the peel dominates the whole sweet."},"543":{"n":"Torrone di Tonara","p":["Heat the honey over a bain-marie for about an hour, stirring now and then, until it thickens slightly.","Whisk the egg whites to very stiff peaks.","Fold the whites into the hot honey a little at a time, still cooking over the bain-marie and stirring constantly.","After about another hour, when the mixture is thick and pale, add the toasted nuts.","Pour onto wafer paper or baking paper, level it and let it cool completely before cutting."],"o":"The very long bain-marie cooking is what makes this torrone pale and chewy, not hard like industrial torrone."},"544":{"n":"Sospiri sardi","p":["Grind the almonds finely and mix them with the flour.","Whisk the eggs with the sugar until pale and foamy.","Fold in the ground almonds and lemon zest.","Shape balls with two spoons and place them on baking paper.","Bake at 170 °C for 15 minutes, then let cool and cover with an icing of icing sugar and water."],"o":"The thin white icing on top is what recalls a small, pure white 'sigh'."},"545":{"n":"Neapolitan pizza","p":["Knead flour, water, yeast and salt into a smooth, even dough.","Leave to rise, covered, for at least six hours at room temperature, ideally eight.","Shape into 250 g balls and leave to rise for another two hours.","Stretch each ball by hand from the centre outwards, leaving a thick rim.","Top with crushed peeled tomatoes, mozzarella and basil, and bake in the hottest oven possible, ideally above 400 °C, for a few minutes."],"o":"The long rise at room temperature is what makes the dough digestible: shortening it changes the whole result."},"546":{"n":"Pizza fritta","p":["Knead flour, water, yeast and salt into a soft dough. Leave to rise for three hours.","Divide into balls and roll them into discs.","Fill half of each disc with ricotta, ciccioli (pork cracklings) and pieces of provola.","Fold into a half-moon and seal the edges well, pressing with a fork.","Fry in plenty of hot oil until golden, then serve with a spoonful of tomato on top."],"o":"It's the oldest fried pizza of Naples, born in the street when an oven was a luxury."},"547":{"n":"Pasta e patate with provola","p":["Fry chopped onion and celery in oil.","Add the potato chunks and crushed tomato and let them take on flavour for five minutes.","Cover with hot water and cook until the potatoes start to break up, about fifteen minutes.","Add the pasta and cook, stirring often, adding water if it gets too dry.","Turn off the heat and stir in the diced provola, which should melt in the residual heat."],"o":"Add the provola off the heat: if it cooks it turns stringy and rubbery instead of creamy."},"548":{"n":"Neapolitan genovese","p":["Slice the onions finely: you'll need an amount that seems excessive.","Brown the whole piece of meat with carrot and celery, then add the onions.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Cover and cook over a very low heat for three hours, stirring often: the onions should melt into a cream.","Chop or shred the meat, return it to the sauce and dress the broken ziti, boiled al dente."],"o":"Despite the name there's nothing Ligurian about it: it was born in Naples, perhaps named after Genoese cooks working in the city."},"549":{"n":"Paccheri alla napoletana","p":["Fry the garlic in plenty of oil.","Add the halved cherry tomatoes and cook over a lively heat for ten minutes, until they break down.","Cook the paccheri al dente in boiling salted water.","Toss them in the sauce for a couple of minutes, loosening with cooking water if needed.","Finish with hand-torn basil and grated cheese."],"o":"Piennolo cherry tomatoes, sweet and not watery, are the right choice: they need nothing else."},"550":{"n":"Sartù di riso","p":["Make a ragù with minced meat, crumbled sausage and passata, cooking for an hour.","Boil the rice very al dente and dress it with some of the ragù, parmesan and a raw egg.","Line a buttered, breadcrumbed mould with the rice, forming a shell high up the sides.","Fill with peas, diced mozzarella, hard-boiled egg wedges and the rest of the ragù.","Close with more rice, sprinkle with breadcrumbs and bake at 190 °C for 30 minutes, then turn it out upside down."],"o":"A festive timbale, born in the kitchens of the French 'monsù' chefs at the Bourbon court: the shell structure is its signature."},"551":{"n":"Neapolitan lasagne","p":["Make a ragù with crumbled sausage and passata, cooking for an hour and a half.","Shape small meatballs with minced meat and fry them briefly.","Boil the lasagne sheets if they aren't pre-cooked and drain them on a cloth.","Build the layers, alternating pasta, ragù, dollops of ricotta, diced mozzarella, meatballs and sliced hard-boiled eggs.","Finish with parmesan and bake at 190 °C for 35 minutes, covered with foil for the first twenty."],"o":"The meatballs and hard-boiled eggs in the layers set it apart from béchamel-based Emilian lasagne."},"552":{"n":"Alici 'ndurate e fritte","p":["Clean the anchovies, removing the backbone and head, and open them out like a book.","Rinse and dry them well on kitchen paper.","Dredge them in flour, shaking off the excess.","Fry in very hot oil for a few minutes, until golden and crisp.","Drain on paper and serve hot with lemon wedges."],"o":"'Ndurate' simply means floured: the frying must be very quick or the anchovies dry out."},"553":{"n":"Casatiello","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm water and lard into an elastic dough. Leave to rise for an hour.","Roll out into a rectangle and scatter over pancetta, diced salami and grated pecorino.","Roll up tightly into a log and close it into a ring in a mould.","Press whole, well-washed eggs in their shells into the top, securing each with two crossed strips of dough.","Leave to rise for an hour and bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes."],"o":"The raw eggs in their shells hard-cook directly in the oven: they're the Easter symbol of casatiello."},"554":{"n":"Tortano","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm water and lard into an elastic dough. Leave to rise for an hour.","Roll out into a rectangle and scatter over pancetta, salami and diced provolone, with plenty of black pepper.","Roll up tightly and close into a ring on a baking tray.","Leave to rise for an hour, until doubled.","Bake at 190 °C for 40 minutes, until the top is well browned."],"o":"It's casatiello's brother but without the whole eggs on top: simpler to make, just as tasty."},"555":{"n":"Cuoppo napoletano","p":["Clean all the fish, cutting the squid into rings.","Rinse and dry every ingredient well.","Dredge everything in flour, a little at a time, shaking off the excess.","Fry in very hot oil, separating by type of fish since cooking times vary.","Fill the cuoppo, the paper cone, mixing the various fried pieces, and serve with lemon."],"o":"It's eaten walking, with your hands, freshly made: it was born as seafront street food."},"556":{"n":"Friarielli and sausage","p":["Trim the friarielli, removing the toughest stems.","Brown the sausage in pieces in a pan until well coloured, then set aside.","In the same pan fry garlic and chilli.","Add the friarielli still damp from washing and sauté over a high heat, covering for a few minutes to wilt them.","Return the sausage to the pan and finish cooking together for five minutes."],"o":"Friarielli, Neapolitan turnip tops, are only ever sautéed: boiled first, they lose their typical bitterness."},"557":{"n":"Sfogliatella riccia and frolla","p":["Cook the semolina in the milk to a firm cream, then let it cool.","Mix the cooled semolina with ricotta, sugar and candied peel.","For the riccia: roll the dough very thinly, brush with lard, roll it into a tight tube and cut into small discs.","Open each disc into a cone by pulling the layers, fill it and close to a point.","For the frolla: use a dough of flour, lard and sugar, cutting out discs to fill and fold into half-moons. Bake both at 200 °C for 20 minutes."],"o":"The riccia has paper-thin crisp layers, the frolla is softer and biscuity: two different pastries with the same filling."},"558":{"n":"Delizia al limone","p":["Make a sponge by whisking the eggs with the sugar and gently folding in the flour. Bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes.","Cut the sponge into small domes and soak them with a syrup of water, sugar and lemon juice.","Make a lemon pastry cream with yolks, sugar, milk and grated zest.","Whip the cream and fold it into the cold pastry cream to make a diplomat cream.","Fill and cover the domes with the cream, smoothing the surface into a drop shape."],"o":"The smooth dome shape is deliberate: it should look like a drop, the symbol of this Sorrento dessert."},"559":{"n":"Graffa napoletana","p":["Boil and mash the potatoes and let them cool slightly.","Knead flour, potatoes, yeast dissolved in lukewarm water, eggs and sugar into a soft dough.","Work in the soft butter and leave to rise for two hours, until doubled.","Roll out 2 cm thick and cut rings with two cutters of different sizes.","Leave to rise for another forty minutes, then fry in oil at 170 °C and roll in granulated sugar while still hot."],"o":"The potato in the dough is what makes it so soft: without it, it's more like an ordinary doughnut."},"560":{"n":"Orecchiette with turnip tops","p":["Trim the turnip tops, keeping only the tender leaves and florets.","Fry garlic, chilli and anchovies in oil until the anchovies melt.","Boil the orecchiette in salted water, adding the turnip tops to the same pot halfway through.","Drain pasta and greens together, keeping a ladle of cooking water.","Toss everything in the garlic oil, loosening with the water if needed."],"o":"Cooking the greens in the pasta water is what really binds them to it, rather than a separate sauce."},"561":{"n":"Orecchiette with braciole ragù","p":["Pound the slices of meat thin and spread chopped pecorino, parsley and garlic on each.","Roll up tightly and tie with kitchen string to form the braciole.","Brown the braciole in oil until well coloured.","Add the passata and cook over a very low heat for two hours, until the meat is very tender.","Dress the orecchiette with the sauce and serve the braciole separately as a main course."],"o":"The long-cooked sauce of the braciole dresses the pasta: the meat is then served separately as a main course."},"562":{"n":"Cavatelli with chickpeas","p":["Fry garlic and rosemary in oil.","Add the cherry tomatoes and cook for five minutes.","Add the chickpeas with a little of their cooking water and let them take on flavour for ten minutes.","Blend some of the chickpeas to thicken, leaving the rest whole.","Cook the cavatelli straight in the chickpea sauce thinned with water, like a thick pasta dish."],"o":"Blending only half the chickpeas gives the right creaminess while keeping the texture of the rest."},"563":{"n":"Tiella barese with rice, potatoes and mussels","p":["Clean the mussels and open them raw with a knife, keeping the liquid they release.","Oil a dish and make a layer of thinly sliced potatoes, onion and courgettes.","Arrange the mussels in their shells, with raw rice scattered over them.","Cover with cherry tomatoes, pecorino and a second light layer of potatoes.","Pour in the strained mussel liquid and water almost to cover, and bake at 190 °C for 45 minutes."],"o":"The rice is scattered raw straight over the mussels: it cooks by absorbing all the liquid in the dish, without ever being stirred."},"564":{"n":"Salento mussel and potato tiella","p":["Clean the mussels and open them raw, keeping just one half-shell for each.","Line a shallow dish with thinly sliced potatoes.","Arrange the mussels in their shells over the potatoes, then sliced tomato and chopped garlic and parsley.","Sprinkle with breadcrumbs and a generous drizzle of oil.","Bake at 190 °C for 40 minutes, until the potatoes are soft and the top golden."],"o":"Without rice, here the breadcrumbs form the crust that the rice itself forms in the Bari version."},"565":{"n":"Fave e cicorie","p":["Cook the split broad beans in unsalted water with half an onion for about forty minutes, until they break down.","Beat vigorously with a wooden whisk into a smooth purée, the 'macco'.","Boil the wild chicory in salted water and drain.","Plate the hot bean purée with the chicory alongside.","Dress both with plenty of raw extra virgin olive oil."],"o":"The signature dish of Apulian peasant cooking: good raw olive oil is the only real dressing."},"566":{"n":"Acquasale","p":["Cut the stale bread into thick slices.","Dip them quickly in cold salted water, just enough to soften without falling apart.","Squeeze lightly and arrange in a deep plate.","Cover with chopped tomato, thinly sliced onion and oregano.","Dress with plenty of raw extra virgin olive oil and serve at once."],"o":"The name comes from the salted water used to moisten the bread: it's the cold summer soup of farm labourers."},"567":{"n":"Panzerotti","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm water, oil and salt into a smooth dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Roll out 12 cm discs.","Fill half of each disc with tomato and diced mozzarella.","Fold into a half-moon and seal well by pressing the edges with a fork.","Fry in very hot oil until puffed and golden on both sides."],"o":"They're small fried calzoni: drain the mozzarella well, or the panzerotto bursts in the oil."},"568":{"n":"Focaccia barese","p":["Mash the boiled potatoes while still warm.","Knead flour, potatoes, yeast and lukewarm water into a very soft, sticky dough.","Leave to rise for two hours, then tip it straight into an oiled tin without working it too much.","Spread it with oiled fingers to fill the tin, then press cherry tomatoes and olives into the dough.","Sprinkle with oregano and a drizzle of oil, and bake at 220 °C for 20 minutes."],"o":"The potato in the dough gives the moist, open crumb typical of focaccia barese."},"569":{"n":"Focaccia salentina","p":["Knead flour, semolina, yeast and lukewarm water into a soft dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Spread it in the tin with your fingers, without a rolling pin.","Scatter over soaked sun-dried tomatoes and thinly sliced onion.","Sprinkle with oregano and a generous drizzle of oil.","Bake at 220 °C for 20 minutes."],"o":"With semolina in the dough the crust is crisper than the softer Bari version."},"570":{"n":"Puccia","p":["Knead flour, semolina, yeast, lukewarm water and a drizzle of oil into a smooth dough.","Work in whole or pitted olives, spreading them through the dough.","Leave to rise for two hours, until doubled.","Shape into round, flattened rolls and leave to rise for another thirty minutes.","Bake at 220 °C for 20 minutes, then cut in half and fill as you like."],"o":"It's the Salento bread roll par excellence: cut into a pocket and filled with vegetables in oil or cold cuts."},"571":{"n":"Friselle with tomato","p":["Pass the friselle quickly under cold water, a couple of seconds per side.","Leave them to soften for a minute, without letting them fall apart.","Rub a garlic clove lightly over the surface.","Cover with tomato cut into small pieces and its juice.","Dress with oil, salt and oregano and serve at once."],"o":"A frisella is already twice-baked and only needs wetting, not long soaking: a few seconds is enough."},"572":{"n":"Taralli","p":["Knead flour, wine, oil, fennel seeds and salt into a firm, smooth dough.","Leave to rest, covered, for thirty minutes.","Roll into thin sticks and close them into rings, sealing the ends well.","Blanch them in boiling water for a few seconds until they float, then drain.","Dry them on a cloth and bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes, until golden and crunchy."],"o":"Blanching before baking is what makes them crunchy rather than crumbly like an ordinary savoury biscuit."},"573":{"n":"Bombette della Valle d'Itria","p":["Place a piece of caciocavallo, pancetta and chopped parsley on each slice of capocollo.","Roll up tightly to form small parcels.","Thread the parcels onto skewers, four or five at a time.","Grill over embers or in a pan, turning often, for fifteen minutes.","Serve hot, just as the cheese inside starts to ooze."],"o":"Cook them over a high heat and turn often: small parcels cook quickly and mustn't dry out."},"574":{"n":"Gnummareddi","p":["Wash the intestines and offal at length in water and lemon.","Cut the offal into strips and season with chopped parsley and garlic.","Wrap each strip in the intestine together with a bay leaf, forming tight little rolls.","Thread onto skewers alternating with more bay leaves.","Cook over hot embers, turning often, for fifteen to twenty minutes."],"o":"Elsewhere in the South they're called 'turcinelli': tight wrapping is what keeps them compact as they cook."},"575":{"n":"Cozze arraganate","p":["Open the mussels raw with a knife, removing the empty half-shell and keeping the mussel in the other.","Mix breadcrumbs, chopped garlic and parsley with a drizzle of oil.","Arrange the mussels in a dish and top each with a piece of cherry tomato and a teaspoon of the crumbs.","Drizzle with more oil.","Bake at 200 °C for 15 minutes, until the crumbs are golden."],"o":"Open them raw with a knife, not by steaming: that way they stay juicy and don't dry out in the oven."},"576":{"n":"Polpo alla pignata","p":["Clean the octopus and cut it into large pieces.","Put everything into a terracotta pot, the pignata, with cherry tomatoes, garlic, chilli and oil, without water.","Cover and cook over a very low heat: the octopus releases its water and cooks in its own liquid.","Simmer for an hour and a half, stirring rarely so as not to break up the pieces.","Sprinkle with fresh parsley and serve hot, perhaps with crostini."],"o":"Terracotta and a very low heat are the key: with no added water, the octopus tenderises in its own juices."},"577":{"n":"Stuffed cuttlefish","p":["Clean the cuttlefish, keeping the bodies whole and chopping the tentacles.","Mix breadcrumbs, chopped tentacles, garlic and parsley with a drizzle of oil.","Fill the bodies with the stuffing without overfilling, and close with a cocktail stick.","Brown the stuffed cuttlefish in oil, then add the cherry tomatoes.","Cook covered over a low heat for twenty minutes, until tender."],"o":"Don't overfill the bodies: they shrink as they cook and too much filling would burst them."},"578":{"n":"Broad bean purée","p":["Cook the beans with half an onion and a bay leaf in unsalted water until they break down, about forty minutes.","Remove the bay leaf and beat vigorously with a whisk until smooth.","Adjust the thickness with a little cooking water if needed.","Salt only at the end.","Serve hot with plenty of raw extra virgin olive oil, perhaps with crostini."],"o":"It's the base of Apulian macco: without chicory alongside it's a simple purée, also excellent as a spread."},"579":{"n":"Cartellate","p":["Knead flour, wine, oil and water into a firm, elastic dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Roll out thin sheets and cut 2 cm wide strips.","Roll each strip up into a spiral, pinching the edges to create the typical 'roses'.","Fry in hot oil until golden and crisp.","Drain and dip them while still hot into warm vincotto."],"o":"The rolled rose shape symbolises the Christ Child's swaddling bands: the Apulian Christmas sweet par excellence."},"580":{"n":"Sporcamuss","p":["Make a thick pastry cream with yolks, sugar, starch and milk, cooking until it coats the spoon.","Leave to cool completely with cling film pressed onto the surface.","Cut the puff pastry into rectangles and bake at 200 °C for 15 minutes, until puffed and golden.","Split each rectangle in half and fill generously with the cold cream.","Dust with icing sugar, which will inevitably get all over your mouth, hence the name."],"o":"The name ('dirty mouth') comes from getting covered in icing sugar as you eat it: that's part of the experience."},"581":{"n":"Pettole","p":["Dissolve the yeast in lukewarm water with the sugar.","Mix the flour with the yeasted water and a pinch of salt into a soft, almost liquid batter.","Leave to rise, covered, for two hours, until bubbles appear on the surface.","Scoop spoonfuls of batter with a wet spoon and drop them into the hot oil.","Fry until golden and puffed, drain and roll in sugar or drizzle with vincotto."],"o":"The batter is deliberately very soft, almost liquid: that's what gives the irregular shape and fluffy inside."},"582":{"n":"Apulian bocconotti","p":["Knead flour, butter, sugar and egg into a smooth shortcrust. Rest for thirty minutes.","Line small moulds with the pastry, leaving an edge for closing.","Fill with sour-cherry jam and chopped almonds.","Close with a disc of pastry, sealing the edges well.","Bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes, until golden."],"o":"They're eaten whole, in one mouthful ('boccone'), hence the name: sour-cherry jam is the most traditional filling."},"583":{"n":"Cavolo nero farinata","p":["Fry onion and garlic in oil.","Add the cavolo nero in strips and let it wilt for ten minutes.","Add the beans and cover with plenty of water, bringing to the boil.","Pour in the cornmeal in a stream, stirring constantly to avoid lumps.","Cook, stirring often, for twenty-five minutes until thick, and serve with a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"It's halfway between a soup and a soft polenta: if it gets too thick, loosen it with hot water."},"584":{"n":"Farro and bean soup","p":["Soak the farro for a couple of hours if it isn't pearled.","Fry chopped carrot, celery and onion, then add the chopped tomato.","Add the beans, cooked or soaked raw, with plenty of water.","Cook for an hour, then add the farro and the cavolo nero in strips.","Cook for another twenty-five minutes, until the farro is soft, and serve with raw oil."],"o":"It's ribollita's grandmother: without the bread, it stays a lighter soup closer to the Garfagnana tradition."},"585":{"n":"Pici all'aglione","p":["Knead flour, water and a drizzle of oil into a firm, elastic dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Roll out the dough and cut wide strips, then roll them by hand on the board into thick spaghetti.","Fry plenty of chopped garlic with the chilli, without letting it colour too much.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and cook for twenty minutes to a thick sauce.","Boil the pici, drain them al dente and dress with the sauce and parmesan."],"o":"The garlic must really be plentiful, almost a whole head: that's what gives the sauce its name, 'aglione'."},"586":{"n":"Pappardelle with wild boar","p":["Marinate the meat in pieces in red wine with chunks of vegetables for at least twelve hours.","Drain and brown the meat in oil over a high heat, then add the vegetables from the marinade.","Deglaze with the strained wine, add the passata and cover with water.","Cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meat shreds easily, then shred it into the sauce.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, roll thick sheets and cut 2 cm wide pappardelle; boil and dress with the ragù."],"o":"The long marinade removes the meat's overly strong gaminess: without it, the sauce is too pungent."},"587":{"n":"Tagliatelle with mushrooms","p":["Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, roll out a sheet and cut 1 cm wide tagliatelle.","Clean the mushrooms with a damp cloth and slice them.","Fry the garlic in oil, add the mushrooms and cook over a high heat for ten minutes.","Add the cream and let it take on flavour.","Boil the tagliatelle, drain them al dente and toss in the sauce with fresh parsley."],"o":"Cook mushrooms over a high heat with no lid, otherwise they release too much water and stew instead of browning."},"588":{"n":"Tortelli mugellani","p":["Boil and mash the potatoes and mix them with the browned minced meat, parmesan and nutmeg.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, rolling it thin.","Place small heaps of filling spaced out over half the sheet and cover with the other half.","Cut out square tortelli, sealing the edges well with your fingers.","Boil in salted water and dress with meat ragù or just butter and sage."],"o":"The potato and meat filling, unusual for tortelli, is the signature of the Mugello area."},"589":{"n":"Gnudi","p":["Blanch the spinach, squeeze it very hard and chop finely.","Mix with ricotta, parmesan, egg and a pinch of nutmeg.","Add a little flour, just enough for body, and shape into small ovals.","Roll them lightly in the remaining flour and boil them in salted water.","Drain as soon as they float and dress with sage butter and parmesan."],"o":"The name means 'naked': ravioli filling without the pasta round it. Keep the flour to the bare minimum."},"590":{"n":"Cacciucco alla livornese","p":["Fry half the garlic with chilli, add the cuttlefish in strips and brown.","Deglaze with red wine, add the peeled tomatoes and cook for thirty minutes.","Add the firmest fish first, then the more delicate, and the prawns last.","Cook covered over a low heat for another twenty minutes, until all the fish is cooked.","Rub the slices of bread with the remaining garlic, put them in the bowls and pour the cacciucco over."],"o":"Tradition calls for at least five kinds of fish, never just one: variety is the whole point of the dish."},"591":{"n":"Florentine tripe","p":["Cut the tripe into thin strips.","Fry chopped carrot, celery and onion in oil.","Add the tripe and let it take on flavour for ten minutes over a high heat.","Add the passata and cook over a low heat for an hour, until the sauce has reduced.","Serve hot with plenty of grated parmesan."],"o":"Tripe already boiled at length by the tripe seller shortens the cooking: here it just needs to absorb the sauce."},"592":{"n":"Lampredotto","p":["Put the lampredotto in a pot with carrot, celery, onion and tomato, covering with water.","Bring to the boil and cook over a low heat for about two hours, until very tender.","Drain and cut into thin strips.","Serve in the traditional bread roll, dipped in its cooking broth.","Add green sauce or chilli sauce as you like."],"o":"The roll is dipped in the broth on the top half too, the 'bagnato': part of Florence's street-food tradition."},"593":{"n":"Bistecca alla fiorentina","p":["Take the meat out of the fridge at least an hour ahead: it must reach room temperature.","Heat the embers or grill until very hot.","Cook the steak for five minutes per side without ever piercing it, turning it only once.","Stand the steak upright on the bone for a minute to cook the fillet side.","Salt only after cooking, with coarse salt, never before."],"o":"It must be served rare, strictly: cooking it further betrays the cut and the breed."},"594":{"n":"Peposo dell'Impruneta","p":["Put the meat in pieces in a terracotta pot with unpeeled garlic and peppercorns.","Cover completely with red wine and add the passata.","Bring to the boil, then turn the heat to minimum and cook uncovered for three hours, without stirring too much.","The sauce should reduce and thicken naturally, and the meat becomes very tender.","Adjust the salt only at the end and serve piping hot."],"o":"Born in the brick kilns of Impruneta, cooked slowly in the still-warm kilns: plenty of pepper is its signature."},"595":{"n":"Arista di maiale","p":["Make cuts in the meat in several places and insert rosemary sprigs and garlic cloves.","Tie the meat with kitchen string to keep its shape.","Brown it in oil on all sides until the crust is well coloured.","Deglaze with white wine and transfer to the oven at 180 °C for an hour, basting now and then with the juices.","Leave to rest for ten minutes before slicing."],"o":"The name comes from the Greek 'aristos', the best: it was the cut chosen for banquets at Florence's medieval church councils."},"596":{"n":"Chicken liver crostini","p":["Clean the chicken livers, removing any greenish parts, and chop roughly.","Fry the chopped onion in oil until well softened.","Add the livers and brown for five minutes over a high heat.","Deglaze with vin santo, add capers and anchovies and cook for another ten minutes, then chop finely or blitz.","Spread on toasted bread crostini while warm."],"o":"The right texture is coarse, not a smooth pâté: chop with a knife, don't blend for long."},"597":{"n":"Lardo di Colonnata","p":["Slice the lardo very thinly with a very sharp knife.","Toast the slices of bread lightly.","Lay the lardo on the still-hot bread so it just begins to melt.","Finish with a sprig of fresh rosemary, broken up.","Serve at once, before the lardo loses its translucency."],"o":"Cured in marble tubs with herbs: the warmth of the bread is what really brings out its aroma."},"598":{"n":"Cantucci with Vin Santo","p":["Knead flour, sugar, baking powder and eggs into a soft mixture.","Add the whole almonds, spreading them through the dough.","Shape into two long logs and place them on baking paper.","Bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes, until golden and firm to the touch.","Slice diagonally while still hot and return to the oven for five minutes per side to crisp."],"o":"The double baking makes them hard and crunchy: that hardness is exactly what calls for vin santo to dunk them in."},"599":{"n":"Schiacciata all'uva","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm water and a drizzle of oil into a soft dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Divide the dough into two parts, one slightly larger.","Spread the larger part in a tin and cover with half the grapes and sugar.","Cover with the second layer of dough, press the remaining grapes into it and sprinkle with sugar and rosemary.","Bake at 200 °C for 35 minutes."],"o":"It's the cake of the September grape harvest: the grapes release their juice as it bakes, soaking the dough from inside."},"600":{"n":"Buccellato lucchese","p":["Dissolve the yeast in a little lukewarm milk.","Knead flour, eggs, sugar, soft butter and the activated yeast into a smooth dough.","Add the soaked raisins and aniseeds, spreading them well.","Shape into a ring or loaf and leave to rise for two hours, until doubled.","Bake at 180 °C for 35 minutes, until the top is well browned."],"o":"Whole aniseeds are the signature of Lucca's buccellato: without them it's just a raisin sweet bread."},"601":{"n":"Bucatini all'amatriciana","p":["Cut the guanciale into thick strips and brown it in a pan without oil over a medium heat until crisp.","Lift it out and set aside, leaving the melted fat in the pan.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and chilli, and cook for fifteen minutes.","Boil the bucatini al dente, drain and toss in the sauce.","Return the crispy guanciale and stir in plenty of pecorino."],"o":"Take the guanciale out and add it back at the end: cooked in the sauce it loses its crunch, which is the point of the dish."},"602":{"n":"Spaghetti alla carbonara","p":["Cut the guanciale into strips and brown over a medium heat without oil until crisp.","Beat the yolks with grated pecorino and plenty of black pepper into a thick cream.","Boil the spaghetti al dente and drain, keeping a ladle of cooking water.","Add them to the guanciale off the heat, stirring.","Pour in the yolk cream and stir vigorously away from the heat, loosening with cooking water until creamy."],"o":"The cream is made off the heat: eggs in direct contact with heat scramble instead of staying silky."},"603":{"n":"Rigatoni con la pajata","p":["Cut the pajata into 15 cm lengths, tying the ends.","Fry the onion, then brown the pajata pieces over a high heat.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and cook over a low heat for an hour, until the sauce has reduced.","Boil the rigatoni, dress with the sauce and serve the pajata separately with pecorino."],"o":"The milk naturally inside the intestine turns into a kind of ricotta as it cooks: that's the secret of the dish."},"604":{"n":"Gricia","p":["Cut the guanciale into strips and brown over a medium heat without oil until crisp.","Set the guanciale aside, leaving the melted fat in the pan.","Boil the pasta al dente and drain, keeping the cooking water.","Toss it in the guanciale fat with a ladle of water, off the heat.","Stir in plenty of pecorino and black pepper, returning the crispy guanciale."],"o":"It's also called white amatriciana: same base, no tomato. Perhaps the oldest of the four Roman pastas."},"605":{"n":"Gnocchi alla romana","p":["Bring the milk to the boil with a pinch of salt and half the butter.","Pour in the semolina in a stream, stirring constantly to avoid lumps.","Cook for ten minutes until thick, then take off the heat and beat in the yolks and half the parmesan.","Spread the mixture about 1 cm thick on a wet surface and leave to cool completely.","Cut out discs with a glass, overlap them in a dish, cover with melted butter and parmesan and bake at 200 °C for 15 minutes."],"o":"They aren't boiled like potato gnocchi: they're semolina discs baked in the oven, overlapping like roof tiles."},"606":{"n":"Abbacchio alla romana","p":["Brown the pieces of lamb in oil with garlic and rosemary until well coloured.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Cover and cook over a low heat for forty minutes, adding water if needed.","Melt the anchovies in the vinegar and pour into the pan for the last five minutes.","Stir well to bind the sauce and serve hot."],"o":"Roman milk-fed lamb is small and tender: the anchovy and vinegar dressing at the end is the real signature of this version."},"607":{"n":"Coratella with artichokes","p":["Clean the artichokes and keep them in water and lemon until needed, then cut into wedges.","Cut the coratella (lamb's pluck) into pieces, separating the livers, which cook faster.","Brown the spring onions, then the coratella over a high heat, adding the livers last.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Add the artichokes and cook for another twenty minutes, until tender."],"o":"Add the livers last, they cook in a few minutes: cooked too long they turn rubbery."},"608":{"n":"Pajata","p":["Cut the pajata into 10 cm lengths, tying each end with string.","Brown the pieces in oil with onion and bay leaf.","Deglaze with white wine.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and cook over a low heat for an hour, until the sauce has reduced.","Serve hot as a main course, with its sauce."],"o":"Tying the ends is essential: it traps the milk inside during cooking, the real speciality of the dish."},"609":{"n":"Porchetta di Ariccia","p":["Lay the boned meat on the board, skin side down.","Spread salt, chopped garlic, rosemary and wild fennel over the whole inner surface.","Roll it up tightly from one side, with the skin wrapping the outside.","Tie firmly with kitchen string at regular intervals.","Roast at 160 °C for three hours, then raise to 220 °C for the last thirty minutes to crackle the skin."],"o":"The skin must be entirely on the outside of the roll: that's what becomes the characteristic crackling."},"610":{"n":"Puntarelle with anchovy sauce","p":["Clean the puntarelle, cutting the shoots into thin strips.","Put them in iced water for at least an hour, until they curl.","Pound in a mortar or finely chop the garlic and anchovies.","Combine with oil and vinegar into a creamy, emulsified sauce.","Drain the puntarelle well and dress with the sauce just before serving."],"o":"Iced water is what makes them curl: without that step they stay straight and less crisp."},"611":{"n":"Fried courgette flowers","p":["Clean the flowers by removing the inner pistil.","Fill each one with a piece of mozzarella and half an anchovy.","Close by folding over the petal tips.","Make a batter with flour and cold sparkling water, just thick.","Dip the flowers in the batter and fry in hot oil until golden."],"o":"Cold sparkling water in the batter is what makes it light and crisp rather than heavy."},"612":{"n":"Tiella di Gaeta","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm water and a drizzle of oil into a soft dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Blanch the escarole, squeeze it and sauté with olives, capers and anchovies.","Roll out two discs of dough and sandwich the escarole between them.","Seal the edges well by pinching them.","Bake at 200 °C for 30 minutes, until golden."],"o":"Gaeta's black olives, softer and less salty than others, are the ingredient that gives the pie its name."},"613":{"n":"Fregnacce alla reatina","p":["Soak the dried mushrooms in lukewarm water.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, roll it out and cut irregular diamonds.","Brown the crumbled sausage, add the squeezed and chopped mushrooms, then the passata.","Cook the sauce for thirty minutes over a low heat.","Boil the fregnacce, drain al dente and dress with the sauce."],"o":"The irregular diamonds are deliberate: 'fregnaccia' means something done roughly, without precision."},"614":{"n":"Acquacotta viterbese","p":["Fry the onion, add the vegetables in pieces and the tomato, and let them take on flavour.","Cover with plenty of water and cook for thirty minutes, until the vegetables are soft.","Put slices of stale bread in deep plates.","Pour the boiling soup over the bread, just enough to soak it.","Crack an egg into each plate straight into the hot soup, let it set and finish with pecorino."],"o":"The egg cooks in the heat of the soup itself, poured over boiling: it isn't cooked separately."},"615":{"n":"Maritozzo con la panna","p":["Knead flour, yeast, eggs, sugar and lukewarm milk into a smooth dough.","Work in the soft butter a little at a time, then the raisins.","Leave to rise for two hours, shape into elongated diamond-shaped buns and leave to rise for another hour.","Bake at 190 °C for 15 minutes, until golden.","Split the maritozzi without separating them completely and fill with stiffly whipped cream."],"o":"The cut mustn't go all the way through: the maritozzo stays hinged, with cream overflowing at the sides."},"616":{"n":"Pangiallo romano","p":["Lightly toast the walnuts, almonds and hazelnuts in the oven, then chop roughly.","Warm the honey until runny.","Mix the nuts with candied peel, cinnamon and the flour, then pour in the hot honey and combine.","Shape into a flattened round loaf with oiled hands.","Bake at 160 °C for 40 minutes, then brush with a yolk diluted in water for the final glaze."],"o":"It's Rome's oldest Christmas cake, mentioned as far back as ancient Rome: the golden crust gives it its name."},"617":{"n":"Ricotta and sour cherry tart","p":["Knead flour, cold butter, sugar and egg into a smooth shortcrust. Rest for thirty minutes.","Sieve the ricotta with a little sugar into a soft cream.","Roll two-thirds of the pastry into a tin, prick it and pour in the ricotta.","Spread the sour cherries in syrup over the ricotta.","Cover with a lattice of the remaining pastry and bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes."],"o":"A Roman-Jewish tradition: the sour cherries, sharper than ordinary cherries, balance the sweetness of the ricotta."},"618":{"n":"Grattachecca","p":["Keep the block of ice well frozen until serving.","Scrape the surface of the block with a special ice grater or plane.","Collect the shaved ice straight into a tall glass.","Pour plenty of your chosen fruit syrup over it.","Finish with a few drops of fresh lemon and serve at once with a straw."],"o":"Shave it to order, never in advance: shaved ice melts in minutes."},"619":{"n":"Tortelloni","p":["Blanch the spinach, squeeze it very hard and chop.","Mix with ricotta, half the parmesan, nutmeg and a pinch of salt.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, rolling it thin.","Place heaps of filling spaced out, cover with another sheet and cut large squares, sealing the edges well.","Boil in salted water and dress with sage butter and the remaining parmesan."],"o":"They're bigger than tortellini and meatless: the generous size and ricotta filling set them apart at once."},"620":{"n":"Green lasagne alla bolognese","p":["Blanch and squeeze the spinach very hard, chop finely and knead with flour and eggs for the green pasta.","Make a classic ragù with soffritto, meat and passata, cooking for two hours over a low heat.","Make a béchamel with butter, flour and milk, cooking until thick.","Roll the pasta thin and cut rectangles, blanching them briefly.","Layer pasta, ragù, béchamel and parmesan, finishing with béchamel and cheese; bake at 190 °C for 30 minutes."],"o":"The green spinach pasta is specifically Bolognese: without it, it's just an ordinary ragù lasagne."},"621":{"n":"Gramigna with sausage","p":["Fry the chopped onion in oil.","Crumble the sausage and brown it over a high heat until well coloured.","Add the passata and cook for ten minutes.","Add the cream and let it take on flavour for another five minutes.","Boil the gramigna al dente and toss in the sauce."],"o":"Gramigna, a short curly pasta, is designed to trap bits of sausage in its curve."},"622":{"n":"Cappelletti","p":["Brown the pork in a little butter until well cooked, then chop it finely with the prosciutto and mortadella.","Mix with parmesan, an egg and nutmeg into a firm filling.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, roll it thin and cut small squares.","Put a pinch of filling in the centre of each square, fold into a triangle and wrap around a finger, sealing the points into a little hat.","Cook in boiling broth for a few minutes and serve in the broth, piping hot."],"o":"The little-hat shape comes from wrapping the triangle around your index finger: it takes practice to make them small and even."},"623":{"n":"Anolini in brodo","p":["Finely chop the leftover stracotto (beef braise) with its cooking juices.","Mix with breadcrumbs, parmesan and an egg into a firm filling.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, rolling it thin.","Cut out small discs, fill them and fold into half-moons, sealing the edges well.","Cook in boiling broth for a few minutes and serve piping hot in the broth."],"o":"The filling was created to use up Sunday's stracotto: a leftovers dish made noble."},"624":{"n":"Pisarei e fasò","p":["Scald the breadcrumbs with boiling water and let them cool slightly.","Knead with the flour into a firm dough, without eggs.","Roll into thin ropes and cut small pieces, pressing each with your thumb.","Fry the onion, add the beans and passata and cook for thirty minutes to a thick sauce.","Boil the pisarei in salted water, drain as soon as they float and dress with the bean sauce."],"o":"The egg-free dough, just flour and moistened breadcrumbs, is what makes these little Piacenza dumplings special."},"625":{"n":"Gnocco fritto","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm milk and lard into a smooth dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Roll out 5 mm thick.","Cut even diamonds or rectangles with a knife or pastry wheel.","Fry in very hot oil until puffed and golden, turning once.","Drain on paper and serve hot with cold cuts and cheeses."],"o":"Serve it freshly made, still puffed and slightly crisp: it loses everything as it cools."},"626":{"n":"Tigelle or crescentine","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm milk and lard into a smooth dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Roll out 1 cm thick and cut discs with a cutter.","Heat a tigelle press, or a heavy frying pan.","Cook the discs on both sides until puffed and lightly golden, about four minutes per side.","Split the hot tigelle and fill with cunza (lard pounded with rosemary and garlic) or cold cuts."],"o":"The name comes from the terracotta disc they were traditionally cooked on: today a special grooved press is used."},"627":{"n":"Piadina romagnola","p":["Knead flour, lard, lukewarm water and a pinch of salt into a smooth, elastic dough.","Leave to rest, covered, for thirty minutes.","Divide into balls and roll them into thin discs.","Cook each disc on a very hot griddle without fat, pricking it if it puffs up, a couple of minutes per side.","Fill while hot with squacquerone and rocket, or with cold cuts as you like."],"o":"The yeast-free dough and cooking on a scorching griddle set it apart from any other flatbread."},"628":{"n":"Cotoletta alla bolognese","p":["Coat the slices in flour, then beaten egg and finally breadcrumbs.","Fry in butter until golden on both sides.","Lay a slice of prosciutto and parmesan shavings on each cutlet.","Put in a hot oven for a few minutes, just to melt the cheese slightly.","Serve hot, perhaps with a spoonful of the pan juices."],"o":"Unlike the Milanese version, here the cutlet is enriched with prosciutto and parmesan after frying."},"629":{"n":"Zampone and cotechino with lentils","p":["Prick the zampone and cotechino with a cocktail stick and wrap them in a tied cloth.","Put them in cold water, bring slowly to the boil and continue over a low heat for at least two and a half hours.","Meanwhile fry onion, carrot and celery, add the lentils and cover with water.","Cook the lentils for forty minutes, until soft but still whole.","Slice the zampone and cotechino and serve with the hot lentils."],"o":"Never pierce them with a fork while cooking: the skin would split and all the fat would run out."},"630":{"n":"Guinea fowl in a salt crust","p":["Rub the inside of the guinea fowl with garlic and rosemary, stuffing the cavity.","Wrap the breast with slices of pancetta.","Make a paste of coarse salt with a little water, just enough to make it hold together.","Encase the guinea fowl completely in the salt, sealing it well with no gaps.","Roast at 200 °C for an hour and a half, then crack the salt crust at the table before serving."],"o":"The salt crust seals in all the meat's juices: break it only when serving, never before."},"631":{"n":"Fish brodetto","p":["Fry garlic in oil, add the cuttlefish in strips and brown.","Deglaze with a little vinegar, then add the chopped tomatoes.","Cook for fifteen minutes, then add the firmest fish first and the most delicate last.","Cover with a little water and cook over a low heat for twenty minutes, until all the fish is cooked.","Serve with toasted crostini of stale bread."],"o":"Every stretch of the Romagna coast has its own version: this base with vinegar is typical of the Comacchio brodetto."},"632":{"n":"Comacchio-style eel","p":["Clean the eel and cut it into 5 cm pieces.","Thread the pieces onto skewers alternating with bay leaves.","Grill over embers, turning often, until the fat starts to melt and the skin is crisp, about twenty minutes.","Heat the vinegar with rosemary almost to the boil.","Serve the eel hot, drizzled with the flavoured vinegar."],"o":"It's a very fatty fish: direct grilling lets the excess drip away as it cooks."},"633":{"n":"Friggione bolognese","p":["Slice the onions very thinly.","Stew them in plenty of oil over a very low heat, covered, for forty minutes, until translucent.","Add the chopped, deseeded tomatoes.","Remove the lid and cook for another forty minutes, stirring often, until the mixture becomes a thick cream.","Serve warm, as a side for grilled meats or spread on crostini."],"o":"The onions stew for a long time before the tomato goes in: slowness, not haste, melts them into a cream."},"634":{"n":"Certosino di Bologna","p":["Warm the honey slightly, without boiling.","Mix the flour with the spices, then pour in the warm honey and combine.","Add almonds, pine nuts, candied peel and roughly chopped chocolate.","Shape into a flattened round loaf, moist to the touch.","Bake at 160 °C for 40 minutes, then leave to rest wrapped for at least a day before cutting."],"o":"It improves greatly after resting a few days wrapped in a cloth: freshly made it's too dense to cut well."},"635":{"n":"Torta Barozzi","p":["Melt the chocolate with the butter over a bain-marie.","Grind the almonds finely, almost to a flour.","Whisk the eggs with the sugar until pale and foamy.","Gently fold in the melted chocolate, almonds, coffee and the little flour.","Pour into a shallow tin and bake at 160 °C for 35 minutes: it should stay slightly moist in the centre."],"o":"The exact recipe is a secret kept by the original Modena pastry shop: this version recreates its dark, moist taste."},"636":{"n":"Ciambella romagnola","p":["Whisk the eggs with the sugar until pale and foamy.","Add the lukewarm melted butter, the milk and the lemon zest.","Fold in the flour sifted with the baking powder, mixing gently.","Pour into a buttered and floured ring tin.","Bake at 180 °C for 35 minutes, until a skewer comes out dry."],"o":"It's dunked in wine or milk at breakfast: it's deliberately dry, not a moist cake to eat on its own."},"637":{"n":"Pampepato ferrarese","p":["Warm the honey slightly, without boiling.","Mix the flour with pepper and cinnamon, then pour in the warm honey and combine.","Add toasted almonds, candied peel and half the roughly chopped chocolate.","Shape into a round loaf and bake at 160 °C for 40 minutes.","Leave to cool, then cover completely with melted dark chocolate."],"o":"The black pepper in the dough isn't a mistake: it's what gives the name 'pampepato', peppered bread."},"638":{"n":"Umbricelli all'amatriciana","p":["Knead flour and water into a firm, elastic dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Cut strips and roll them by hand on the board into thick, irregular spaghetti.","Brown the guanciale strips until crisp, then set aside.","In the same pan add the crushed peeled tomatoes and chilli, cooking for fifteen minutes.","Boil the umbricelli, dress with the sauce, return the guanciale and finish with pecorino."],"o":"Umbricelli, thick egg-free handmade spaghetti, are the Umbrian touch that sets this apart from Roman bucatini."},"639":{"n":"Umbricelli alla spoletina","p":["Knead flour and water into a firm, elastic dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Cut strips and roll them by hand into thick spaghetti.","Fry plenty of garlic and chilli in oil.","Add the chopped tomatoes and cook for fifteen minutes over a lively heat.","Boil the umbricelli and toss them in the sauce with fresh parsley."],"o":"It's the oldest, plainest version, with no cheese or meat: just tomato, garlic and good oil."},"640":{"n":"Pappardelle with hare","p":["Marinate the hare in pieces in wine with chunks of vegetables for at least twelve hours.","Drain and brown the meat in oil, then return the vegetables from the marinade.","Deglaze with the strained wine, add the passata and cover with water.","Cook over a low heat for two and a half hours, until the meat falls off the bone, then shred it into the sauce.","Knead flour and eggs, roll thick sheets and cut wide pappardelle; boil and dress with the hare ragù."],"o":"Hare has a very pronounced gaminess: the long wine marinade is essential, not optional."},"641":{"n":"Ciriole alla ternana","p":["Knead flour and water into a firm dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Cut thin strips and roll them by hand, thinner than umbricelli.","Fry garlic and chilli, add the crushed peeled tomatoes and cook for twenty minutes.","Boil the ciriole in salted water.","Drain and toss them in the sauce with fresh parsley."],"o":"They're named after the eels of the Tiber for their thin, wavy shape: handmade pasta is never perfectly regular."},"642":{"n":"Rocciata di Assisi","p":["Knead flour, oil, water and a pinch of salt into an elastic dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Slice the apples thinly and mix with soaked raisins, chopped walnuts and figs, and sugar.","Roll the dough very thinly into a rectangle.","Spread the filling over the whole surface and roll up tightly into a log, then curve it into a horseshoe.","Bake at 180 °C for 35 minutes, until golden."],"o":"The curved horseshoe shape is the signature of this Umbrian strudel, which differs from the Trentino one only in shape and its more varied filling."},"643":{"n":"Crescia umbra","p":["Knead flour, lard, egg, baking powder and water into a smooth dough.","Leave to rest for twenty minutes.","Roll out thin 20 cm discs.","Cook on a very hot griddle without fat, pricking it if it puffs up, a couple of minutes per side.","Serve hot, filled with cold cuts and cheeses, like a piadina."],"o":"It's the Umbrian cousin of the Romagna piadina: here, though, the dough often includes egg and baking powder."},"644":{"n":"Umbrian panzanella","p":["Soak the stale bread in cold water, then squeeze it well and crumble it roughly.","Slice the onion, cucumber and tomatoes.","Mix everything in a large bowl.","Dress with oil, vinegar, salt and torn basil.","Chill for half an hour before serving so the flavours come together."],"o":"Squeeze the bread well but not too much: it should stay moist, neither dry like bruschetta nor sodden."},"645":{"n":"Stewed Trasimeno beans","p":["Fry chopped onion and celery in oil.","Add the chopped tomatoes and sage, cooking for ten minutes.","Add the fagiolina, soaked first if dried, with water to cover.","Cook over a low heat for forty minutes, until soft but still whole.","Serve hot with a drizzle of raw oil, as a side or a light one-dish meal."],"o":"It's a tiny, ancient pulse, almost extinct, grown only around Lake Trasimeno: it cooks faster than ordinary beans."},"646":{"n":"Palomba alla ghiotta","p":["Brown the whole birds in oil with sage until well coloured on all sides.","Deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate.","Chop the giblets together with anchovies and olives into a paste.","Add the paste to the pan with a little water, cover and cook for forty minutes.","Serve on toasted crostini of stale bread, soaked in the cooking juices."],"o":"The paste of giblets, anchovies and olives is what makes it 'ghiotta': without it, it would be a simple roast."},"647":{"n":"Umbrian Easter pie","p":["Knead flour, oil, water and salt into a smooth dough. Divide in two and rest for thirty minutes.","Blanch the greens, squeeze and chop them, then mix with ricotta, parmesan and two eggs.","Roll out one disc of dough into a tin, pour in the filling and level it.","Make four small hollows and crack the remaining eggs into them whole.","Cover with the second disc, seal the edges and bake at 190 °C for 40 minutes."],"o":"The whole eggs hidden inside, which show as hard-boiled slices when you cut it, are the special feature of this Easter pie."},"648":{"n":"Umbrian porchetta","p":["Lay the boned meat skin side down.","Spread salt, chopped garlic, pepper, rosemary and plenty of wild fennel over the inner surface.","Roll up tightly with the skin wrapping the outside and tie firmly with string.","Roast at 160 °C for three hours.","Raise to 220 °C for the last thirty minutes to crackle the skin well."],"o":"Plenty of wild fennel, more than in the Lazio version, is the distinctive signature of Umbrian porchetta."},"649":{"n":"Truffle frittata","p":["Beat the eggs with a pinch of salt and the parmesan.","Grate half the truffle straight into the beaten egg.","Melt the butter in a non-stick pan and pour in the mixture.","Cook over a low heat, lifting the edges, until set but still soft in the centre.","Serve at once with shavings of the remaining truffle on top."],"o":"Grate the truffle at the last moment: heat and time quickly disperse its aroma."},"650":{"n":"Tegamaccio del Trasimeno","p":["Clean the lake fish, cutting the larger ones into steaks.","Fry onion and garlic in oil, add the chopped tomatoes and cook for ten minutes.","Deglaze with white wine.","Lay the fish in the sauce, cover with a little water and cook over a low heat for twenty-five minutes.","Serve with toasted crostini of stale bread."],"o":"It's Lake Trasimeno's cacciucco: it uses freshwater fish, tench, pike or perch, instead of sea fish."},"651":{"n":"Tozzetti","p":["Whisk the eggs with the sugar until pale.","Fold in the flour and baking powder, then the whole toasted hazelnuts.","Shape two long logs on baking paper.","Bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes, until golden and firm to the touch.","Slice diagonally while still hot and return to the oven for five minutes per side to crisp."],"o":"They're the Umbrian cousins of Tuscan cantucci, with hazelnuts instead of almonds: dunked in vin santo the same way."},"652":{"n":"Torciglione","p":["Grind the almonds very finely, almost to a flour.","Knead with the sugar, lemon zest and orange-blossom water into a firm paste.","Shape a long rope and coil it in a spiral, giving it the shape of a snake.","Model a head at one end, marking eyes and mouth, and cover with flaked almonds for scales.","Bake at 150 °C for 20 minutes, just to dry and lightly colour the surface."],"o":"The snake shape is tied to an old local legend: model the head by hand before baking, while the paste is still soft."},"653":{"n":"Ciaramicola","p":["Whisk the eggs with the sugar, then add lukewarm melted butter and alchermes until the mixture turns red.","Fold in the flour sifted with the baking powder.","Shape into a ring in a tin and bake at 180 °C for 35 minutes.","Whisk the egg whites with a little sugar into a firm meringue.","Ice the cooled ring with the meringue and decorate with coloured sprinkles."],"o":"The bright red of the cake comes entirely from the alchermes: it's Perugia's symbolic Easter cake."},"654":{"n":"Cotoletta alla milanese","p":["Pound the cutlets lightly without breaking them, keeping the bone on.","Dip them in beaten egg and then breadcrumbs, pressing well so it sticks.","Melt plenty of clarified butter in a pan: it should come almost to the top of the meat.","Fry over a medium heat for three minutes per side, until the crust is golden and crisp.","Drain on paper and serve at once, with a lemon wedge on the side."],"o":"It's fried in clarified butter, never oil: that's what gives the golden colour and characteristic flavour."},"655":{"n":"Busecca","p":["Cut the tripe into strips.","Fry chopped carrot, celery and onion in oil.","Add the tripe and passata, covering with plenty of water.","Cook over a low heat for an hour and a half, then add the cooked beans.","Cook for another twenty minutes and serve hot with grated parmesan."],"o":"It's Milanese tripe with beans: soupier and heartier than the Florentine version."},"656":{"n":"Nervetti salad","p":["Cut the already boiled nervetti (veal cartilage) into thin strips.","Slice the red onion very thinly.","Mix nervetti, onion and beans in a bowl.","Dress with oil, vinegar, salt and chopped parsley.","Chill for at least an hour before serving so it takes on more flavour."],"o":"A humble dish from Milan's taverns: the long cold marinade is what makes the connective tissue tender."},"657":{"n":"Rostin negàa","p":["Tie the veal chops with kitchen string to keep their shape while cooking.","Brown them in butter with rosemary until well coloured on both sides.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Cover with a lid and cook over a very low heat for forty minutes, adding stock a little at a time.","The sauce should stay thick and glossy: if it dries out too much, add more stock."],"o":"The name means 'drowned roast': the meat cooks covered, almost braised, rather than roasted over a high heat."},"658":{"n":"Valtellina pizzoccheri","p":["Boil the pizzoccheri, potato chunks and savoy cabbage strips together in plenty of salted water.","Drain everything together when the pasta is al dente.","Layer pasta and vegetables in an ovenproof dish with slices of casera cheese.","Melt the butter with garlic and sage until lightly coloured.","Pour the sizzling butter over everything and serve at once, mixing lightly at the table."],"o":"Pasta and vegetables are all boiled together in the same water: that's how the dish gets its bound, cohesive texture."},"659":{"n":"Sciatt","p":["Mix the two flours with cold sparkling water and the grappa into a thick batter.","Leave to rest for twenty minutes.","Cut the casera into small cubes and drop them into the batter.","Scoop out one cube at a time with a spoon, coated in batter, and drop into the hot oil.","Fry until golden and crisp outside, melting inside."],"o":"They're little bite-sized fritters with a molten cheese heart: serve them hot, straight after frying."},"660":{"n":"Chisciöi","p":["Cut the cheese into 1 cm thick slices.","Make a soft batter with buckwheat flour and milk.","Dip each slice of cheese in the batter, coating it completely.","Fry in very hot oil until the batter is golden and crisp.","Serve hot, just as the cheese inside starts to run."],"o":"It's almost a batter fritter with a cheese heart: the buckwheat crust stays rustic and slightly bitter."},"661":{"n":"Manfrigole","p":["Whisk the two flours with eggs and milk into a smooth, runny batter, as for crêpes.","Leave to rest for thirty minutes.","Cook thin crêpes in a buttered pan, one or two minutes per side.","Fill each crêpe with flakes of casera and fold into a triangle.","Return to the pan for a minute per side, just enough to melt the cheese."],"o":"They're the Valtellina crêpes, cousins of pizzoccheri in ingredients but completely different in texture."},"662":{"n":"Bergamo-style casoncelli","p":["Chop the amaretti and pear and mix with soaked raisins and breadcrumbs into a firm filling.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, rolling it thin.","Cut out discs, fill and fold into half-moons, then fold the points round to form a little parcel.","Boil in salted water.","Dress with sage butter."],"o":"The sweet-and-sour filling with amaretti and pear is what makes these casoncelli Bergamasque, very different from the savoury Brescia ones."},"663":{"n":"Casoncelli bresciani","p":["Brown the minced meat, then chop it together with the salami.","Mix with parmesan and an egg into a firm filling.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, roll it thin and cut out discs.","Fill, fold into half-moons and fold the points into a parcel.","Boil and dress with melted butter, crispy pancetta and sage."],"o":"The savoury meat and salami filling is the Brescia signature, the opposite of the sweet amaretti filling of the Bergamo version."},"664":{"n":"Marubini in brodo","p":["Finely chop the stracotto and salami together.","Mix with parmesan and an egg into a firm filling.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, roll it thin and cut small discs.","Fill and fold into half-moons, sealing the edges well.","Cook in boiling broth for a few minutes and serve hot in the broth."],"o":"The broth of three meats, beef, hen and capon, is a Cremona tradition: it's what gives the depth of flavour."},"665":{"n":"Mantuan pumpkin tortelli","p":["Bake the pumpkin until soft, then push it through a sieve to dry it well.","Mix the purée with crumbled amaretti, chopped mostarda and parmesan.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, rolling it thin.","Fill and close into squares or half-moons, sealing the edges well.","Boil and dress with melted butter and more parmesan."],"o":"The sweet-bitter balance of pumpkin, amaretti and mostarda is what makes this tortello Mantuan rather than ordinary."},"666":{"n":"Risotto alla pilota","p":["Crumble the sausage and brown it in a pan until well cooked.","Bring salted water equal to twice the volume of the rice to the boil.","Pour in the rice, stir once only and turn off the heat immediately.","Cover and leave to rest for ten minutes without ever lifting the lid.","Stir in the sausage, butter and parmesan before serving."],"o":"It isn't stirred like a normal risotto: it cooks off the heat under a lid, the typical Mantuan method."},"667":{"n":"Mantuan mostarda","p":["Peel the quinces and cut them into thick slices.","Cook them with the sugar over a low heat until they release their liquid, about forty minutes.","Drain the fruit, keeping the syrup, and reduce the syrup until thick.","Add the mustard powder to the hot syrup, stirring well.","Return the fruit to the syrup, let it cool and pot it."],"o":"Dissolve the mustard in the already hot syrup, never boil it for long: it would lose its characteristic pungency."},"668":{"n":"Missoltini with polenta","p":["Make the polenta by pouring the cornmeal in a stream into boiling salted water, stirring for forty minutes.","Meanwhile heat a non-stick pan with no fat.","Put the missoltini, already dried and salted, on the hot pan for a few minutes per side, just to warm them.","Drizzle with a little oil and a splash of vinegar.","Serve hot with the freshly poured polenta."],"o":"Missoltini are shad from Lake Como, sun-dried and pressed: they only need warming, never long cooking."},"669":{"n":"Polenta e osei","p":["Wrap each piece of meat with a slice of pancetta and a sage leaf, securing with a cocktail stick.","Brown in butter until well coloured on all sides.","Cover and cook over a low heat for twenty minutes, adding a little water if needed.","Make the polenta by pouring the cornmeal in a stream into boiling salted water, stirring for forty minutes.","Serve the hot rolls on the freshly poured soft polenta."],"o":"The modern version replaces wild songbirds with meat rolls, keeping the same spirit of the dish."},"670":{"n":"Panettone","p":["Refresh the sourdough starter and make a first dough with some of the flour, sugar and yolks. Leave to rise overnight.","The next day knead in the remaining ingredients, adding the soft butter a little at a time.","Leave the finished dough to rise until tripled, several hours.","Add the raisins and candied peel, shape into a ball and put it in the paper mould for a final rise of a few hours.","Cut a cross on top, bake at 170 °C for 50 minutes, then hang upside down on skewers until completely cool."],"o":"Hanging it upside down after baking is essential: otherwise the panettone collapses on itself as it cools."},"671":{"n":"Torta sbrisolona","p":["Roughly chop the almonds, keeping a few whole for the top.","Mix the two flours with the sugar and chopped almonds.","Rub in the cold butter in pieces with your fingertips, without kneading, until you have coarse crumbs.","Add the yolks, still crumbling, without ever forming a compact dough.","Tip the crumbs into a tin without pressing and bake at 170 °C for 35 minutes."],"o":"It must never be kneaded like shortcrust: it should stay crumbly, and indeed it's broken by hand, not sliced."},"672":{"n":"Torta del paradiso","p":["Beat the soft butter with the sugar until pale and fluffy.","Add the eggs one at a time, waiting until each is absorbed.","Fold in the sifted flour, starch, baking powder and lemon zest, mixing gently.","Pour into a buttered and floured tin.","Bake at 170 °C for 40 minutes, until a skewer comes out dry."],"o":"Cornflour in place of some of the flour is what gives it that crumbly, very light texture."},"673":{"n":"Tortionata lodigiana","p":["Chop the almonds together with the amaretti, not too finely.","Beat the soft butter with the sugar, then add the eggs one at a time.","Fold in the flour and the almond and amaretti mixture.","Pour into a buttered and floured tin.","Bake at 170 °C for 40 minutes, until the top is golden."],"o":"Crumbled amaretti in the mixture are the Lodi speciality compared with an ordinary almond cake."},"674":{"n":"Barbajada","p":["Melt the chocolate pieces in a small pan with a little milk over a very low heat.","Add the rest of the milk and the sugar, stirring until smooth.","Add the freshly made strong coffee.","Heat everything together without boiling, stirring with a whisk.","Whisk briefly to create some froth and serve hot in a cup."],"o":"It's the ancestor of the Milanese cappuccino, born in 19th-century cafés: chocolate, coffee and milk in almost equal parts."},"675":{"n":"Trofie with pesto","p":["Knead flour and water into a firm dough, without eggs.","Take small pieces and roll them between your hands to form thin, twisted trofie.","Pound basil, garlic and pine nuts in a mortar with a pinch of salt, then add the cheeses and loosen with oil.","Boil the diced potatoes, green beans and trofie together in the same water.","Drain everything together and dress with the pesto, loosened with a little cooking water."],"o":"Potatoes and green beans boiled with the pasta in the same pot are an integral part of the dish, not a separate side."},"676":{"n":"Trenette al pesto","p":["Pound basil, garlic and pine nuts in a mortar with a pinch of salt.","Add the parmesan and loosen with oil in a thin stream into a cream.","Boil the diced potatoes, green beans and trenette together in the same salted water.","Drain everything, keeping a ladle of cooking water.","Dress with the pesto, loosening with the water you kept back."],"o":"It's the long dried-pasta version of the Ligurian classic: same principle as trofie, different shape."},"677":{"n":"Testaroli al pesto","p":["Whisk flour, water and a pinch of salt into a runny, lump-free batter.","Heat a testo, or a heavy pan, and pour in the batter in a thin layer.","Cook like a large, thick crêpe, on one side only, covered, until the top is dry.","Cut the cooked disc into diamonds and boil them briefly in salted water.","Drain and dress with mortar-pounded pesto."],"o":"They're neither fried nor flipped: a testarolo cooks on one side only, like a thick flatbread."},"678":{"n":"Mandilli de saea","p":["Knead flour and eggs for a very thin, almost transparent sheet.","Cut the sheet into squares the size of a handkerchief.","Pound basil, garlic and pine nuts in a mortar, add the parmesan and loosen with oil.","Boil the 'handkerchiefs' for a few seconds, just until they float.","Drain them gently and dress with the pesto."],"o":"The name means silk handkerchiefs: the pasta must be so thin it really looks like a light fabric."},"679":{"n":"Corzetti","p":["Knead flour, water and white wine into a firm, elastic dough.","Roll it thin and cut out discs, decorating them with a carved wooden stamp, the traditional mould.","Boil the corzetti in salted water for a few minutes.","Melt the butter with marjoram and pine nuts until lightly coloured.","Drain the pasta and dress with the fragrant butter and parmesan."],"o":"The wooden stamp that decorates each disc with a carved pattern is what makes these Ligurian corzetti unique."},"680":{"n":"Genoese minestrone","p":["Cut all the vegetables into small pieces and put them in a pot with the beans and plenty of water.","Cook over a low heat for an hour, until the vegetables are soft.","Add the short pasta and cook until al dente.","Pound basil, pine nuts and parmesan with oil in a mortar for a light pesto.","Stir a spoonful of pesto into the hot soup when serving."],"o":"Stir the pesto in only on the plate, never cook it in the pot: prolonged heat would darken its colour and flavour."},"681":{"n":"Mesciua","p":["Soak the chickpeas, beans and farro separately overnight, changing the water.","Boil each pulse separately, in different water, until soft.","Boil the farro separately until tender.","Combine the three drained ingredients in a tureen.","Dress with plenty of raw extra virgin olive oil and ground black pepper."],"o":"It was born at the docks of La Spezia, from pulses spilled from sacks: separate cooking is essential because of the different timings."},"682":{"n":"Focaccia genovese","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm water and oil into a soft dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Tip it into an oiled tin without working it too much, spreading it with your fingers.","Leave to rest for thirty minutes, then spread it again until it covers the whole tin.","Make the characteristic dimples with your fingers, pressing right down.","Pour a brine of water, oil and coarse salt into the dimples and bake at 220 °C for 20 minutes."],"o":"The brine poured into the dimples before baking creates those little pools of golden oil on top."},"683":{"n":"Focaccia di Recco","p":["Knead flour, water, oil and a pinch of salt into a smooth, elastic dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Divide in two and roll each part out very thin, almost transparent.","Dot the stracchino in irregular spoonfuls over one sheet.","Cover with the second sheet, sealing the edges and tearing holes here and there to let the steam out.","Bake at 250 °C, as hot as possible, for ten minutes, until golden and blistered."],"o":"Yeast-free, unlike Genoese focaccia: the thinness of the dough and the very high heat are everything."},"684":{"n":"Sardenaira","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm water and oil into a soft dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Spread it in the tin with your fingers, without a rolling pin.","Cover with crushed peeled tomatoes, rinsed anchovies and whole Taggiasca olives.","Sprinkle with oregano and a drizzle of oil.","Bake at 220 °C for 25 minutes."],"o":"It's the Ligurian pizza of Sanremo, related to the French pissaladière: Taggiasca olives are its defining ingredient."},"685":{"n":"Brandacujùn","p":["Boil the stockfish and potatoes together in unsalted water until soft.","Drain and break up the stockfish, removing skin and bones.","Roughly crush the potatoes, not to a smooth mash.","Combine stockfish, potatoes, chopped garlic and parsley in a large bowl.","Beat in the oil in a thin stream, shaking the covered bowl vigorously, until creamy."],"o":"The name means 'shake it well': it's emulsified by shaking the closed bowl, not stirring with a spoon."},"686":{"n":"Buridda","p":["Soak the dried mushrooms in lukewarm water.","Fry garlic in oil, then add the squeezed, chopped mushrooms and pine nuts.","Deglaze with white wine, then add the crushed peeled tomatoes and cook for fifteen minutes.","Lay the fish in the sauce and cover with a little water.","Cook over a low heat for twenty-five minutes, until the fish is cooked and the sauce has reduced."],"o":"Dried mushrooms and pine nuts in a fish sauce are a purely Ligurian touch, unusual elsewhere."},"687":{"n":"Stoccafisso accomodato","p":["Soak the dried mushrooms in lukewarm water.","Fry garlic in oil, add the squeezed mushrooms, olives and pine nuts.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and cook for ten minutes.","Lay the stockfish pieces in the sauce and cover with large chunks of potato.","Add water just to cover, put the lid on and cook over a low heat for an hour, shaking the pot instead of stirring."],"o":"A close relative of Messina stockfish, but with Taggiasca olives, pine nuts and dried mushrooms instead of celery."},"688":{"n":"Cappon magro","p":["Boil all the vegetables separately, each to the right point, and cut them into pieces.","Moisten the ship's biscuits with water and vinegar and lay them as a base on the serving dish.","Build alternating layers of vegetables, poached fish and prawns, keeping a dome shape.","Make a green sauce by pounding anchovies, capers, parsley and a hard-boiled yolk with oil.","Cover the dome with the green sauce and decorate with slices of hard-boiled egg, whole prawns and capers."],"o":"A showpiece for a grand occasion, built in layers like a monument: it takes time and precision to assemble."},"689":{"n":"Cima alla genovese","p":["Make a filling by mixing minced meat, sweetbreads, peas, parmesan, pine nuts and marjoram with a raw egg.","Beat the remaining eggs and stir them into the mixture.","Fill the veal pocket with the stuffing, without overdoing it, and sew up the opening with kitchen string.","Prick the pocket in several places with a cocktail stick and simmer in broth for an hour and a half.","Leave to cool completely under a weight, then slice and serve cold."],"o":"It must cool under a weight before cutting: that's what firms up the filling and gives clean slices."},"690":{"n":"Salt cod fritters","p":["Poach the salt cod and break it up roughly, removing skin and bones.","Make a batter with flour, yeast, lukewarm water and chopped parsley.","Leave the batter to rest for thirty minutes.","Add the flaked cod to the batter.","Fry spoonfuls in hot oil until golden and puffed."],"o":"Leave the cod in irregular pieces, not finely chopped: biting into chunks is part of the pleasure."},"691":{"n":"Canestrelli","p":["Push the hard-boiled yolks through a sieve to get a fine, sandy powder.","Knead with flour, cold butter in pieces, icing sugar and wine, working it as little as possible.","Leave to rest for thirty minutes in the fridge.","Roll out 1 cm thick and cut with a flower cutter with a hole in the middle.","Bake at 160 °C for 15 minutes without letting them colour too much, then dust with icing sugar once cold."],"o":"Sieved hard-boiled yolks instead of raw eggs are what make these biscuits so crumbly and sandy."},"692":{"n":"Pandolce genovese","p":["Knead flour, yeast, soft butter, sugar and lukewarm water into a smooth dough.","Leave to rise for two hours.","Work in raisins, pine nuts, candied peel and fennel seeds, spreading them well.","Shape into a flattened round loaf and leave to rise for another hour.","Cut a triangle into the top and bake at 170 °C for 45 minutes."],"o":"Fennel seeds in the dough are a traditional touch often forgotten in modern versions."},"693":{"n":"Sacripantina","p":["Make a sponge by whisking the eggs with the sugar and folding in the flour. Bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes.","Cut the sponge horizontally into three discs.","Make a buttercream by beating soft butter with sugar and melted chocolate.","Moisten each disc with a rum and coffee syrup.","Fill the layers with the buttercream and cover the whole cake, smoothing the surface well."],"o":"Buttercream, not whipped cream, sets it apart from other layer cakes: it gives body and lets it stay out of the fridge longer."},"694":{"n":"Baci di Alassio","p":["Grind the toasted hazelnuts finely, almost to a flour.","Mix with sugar and unsweetened cocoa.","Whisk the egg whites to soft peaks and fold them gently into the mixture.","Shape small balls and place them on baking paper.","Bake at 160 °C for 15 minutes, then let cool and join two biscuits with melted chocolate, like a kiss."],"o":"Cocoa in the mixture makes them darker and more intense than Piedmontese baci di dama, which are pale and hazelnut only."},"695":{"n":"Bigoli with duck","p":["Brown the pieces of duck in oil until well coloured, then set aside.","In the same pan fry chopped onion, carrot and celery.","Return the duck, deglaze with white wine and cover with water.","Cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meat falls off the bone, then bone it and shred it into the sauce.","Boil the bigoli, drain al dente and dress with the duck sauce and parmesan."],"o":"Bigoli, a rough pasta extruded through a press, hold the rich duck sauce far better than smooth pasta."},"696":{"n":"Amarone risotto","p":["Soften the chopped onion in half the butter without colouring.","Toast the rice, then add a third of the Amarone and let it absorb.","Add the hot stock a ladle at a time, adding the rest of the wine halfway through.","Cook for sixteen minutes, until the rice is creamy and coloured deep purple.","Off the heat, beat in cold butter and parmesan."],"o":"Amarone, a robust, strong wine, should be used generously: its intensity defines the whole dish."},"697":{"n":"Pasta e fasioi","p":["Boil the beans with rosemary until soft, about an hour if using soaked dried beans.","Blend half the beans with a little of their cooking water to thicken.","Fry pancetta and onion, then add the remaining whole beans and the blended cream.","Bring to the boil and cook the pasta straight in the bean broth.","Serve thick, with a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"Blending only half the beans gives the right creaminess while keeping some whole beans for texture."},"698":{"n":"Gnocchi with fioreta","p":["Boil the potatoes in their skins, peel them while hot and pass them through a ricer.","Knead with flour and egg into a soft dough.","Roll into ropes, cut the gnocchi and ridge them with a fork.","Boil in salted water, draining as soon as they float.","Dress with melted butter and plenty of grated fioreta on top."],"o":"Fioreta is a strongly flavoured smoked ricotta from Belluno: it replaces parmesan in mountain dishes."},"699":{"n":"Polenta e schie","p":["Make the polenta by pouring the cornmeal in a stream into boiling salted water, stirring for forty minutes.","Boil the schie for a few minutes, then peel them.","Fry garlic in oil, add the peeled schie and sauté briefly.","Sprinkle with chopped parsley.","Serve the hot schie on top of slices of freshly poured soft polenta."],"o":"Schie, tiny grey shrimp from the Venetian lagoon, are peeled by hand after a very brief cooking."},"700":{"n":"Vicenza-style liver","p":["Slice the onions very thinly and stew them in butter over a very low heat until sweet and translucent, about thirty minutes.","Cut the liver into thin strips.","Turn up the heat, add the liver to the onions and brown quickly, a couple of minutes.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Serve immediately: the liver should stay pink inside."],"o":"The onions stew long first, the liver cooks very briefly after: reversing the timings ruins both."},"701":{"n":"Sepe col nero","p":["Clean the cuttlefish, setting aside the ink sacs.","Fry the chopped onion in oil, add the cuttlefish in strips and brown.","Deglaze with white wine and add a little passata.","Dissolve the ink in a little water and pour it into the pan.","Cook over a low heat for twenty minutes, until the sauce is dark and thick, and serve with polenta."],"o":"Dissolve the ink separately in water before adding it: squeezed straight from the sac it may not spread evenly."},"702":{"n":"Fried moeche","p":["Leave the live moeche in a bowl of beaten egg for a couple of hours so they absorb a little.","Drain them and coat in flour, shaking off the excess.","Fry in very hot oil until golden and crisp, a few minutes.","Drain on kitchen paper.","Serve hot: they're eaten whole, soft shell and all."],"o":"Moeche are lagoon crabs caught while moulting, when the shell is still soft: they're fried and eaten whole."},"703":{"n":"Folpetti alla veneziana","p":["Boil the baby octopus in boiling water for twenty-five minutes, until tender.","Drain and let them cool slightly.","Dress with oil, and chopped garlic and parsley.","Add lemon juice and a pinch of salt.","Serve warm or cold, as a seafood starter."],"o":"Boil them whole, without cutting them first: they're only cut after cooking, once tender."},"704":{"n":"Pastissada de caval","p":["Marinate the whole piece of meat in the wine with chunks of vegetables and spices for at least twelve hours.","Drain and brown the meat in oil until well coloured.","Return the vegetables from the marinade and deglaze with the strained wine.","Cover and cook over a very low heat for three hours, until the meat is very tender.","Slice and serve with its thick sauce, traditionally with polenta."],"o":"It's Verona's typical stew, linked to the legend of a battle against the Ostrogoths: the long spiced marinade is its signature."},"705":{"n":"Bacalà mantecato (Venetian)","p":["Simmer the stockfish in unsalted water for twenty minutes, until soft.","Drain it and remove skin and bones while still hot.","Work it with a fork or electric whisk, adding oil slowly in a thin stream.","Alternate oil with a little milk, whipping to a light, white cream.","Season with chopped garlic and parsley and serve on polenta crostini."],"o":"Despite the name, in the Veneto stockfish is used, not salted cod: whisking in the oil slowly is what creates the cream."},"706":{"n":"Sopressa veneta","p":["Slice the sopressa not too thinly, thicker than an ordinary salami.","If just out of the fridge, leave it a few minutes at room temperature before serving.","Arrange it on a board with the bread.","Serve with a good local red wine.","Serve as a starter, with nothing added that would mask the flavour."],"o":"Cut it thicker than ordinary salami: the soft, rich texture is only appreciated in generous slices."},"707":{"n":"Grilled Treviso radicchio","p":["Halve the heads of radicchio lengthways.","Season with oil, salt and pepper.","Heat the grill or griddle well.","Cook the radicchio for two or three minutes per side, until wilted and slightly charred at the edges.","Serve hot with a drizzle of balsamic vinegar."],"o":"The bitterness of radicchio softens on the grill: don't cook it too long or it loses its crunch in the middle."},"708":{"n":"Venetian fritters","p":["Dissolve the yeast in lukewarm milk with a spoonful of sugar.","Mix flour, eggs, the yeasted milk and sugar into a thick batter.","Leave to rise for two hours, until doubled.","Fold in soaked raisins and pine nuts.","Fry spoonfuls in hot oil until golden and puffed, then roll in granulated sugar."],"o":"It's Venice's oldest carnival sweet, already in 16th-century recipe books: the batter must stay soft."},"709":{"n":"Zaleti","p":["Soak the raisins in warm water for ten minutes, then squeeze them.","Knead the two flours with soft butter, sugar and egg.","Add the raisins, spreading them well.","Shape small elongated ovals by hand.","Bake at 180 °C for 18 minutes, until golden."],"o":"The name comes from the yellow ('zalo') of the cornmeal: the simplest Venetian biscuits, for dunking in sweet wine."},"710":{"n":"Baicoli","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm milk, butter and sugar into a smooth dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Shape narrow logs and leave to rise for another hour.","Bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes, until golden.","Leave to cool completely, even for a whole day.","Slice very thinly on the diagonal and return to the oven at 150 °C for 15 minutes, until dry and crisp."],"o":"The double baking, a day apart, is what makes them so thin and crisp: the Venetian dunking biscuit par excellence."},"711":{"n":"Pandoro","p":["Refresh the sourdough starter and make a first dough with some of the flour, sugar and yolks. Leave to rise overnight.","The next day knead in the remaining ingredients, adding the soft butter a little at a time until the dough is glossy and elastic.","Leave to rise until tripled, several hours.","Shape into a ball and put it in the star-shaped mould for a long final rise.","Bake at 170 °C for 40 minutes, then cool upside down and dust with icing sugar."],"o":"The eight-pointed star mould creates the typical triangular sections when sliced: without it, it isn't a real pandoro."},"712":{"n":"Olive ascolane","p":["Pit the olives by cutting in a spiral with a special small knife, keeping them in one piece.","Brown the minced meat with chopped celery, carrot and onion, then chop finely with parmesan and an egg.","Fill each olive with a little stuffing, closing it back around the filling.","Coat in flour, the remaining beaten egg and breadcrumbs.","Fry in hot oil until golden and crisp."],"o":"Spiral pitting, without cutting the olive in two, is the technique that lets you close it back round the filling."},"713":{"n":"Maccheroncini di Campofilone","p":["Fry the chopped onion in oil.","Add the minced meat and brown until well coloured.","Add the passata and cook for forty minutes over a low heat.","Boil the very thin maccheroncini for just a few seconds, as soon as they float.","Dress with the ragù and parmesan."],"o":"They're an ultra-thin egg pasta, almost like hair: they cook in very little time, so watch them closely."},"714":{"n":"Frascarelli","p":["Bring salted water to the boil in a wide pan.","Sprinkle in the flour with one hand, stirring constantly with a fork or the fingers of the other.","Keep going until lots of small irregular lumps form, like a rustic couscous.","Cook the lumps in the boiling water for five minutes, then drain.","Fry garlic, add the passata and cook for ten minutes; dress the frascarelli with the sauce and pecorino."],"o":"The technique of sprinkling flour while stirring with your fingers to form lumps is what makes this very humble dish unique."},"715":{"n":"Tagliatelle with Marche-style ragù","p":["Fry chopped onion, carrot and celery.","Add the minced meat and chopped chicken livers, browning well.","Add the passata and cook over a low heat for an hour and a half.","Knead flour and eggs for the pasta, roll it out and cut wide tagliatelle.","Boil the pasta, drain and dress with the ragù."],"o":"Chicken livers chopped in with the meat are what set Marche ragù apart from Bolognese."},"716":{"n":"Crescia sfogliata di Urbino","p":["Knead flour, eggs, white wine and a little lard into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Roll the dough very thinly and brush with soft lard.","Roll it up tightly into a log, then coil the log into a snail.","Leave to rest for twenty minutes, then roll out into a disc again, keeping the layers.","Cook on a very hot griddle without fat for a few minutes per side, until golden and blistered."],"o":"The layering made by rolling and re-flattening the dough is what sets it apart from plain crescia."},"717":{"n":"Crescia al formaggio","p":["Dissolve the yeast in a little lukewarm water.","Knead flour, eggs, grated cheeses, lard and yeast into a soft, sticky dough.","Leave to rise for two hours in a tall mould, like a panettone mould.","The mixture should come almost to the rim of the mould.","Bake at 180 °C for 45 minutes, until well risen and golden."],"o":"It's the savoury Easter bread of the Marche: eaten at breakfast with cured meats on Easter morning."},"718":{"n":"Piconi ascolani","p":["Knead flour, two eggs, lard and a little water into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Mix grated pecorino with sugar, an egg and lemon zest for the filling.","Roll out the dough and cut discs, filling them with the mixture.","Fold into half-moons, sealing the edges well.","Bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes, until golden."],"o":"The sweet pecorino filling, unusual outside the Marche, is what makes these piconi so distinctive."},"719":{"n":"Brodetto alla sambenedettese","p":["Fry garlic and pepper strips in oil.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and cook for fifteen minutes.","Deglaze with vinegar, the distinctive element of this version.","Lay in the firmest fish first and the most delicate last, covering with a little water.","Cook over a low heat for twenty minutes and serve with crostini of stale bread."],"o":"Vinegar in the sauce and green pepper are the two features that set this brodetto apart from Ancona's or Fano's."},"720":{"n":"Pollo in potacchio","p":["Brown the chicken pieces in oil with unpeeled garlic, rosemary and chilli.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes.","Cover and cook over a low heat for forty minutes, until the chicken is tender and the sauce has reduced.","Serve hot with its sauce."],"o":"'Potacchio' is the aromatic base of garlic, rosemary and tomato also used for rabbit and other white meats."},"721":{"n":"Ciauscolo","p":["Leave the ciauscolo at room temperature for a few minutes: it should be spreadable.","Lightly toast the bread, if you like.","Cut the sausage into pieces or leave it whole to spread directly.","Spread the ciauscolo on the bread with a knife, like a pâté.","Serve as a starter, perhaps with a drizzle of honey."],"o":"It's a very soft salami for spreading, not slicing: its creamy texture sets it apart from other cured sausages."},"722":{"n":"Marche porchetta","p":["Lay the boned meat skin side down.","Spread salt, chopped garlic, pepper, rosemary and plenty of wild fennel over the inner surface.","Roll up tightly with the skin wrapping the outside and tie firmly with string.","Roast at 160 °C for three hours.","Raise to 220 °C for the last thirty minutes to crackle the skin well."],"o":"Here too, as in Lazio, wild fennel is central: the Marche difference lies more in the cut of meat used."},"723":{"n":"Lumache in porchetta","p":["Brown the snails in oil with garlic, diced pancetta and chopped wild fennel.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Add the passata and cover with water.","Cook over a low heat for two and a half hours, until the snails are very tender.","Serve hot with their aromatic sauce."],"o":"Wild fennel and pancetta recreate the flavour of porchetta, hence the name, without using any pork meat."},"724":{"n":"Calcioni","p":["Knead flour, two eggs, lard and water into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Mix grated pecorino, sugar, an egg and lemon zest for the filling.","Roll out the dough and cut large discs, filling them with the mixture.","Fold into half-moons, sealing the edges well.","Fry in hot oil until golden, or bake at 190 °C for 25 minutes."],"o":"They're very like the Ascoli piconi: some areas fry them, others bake them, but the filling stays the same."},"725":{"n":"Easter cheese bread","p":["Dissolve the yeast in a little lukewarm water with a little flour and leave to ferment for thirty minutes.","Knead with the rest of the flour, eggs, grated cheeses and lard into a soft, sticky dough.","Leave to rise for three hours in a tall mould.","The mixture should come almost to the rim of the mould.","Bake at 180 °C for 50 minutes, until well risen and golden on top."],"o":"It's the sweet-savoury Easter version, differing from crescia al formaggio only in proportions and sometimes a little added sugar."},"726":{"n":"Serpe di Cingoli","p":["Knead flour, an egg and a little water into a firm dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Chop the almonds and mix with sugar, lemon zest and an egg into a firm filling.","Roll the dough into a rectangle, spread the filling along a strip and roll it into a log.","Curve the roll into a snake shape, modelling the head at one end.","Bake at 170 °C for 30 minutes, then decorate with cocoa icing for the scales."],"o":"The coiled snake shape recalls the Umbrian torciglione, but here the filling is sweeter and the decoration is cocoa."},"727":{"n":"Frustingo","p":["Soften the dried figs in warm water, then chop them roughly.","Chop the walnuts and almonds, keeping a few whole for decoration.","Mix figs, nuts, soaked raisins, candied peel, cocoa and flour.","Add the oil, combining into a dense, compact mixture.","Pour into a shallow tin, decorate with whole nuts and bake at 160 °C for 45 minutes."],"o":"It improves after resting a few days: freshly made it's drier, after a few days it becomes moist and dense."},"728":{"n":"Bostrengo","p":["Cook the rice in the milk until soft and it has absorbed almost all the liquid, about twenty minutes.","Roughly chop dried figs, soaked raisins and apples in small pieces.","Add everything to the still-warm rice with sugar and chocolate chips.","Pour into a shallow tin and level it well.","Bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes, until the top is golden."],"o":"It's a peasant use-it-up cake, with rice instead of flour: the recipe varies a lot from family to family."},"729":{"n":"Cavallucci di Apiro","p":["Roughly chop the walnuts and dried figs.","Knead flour, vino cotto, oil and cinnamon into a firm dough.","Add the walnuts and figs, spreading them through the dough.","Shape into small flattened ovals.","Bake at 170 °C for 25 minutes, until golden and firm."],"o":"Vino cotto, a reduced grape must typical of the Marche, replaces sugar and eggs: it gives a dark, rustic biscuit."},"730":{"n":"Chitarra with tiny meatballs","p":["Knead flour and eggs into a firm dough, rolling it 5 mm thick.","Lay the sheet on the chitarra, the steel-stringed tool, and press with the rolling pin to cut square spaghetti.","Shape very small meatballs with meat, breadcrumbs, parmesan and an egg.","Cook the meatballs in a light tomato sauce for twenty minutes.","Boil the spaghetti alla chitarra and dress with the sauce and pallottine."],"o":"Pallottine are tiny meatballs, almost like grains: much smaller than ordinary main-course meatballs."},"731":{"n":"Sagne e ceci","p":["Soak the chickpeas overnight, then boil them with rosemary until soft, about an hour and a half.","Knead flour and water for the pasta, roll it out and cut irregular diamonds, the sagne.","Fry garlic and chilli in oil.","Add the chickpeas with some of their cooking water, blending a small part to thicken.","Cook the sagne straight in the chickpea soup until al dente."],"o":"Sagne, egg-free pasta cut into irregular diamonds, are the base of many Abruzzo pulse soups."},"732":{"n":"Sagne e fascioli","p":["Soak the beans overnight and boil until soft, about an hour and a half.","Knead flour and water for the pasta, roll it out and cut irregular diamonds.","Fry garlic and chilli, add the peeled tomatoes and cook for ten minutes.","Add the beans with some of their water, blending a small part to thicken.","Cook the sagne straight in the soup until al dente."],"o":"Same principle as sagne e ceci, with beans instead of chickpeas: the twin soups of Abruzzo tradition."},"733":{"n":"Virtù teramane","p":["Soak the pulses overnight, then boil them separately until soft.","Boil the pork rind separately until tender, then cut into strips.","Make a soffritto with prosciutto and chopped vegetables, adding all the pulses and the rind in turn.","Cover with plenty of water and cook over a low heat for at least two hours.","Add mixed pasta in the last few minutes and serve with parmesan."],"o":"It's made in late spring, when the last winter stores meet the first new vegetables: the more different ingredients, the better."},"734":{"n":"Abruzzo timballo","p":["Make a ragù with meat, passata and peas, cooking for an hour.","Make a batter of flour and eggs for thin crêpes, cooking them one at a time in a pan.","Shape small meatballs and fry them briefly.","Layer crêpes, ragù, meatballs, diced scamorza and parmesan.","Bake at 180 °C for 30 minutes, until piping hot and stringy."],"o":"Instead of pasta sheets, thin crêpes are used here: that's what sets it apart from other southern timbales."},"735":{"n":"Mazzarelle teramane","p":["Cut the offal into strips and season with chopped garlic, parsley and chilli.","Wrap each portion in a lettuce leaf, then bind with a strip of intestine.","Pack the mazzarelle tightly side by side in a pan.","Cover with a little water and cook covered over a low heat for an hour, until very tender.","Serve hot in their juices."],"o":"The double wrapping, first in lettuce and then in intestine, keeps the filling together during the long cooking."},"736":{"n":"Pecora alla callara","p":["Put the pieces of mutton in a large pot with cold water to cover.","Bring to the boil and skim carefully, then add onion, bay leaf and chilli.","Cook over a low heat for an hour and a half, until the meat starts to soften.","Add the chopped tomatoes and cook for another forty minutes.","Serve hot in bowls, with its rich broth."],"o":"The 'callara' is the large copper cauldron in which it's traditionally cooked outdoors at village festivals."},"737":{"n":"Lamb with cheese and egg","p":["Brown the lamb in oil until well coloured.","Deglaze with white wine and let it evaporate.","Cover and cook over a low heat for thirty minutes, adding water if needed.","Beat the eggs with pecorino and lemon juice.","Pour the mixture over the hot meat off the heat, stirring until it sets slightly, as for carbonara."],"o":"Pour in the egg mixture off the heat and stir at once: if it cooks too much the eggs scramble instead of staying creamy."},"738":{"n":"Pollo alla canzanese","p":["Put the whole chicken in a pot with the vegetables, cloves and bay leaf, covering with water.","Bring to the boil and cook over a low heat for an hour and a half, until the meat comes away easily.","Drain the chicken, bone it and break it into rough pieces.","Strain the broth and reduce it until slightly thickened.","Arrange the chicken in a terrine, pour the reduced broth over and chill until set into jelly."],"o":"The reduced broth sets into jelly when cold, enveloping the chicken: serve it cold, sliced, as a starter in aspic."},"739":{"n":"Mussels with saffron","p":["Clean the mussels, scraping the shells well.","Fry the garlic in oil, add the mussels and deglaze with white wine.","Cover and cook until they open, five minutes.","Partly shell them and keep the strained liquid.","Heat the liquid with cream and saffron, then pour it over the mussels and serve hot."],"o":"L'Aquila saffron, among the finest in the world, should be dissolved in a little hot liquid before adding, never added dry."},"740":{"n":"Fiadone abruzzese","p":["Knead flour, two eggs, lard and water into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Beat the remaining eggs with the grated cheeses into a thick mixture.","Roll out the dough, line a mould and pour in the filling.","Cover with a second layer of dough, sealing the edges and pricking the top.","Bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes, until well risen and golden."],"o":"Unlike the Molise fiadone, here the filling is denser with cheese and less like a soufflé."},"741":{"n":"Parrozzo","p":["Grind the almonds finely, almost to a flour.","Whisk the eggs with the sugar until pale and foamy.","Fold in the flour, almonds and lukewarm melted butter gently.","Pour into a dome-shaped, hemispherical mould and bake at 170 °C for 35 minutes.","Let cool and cover completely with melted chocolate, to imitate the dark crust of a loaf."],"o":"The dome shape and dark chocolate coating deliberately imitate a small loaf of toasted bread."},"742":{"n":"Cicerchiata","p":["Knead flour and eggs into a firm, elastic dough.","Roll into thin ropes and cut small balls, like chickpeas, hence the name cicerchiata.","Fry the balls in hot oil until golden.","Warm the honey until runny, without boiling.","Add the fried balls to the warm honey, mix and arrange in a ring, decorating with sprinkles."],"o":"The balls must be really small, like chickpeas: that's where the whole sweet gets its name, from 'cicerchia' (grass pea)."},"743":{"n":"Bocconotti (Abruzzo)","p":["Knead flour, butter, sugar and egg into a smooth shortcrust. Rest for thirty minutes.","Chop the chocolate and almonds and mix with the jam for the filling.","Line small moulds with the pastry, leaving an edge for closing.","Fill and close with a disc of pastry, sealing well.","Bake at 180 °C for 25 minutes, until golden."],"o":"The chocolate and almond filling, besides the jam, is what sets them apart from the simpler Apulian bocconotti."},"744":{"n":"Ferratelle","p":["Whisk the eggs with the sugar until pale.","Add the lukewarm melted butter and the aniseeds.","Fold in the flour to a soft but not runny batter.","Heat the special iron, grease it lightly and pour in a small amount of batter.","Close the iron and cook for a minute per side, until golden and patterned by the iron."],"o":"The decorated iron ('ferro'), from which they take their name, stamps the characteristic honeycomb pattern on both sides."},"745":{"n":"Confetti di Sulmona","p":["Toast the almonds lightly in the oven.","Dissolve the sugar in the water to a thick syrup.","Dip the almonds in the syrup, stirring until well coated.","Spread them apart on baking paper and let the first layer of sugar dry.","Repeat several times, building up layers, until the sugared almond is as thick as you want."],"o":"Coating in repeated layers is slow craft work: each layer must dry before the next."},"746":{"n":"Cavatelli al ragù","p":["Knead flour and water into a firm dough, without eggs.","Roll into ropes and make small hollow dumplings by dragging them over the back of a fork.","Fry the onion, add the meat and brown well.","Add the passata and cook over a low heat for an hour and a half.","Boil the cavatelli, drain and dress with the ragù and pecorino."],"o":"The hollow in cavatelli, made by hand on the back of a fork, is there precisely to hold the thick sauce better."},"747":{"n":"Cavatelli with broccoli and breadcrumbs","p":["Knead flour and water for the cavatelli, shaping them by hand into hollows.","Boil the broccoli florets in salted water, then cook the cavatelli in the same water.","Toast the breadcrumbs in a pan with a drizzle of oil until golden and crisp.","Fry garlic and chilli in oil.","Drain pasta and broccoli together, toss in the garlic oil and finish with the crunchy breadcrumbs."],"o":"Toasted breadcrumbs replace the cheese: they give crunch to an otherwise soft dish."},"748":{"n":"Fusilli alla molisana","p":["Knead flour and water into a firm, egg-free dough.","Roll small pieces around a thin rod and slide them off to form long, twisted fusilli.","Brown the mixed meats in pieces with the onion.","Add the passata and cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meats are very tender.","Boil the fusilli, drain and dress with the mixed ragù and pecorino."],"o":"Real Molise fusilli are rolled by hand around a thin rod, very different from short industrial fusilli."},"749":{"n":"Crioli","p":["Knead the two flours with water into a firm dough.","Roll thin and cut into small squares or diamonds.","Fry garlic, add the chopped tomatoes and cook for ten minutes.","Add the beans with a little of their water.","Cook the crioli in the bean soup until al dente, just a few minutes as they're so thin."],"o":"Cornmeal in the pasta dough, unusual elsewhere, gives crioli a golden colour and rustic flavour."},"750":{"n":"Tacconi","p":["Knead the two flours with water into a firm, elastic dough.","Roll out and cut into large irregular diamonds, wider than sagne.","Fry garlic and rosemary in oil, then add the chickpeas with their water.","Blend some of the chickpeas to thicken the soup.","Cook the tacconi straight in the chickpea soup until al dente."],"o":"Chickpea flour mixed with wheat flour in the dough is what sets them apart from ordinary sagne."},"751":{"n":"Fried pizzelle","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm water and a pinch of salt into a soft dough.","Leave to rise for two hours, until doubled.","Roll out thin discs and press a few broken peeled tomatoes and a piece of anchovy into the centre.","Fold the edges slightly inwards, without closing completely.","Fry in hot oil until golden and puffed."],"o":"Unlike Neapolitan fried pizza, here the topping stays uncovered in the centre, not sealed inside."},"752":{"n":"Pampanella (spicy pork ribs)","p":["Rub the ribs with chopped garlic, plenty of chilli powder and salt.","Arrange them in a tin and pour over the vinegar.","Leave to marinate for at least two hours, ideally overnight.","Roast at 200 °C for an hour, basting now and then with the juices.","Serve piping hot, and spicy as tradition demands."],"o":"Traditionally a very hot dish: don't reduce the chilli if you want authentic pampanella."},"753":{"n":"Polenta with pork sauce","p":["Brown the ribs and sausage in oil with the onion until well coloured.","Add the passata and cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meat is very tender.","Make the polenta by pouring the cornmeal in a stream into boiling salted water, stirring for forty minutes.","Pour the polenta onto a wooden board.","Spread the meat and sauce over the hot polenta and serve straight from the board."],"o":"It's traditionally tipped out onto a big wooden board in the middle of the table, and everyone eats from it."},"754":{"n":"Mignacci","p":["Make a firmer polenta than usual with the two flours and boiling salted water, cooking for thirty minutes.","Pour the hot polenta onto a wet surface and level it 1 cm thick.","Leave to cool completely, then cut into diamonds or squares.","Layer in a dish, alternating with grated pecorino and flakes of butter.","Bake at 200 °C for 15 minutes, until golden on top."],"o":"It's Molise's layered baked polenta: simpler than the Aosta Valley polenta concia, with no fontina."},"755":{"n":"Pezzata di Capracotta","p":["Put the pieces of mutton in a large pot with cold water to cover.","Bring to the boil, skim carefully and add onion, bay leaf and chilli.","Cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meat starts to soften.","Add the chopped tomatoes and cook for another forty minutes.","Serve hot in bowls with its broth: traditionally cooked outdoors for big occasions."],"o":"Born of sheep farming in upper Molise: very long outdoor cooking in large shared cauldrons is part of the tradition."},"756":{"n":"Turcinelli","p":["Wash the intestines and offal at length in water and lemon.","Cut the offal into strips and season with chopped parsley, garlic and chilli.","Wrap each portion tightly in the intestine, forming small twisted rolls.","Thread onto skewers.","Cook over hot embers, turning often, for fifteen to twenty minutes."],"o":"The name comes from twisting ('torcere') the intestine around the filling: the same offal rolls known elsewhere as gnummareddi."},"757":{"n":"Molise-style tripe","p":["Cut the tripe into thin strips.","Fry garlic and chilli in oil.","Add the tripe and let it take on flavour for ten minutes.","Add the passata and cook over a low heat for an hour, until the sauce has reduced.","Serve hot with plenty of pecorino."],"o":"Compared with the Florentine version, there's no carrot and celery soffritto: the seasoning stays more essential and spicy."},"758":{"n":"Baccalà alla portocannone","p":["Cut the salt cod into steaks.","Fry garlic and chilli in oil, add the chopped tomatoes and cook for ten minutes.","Lay the cod in the sauce with the olives.","Cook over a low heat for twenty minutes, never stirring, shaking the pan so as not to break the steaks.","Serve hot with its sauce."],"o":"Named after the village of Portocannone: never stir salt cod with a spoon; only by shaking the pan do you avoid breaking it."},"759":{"n":"Cardoon soup","p":["Clean the cardoons, removing the tough strings, and cut them into chunks, keeping them in water and lemon.","Shape small meatballs with meat, breadcrumbs, parmesan and an egg.","Simmer the cardoons in boiling stock until soft, about forty minutes.","Add the meatballs and cook for another ten minutes.","Beat the remaining egg with parmesan and pour it into the hot soup in a thin stream, stirring."],"o":"As with Roman stracciatella, pour the egg in a thin stream while stirring: that way it forms strands instead of lumps."},"760":{"n":"Frittata with curatella","p":["Cut the curatella into small pieces.","Brown the spring onion in oil, add the curatella and cook until well done, ten minutes.","Beat the eggs with pecorino and a pinch of salt.","Pour the eggs over the hot curatella in the pan.","Cook over a low heat, turning the frittata halfway, until set."],"o":"Curatella, the liver and other tender lamb offal, cooks quickly: cut it small for even cooking."},"761":{"n":"Molise-style scapece","p":["Slice the courgettes thinly lengthways.","Dust them lightly with flour and fry in hot oil until golden.","Heat the vinegar with the saffron and crushed garlic, without boiling.","Layer the fried courgettes in a container, pouring the flavoured vinegar over each layer.","Leave to marinate in the fridge for at least a day before serving."],"o":"Saffron in the vinegar is what sets this scapece apart from other regions' versions, usually just vinegar and mint."},"762":{"n":"Ostie ripiene","p":["Roughly chop the almonds and walnuts.","Warm the honey until runny, without boiling.","Add the chopped nuts and cocoa to the honey, mixing well.","Spread a spoonful of the mixture between two wafers, pressing lightly so it sticks.","Leave to cool and set before serving."],"o":"The very thin wafers soften slightly against the warm filling: fill them as soon as the honey is warmed."},"763":{"n":"Pigna molisana","p":["Dissolve the yeast in a little lukewarm milk with a spoonful of sugar.","Knead flour, eggs, the remaining sugar and aniseeds into a smooth dough.","Work in the soft butter a little at a time.","Leave to rise for three hours, until tripled in volume.","Shape into a tall loaf and bake at 170 °C for 45 minutes."],"o":"It's the Molise Easter cake, tall like a pine cone: whole aniseeds are its characteristic aromatic note."},"764":{"n":"Cancelle","p":["Whisk the eggs with the sugar until pale.","Add the lukewarm melted butter and the aniseeds.","Fold in the flour to a soft but not runny batter.","Heat the special iron, grease it lightly and pour in a small amount of batter.","Close the iron and cook for a minute per side, until golden and patterned by the iron."],"o":"They're practically identical to Abruzzo ferratelle: same decorated iron, same technique, just a different name."},"765":{"n":"Molise mostaccioli","p":["Warm the honey slightly, without boiling.","Mix the flour with cinnamon and cocoa, then pour in the warm honey and combine.","Add the roughly chopped almonds.","Roll out 1 cm thick and cut into diamonds.","Bake at 170 °C for 15 minutes: they stay soft in the centre and harden as they cool."],"o":"Cocoa in the dough makes them darker than Sicilian mustazzoli, which stay honey-coloured."},"766":{"n":"Frico","p":["Boil the potatoes, peel them and cut into small dice.","Fry the onion in butter until golden.","Add the potatoes and crush them lightly in the pan.","Add the coarsely grated Montasio, spreading it evenly.","Cook over a medium heat until a golden crust forms underneath, then flip like a frittata and brown the other side."],"o":"The cheese must melt and then firm up again into a crust: the patience not to turn it too soon is the key."},"767":{"n":"Jota","p":["Soak the beans overnight, then boil them with bay leaf until soft.","Separately simmer the smoked ribs until tender.","Fry garlic in oil, add the sauerkraut and let it take on flavour for ten minutes.","Add the beans, potato chunks and ribs with their broth.","Cook over a low heat for an hour, until the potatoes are soft and the soup thick."],"o":"The sauerkraut gives the characteristic sourness: it's Trieste's signature soup, halfway between Italian and Central European cooking."},"768":{"n":"Blecs","p":["Knead the two flours with eggs and a little water into a firm dough.","Roll out the sheet and cut irregular triangles.","Boil the blecs in salted water for a few minutes.","Melt the butter until it starts to colour slightly.","Dress the blecs with the melted butter and plenty of grated smoked ricotta."],"o":"The irregular triangle shape, cut by hand without precision, is deliberate: it's a dish for using up pasta scraps."},"769":{"n":"Plum gnocchi","p":["Boil the potatoes, peel them while hot and pass them through a ricer.","Knead with flour and egg into a soft dough.","Pit the plums and replace each stone with a sugar cube.","Wrap each plum in a portion of dough, sealing well.","Boil in salted water until they float, then roll in breadcrumbs toasted in butter."],"o":"The whole plum hidden inside the gnocco is the surprise of the dish: when you bite in it releases its hot, sweet juice."},"770":{"n":"Bread dumplings","p":["Cut the stale bread into small dice and moisten with the milk, leaving it to soften for ten minutes.","Brown the diced speck in a little butter.","Mix the bread with eggs, flour and the browned speck into a firm mixture.","Shape into compact balls with damp hands.","Boil in salted water until they float, then dress with melted butter."],"o":"A dish for using up old bread, very like Trentino canederli but with less cheese and more speck."},"771":{"n":"Orzotto (barley risotto)","p":["Soften the chopped onion in half the butter.","Toast the barley for a couple of minutes.","Add the hot stock a ladle at a time, as for a risotto, for about thirty minutes.","The barley should stay slightly al dente, more rustic than rice.","Off the heat, beat in cold butter and parmesan."],"o":"Barley absorbs liquid more slowly than rice: it takes patience and more stock than you'd expect."},"772":{"n":"Brovada e muset","p":["Prick the muset (cotechino) with a cocktail stick and simmer in water for an hour and a half, until tender.","Fry the onion in oil, add the brovada, the fermented turnip strips, and let it take on flavour.","Cover with a little water and bay leaf and cook over a low heat for forty minutes.","Cut the muset into thick slices.","Serve the hot muset on top of the brovada."],"o":"Brovada, white turnips fermented in grape pomace, has a unique sour taste: it can't be replaced with fresh vegetables."},"773":{"n":"Rice and savoy cabbage","p":["Brown the pancetta with the chopped onion.","Add the cabbage in strips and let it wilt for ten minutes.","Toast the rice for a couple of minutes.","Add stock or hot water a ladle at a time, as for a risotto, for sixteen minutes.","Off the heat, stir in parmesan."],"o":"It should stay looser than a classic risotto, almost a thick soup: don't let it dry out too much."},"774":{"n":"Mignàc di gries","p":["Bring the milk to the boil with the lemon zest and half the sugar.","Pour in the semolina in a stream, stirring constantly.","Cook for ten minutes until thick, then take off the heat and let it cool slightly.","Beat in the eggs beaten with the remaining sugar.","Pour into a buttered mould and bake at 180 °C for 30 minutes, until golden on top."],"o":"It's a baked semolina pudding, a simple sweet of Friulian farmhouse tradition, often served with jam."},"775":{"n":"Pitina","p":["Slice the pitina thinly.","Leave it a few minutes at room temperature before serving.","Arrange it on a board with the bread.","Serve with a good local wine.","Serve as a starter, with nothing added."],"o":"It's a smoked cured meat made from minced, spiced goat or sheep meat, typical of the Val Tramontina."},"776":{"n":"Prosciutto di San Daniele with melon","p":["Cut the melon into wedges, removing seeds and rind.","Slice the prosciutto very thinly.","Wrap each melon wedge in a slice of prosciutto, or arrange them separately on the plate.","Serve chilled, with the melon well cold.","Don't add any other dressing, which would mask the two flavours."],"o":"San Daniele, sweeter and more delicate than other cured hams, should be sliced very thin to bring out its sweet note."},"777":{"n":"Trieste goulash","p":["Slice the onions thinly and stew them in oil until sweet and translucent, about twenty minutes.","Brown the meat in pieces separately, then add it to the onions.","Sprinkle with paprika off the heat, stirring at once so it doesn't burn.","Add passata, garlic and caraway, covering with water.","Cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meat is very tender and the sauce thick."],"o":"Always add paprika off the heat: over a high flame it burns in seconds and turns bitter."},"778":{"n":"Scampi alla busara","p":["Fry garlic and chilli in plenty of oil.","Add the whole scampi and brown for two minutes.","Deglaze with white wine.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and cook for ten minutes over a lively heat.","Sprinkle with parsley and serve hot with bread for mopping up."],"o":"Scampi need very little cooking: beyond ten minutes in total the flesh turns rubbery."},"779":{"n":"Breaded sardoni","p":["Clean the sardoni, opening them out like a book and removing the backbone.","Dip them in beaten egg and then breadcrumbs, pressing well.","Fry in very hot oil for a few minutes per side, until golden.","Drain on kitchen paper.","Serve hot with lemon wedges."],"o":"Sardoni, Adriatic sardines, must be fried very quickly: the flesh is delicate and dries out fast."},"780":{"n":"Calandraca","p":["Soak the beans and barley separately for a few hours.","Fry the onion, add the potato chunks, the savoy cabbage and the drained beans.","Cover with plenty of water and cook for an hour.","Add the barley and cook for another thirty minutes, until everything is soft.","Serve hot and thick, with a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"It's a mixed soup from the Karst that brings together whatever is in the cupboard: pulses, grains and vegetables in one dish."},"781":{"n":"Bobici","p":["Brown the pancetta with the chopped onion.","Add the fresh sweetcorn kernels and diced potatoes.","Cover with water and cook over a low heat for forty minutes, until the potatoes are soft.","The texture should stay thick, almost a reduced soup.","Finish with torn fresh basil before serving."],"o":"Fresh corn kernels, not cornmeal, are the speciality of this Istrian soup: young summer cobs are used."},"782":{"n":"Strucchi","p":["Knead flour, butter, egg and a little water into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Chop the walnuts and mix with soaked raisins, sugar and rum for the filling.","Roll the dough thinly and cut out discs.","Put a teaspoon of filling on each disc, fold into a half-moon and roll it slightly into a swirl.","Fry in hot oil until golden, or bake at 190 °C for 20 minutes."],"o":"They're the fried cousins of gubana: same walnut and raisin filling, but in small individual portions."},"783":{"n":"Gubana","p":["Knead flour, yeast, eggs, soft butter and sugar into a smooth dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Chop walnuts and pine nuts and mix with soaked raisins and grappa for the filling.","Roll the dough into a thin rectangle and spread the filling over it.","Roll up tightly into a log, then coil it into a snail.","Leave to rise for another hour and bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes."],"o":"The coiled snail shape is what reveals the spiral of filling inside when sliced."},"784":{"n":"Presnitz","p":["Chop walnuts and almonds and mix with soaked raisins, candied peel and rum for the filling.","Roll the puff pastry into a rectangle and spread the filling over the whole surface.","Roll up tightly into a log.","Curve the roll into a coil, forming a flat spiral.","Bake at 190 °C for 30 minutes, until golden."],"o":"The flat spiral shape, unlike gubana's tall snail, is what visually sets this Trieste cake apart."},"785":{"n":"Putizza","p":["Knead flour, yeast, eggs, soft butter and sugar into a smooth dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Chop walnuts and chocolate and mix with soaked raisins for the filling.","Roll the dough into a rectangle and spread the filling over it.","Roll up tightly and coil into a snail, as for gubana.","Leave to rise for another hour and bake at 180 °C for 40 minutes."],"o":"It's very like gubana, but with chocolate added to the filling: the typical cake of the Gorizia area."},"786":{"n":"Fritole triestine","p":["Dissolve the yeast in lukewarm milk with a spoonful of sugar.","Mix flour, eggs, the yeasted milk and grappa into a thick batter.","Leave to rise for two hours, until doubled.","Fold in soaked raisins and pine nuts.","Fry spoonfuls in hot oil until golden and puffed, then roll in granulated sugar."],"o":"They're the Trieste version of Venetian carnival fritters, with grappa added to the batter."},"787":{"n":"Speck canederli","p":["Cut the stale bread into small dice and moisten with the milk, leaving it to soften for ten minutes.","Brown the diced speck in a pan with no fat.","Mix the bread with speck, eggs, flour and chives into a firm mixture.","Shape into egg-sized balls with damp hands.","Cook in boiling broth for fifteen minutes and serve in the broth, or drained with melted butter."],"o":"Let the mixture rest a few minutes before shaping, so the bread absorbs the liquid well and the canederli don't fall apart while cooking."},"788":{"n":"Schlutzkrapfen","p":["Knead the two flours with eggs and water into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Blanch the spinach, squeeze it very hard and chop it.","Mix with ricotta and a pinch of nutmeg for the filling.","Roll the dough thin, cut out discs, fill and fold into half-moons.","Boil in salted water and dress with melted butter and chives."],"o":"They're South Tyrolean buckwheat ravioli, very like casunziei but with a spinach and ricotta filling."},"789":{"n":"Spätzle","p":["Whisk flour, eggs, milk and a pinch of salt into a thick batter that falls in ribbons.","Let the batter drip in small drops into boiling salted water, using a spätzle maker or a large-holed colander.","Lift out the spätzle as soon as they float, after a couple of minutes.","Toss them in a pan with melted butter.","Finish with cheese shavings just before serving."],"o":"The purpose-made spätzle grater makes the job much faster than dripping the batter by hand with a knife."},"790":{"n":"Strangolapreti","p":["Dice the bread and moisten with the milk, leaving it to soften.","Blanch the spinach, squeeze it very hard and chop it.","Mix bread, spinach, eggs and flour into a firm mixture.","Shape small oval dumplings with damp hands.","Boil in salted water and dress with melted butter and parmesan."],"o":"The name means 'priest stranglers': legend has it greedy priests wolfed them down so fast they nearly choked."},"791":{"n":"Tirtlen","p":["Knead the two flours with water and a pinch of salt into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Drain the sauerkraut well, squeezing it, and mix with the cheese in small pieces.","Roll the dough thin and cut out large discs.","Put the filling on half of each disc and fold into a half-moon, sealing the edges well.","Fry in hot oil until golden on both sides."],"o":"They're the fried pockets of the Val Pusteria: the filling can vary, but sauerkraut or spinach remain the most common."},"792":{"n":"Sweet apricot dumplings","p":["Boil the potatoes, peel them while hot and pass them through a ricer.","Knead with flour and egg into a soft dough.","Pit the apricots and replace each stone with a sugar cube.","Wrap each apricot in a portion of dough, sealing well.","Boil until they float, then roll in breadcrumbs toasted in butter and sugar."],"o":"They're the sweet cousins of Friulian plum gnocchi: same technique, with apricot instead of plum."},"793":{"n":"Tyrolean goulash","p":["Slice the onions thinly and stew them in oil until sweet, about twenty minutes.","Brown the meat in pieces separately, then add it to the onions.","Sprinkle with paprika off the heat, stirring at once.","Deglaze with the wine, add tomato purée and caraway, covering with water.","Cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meat is very tender; serve with polenta or canederli."],"o":"Compared with Trieste goulash, here it's more often served with canederli or polenta than plain bread."},"794":{"n":"Carne salada e fasoi","p":["Slice the carne salada very thinly, like carpaccio.","Warm the boiled beans with a drizzle of oil and a pinch of salt.","Slice the red onion thinly and leave it in cold water for ten minutes.","Arrange the carne salada on a plate and top with the still-warm beans.","Finish with the onion and a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"The hot-cold contrast between the freshly warmed beans and the thinly sliced cured raw beef is the heart of the dish."},"795":{"n":"Polenta di Storo","p":["Bring plenty of salted water to the boil.","Pour in the cornmeal in a stream, stirring constantly to avoid lumps.","Cook, stirring often, for forty minutes, until it comes away from the sides of the pot.","Stir in the butter and the cheese in chunks for the last few minutes.","Pour onto a wooden board and serve hot."],"o":"Storo cornmeal, a local Trentino variety, has a deeper colour and a stronger flavour than ordinary cornmeal."},"796":{"n":"Speck with bread and pickles","p":["Slice the speck thinly.","Cut the rye bread into not-too-thick slices.","Arrange speck, bread and gherkins on a board.","Add fresh butter to spread on the bread, if you like.","Serve as a starter or snack, with nothing added."],"o":"It's the classic South Tyrolean 'Marende' snack: a few quality ingredients, no cooking needed."},"797":{"n":"Trentino luganega","p":["Coil the luganega into a spiral in a frying pan.","Brown over a medium heat, turning the whole spiral, until well coloured on both sides.","Deglaze with white wine and let it dry out.","Make the polenta by pouring the cornmeal in a stream into boiling salted water, stirring for forty minutes.","Serve the hot luganega on the soft polenta."],"o":"Cook the sausage whole in a spiral, not cut into pieces: it keeps its juices better while cooking."},"798":{"n":"Smacafam","p":["Crumble the sausage and spread it in an oiled tin.","Whisk flour, milk, eggs and a pinch of salt into a smooth batter.","Pour the batter over the sausage in the tin.","Bake at 200 °C for 35 minutes, until the top is golden and firm.","Cut into squares and serve hot, as a one-dish meal."],"o":"The name literally means 'kill hunger': a humble, hearty dish from the Val di Fiemme."},"799":{"n":"Tonco de pontesel","p":["Brown the meat in pieces in oil with the chopped vegetables.","Deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate.","Sprinkle with flour and stir for two minutes.","Cover with water and cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meat falls apart easily.","Shred the meat into the thick sauce and serve with polenta."],"o":"It's the Trentino stew traditionally served with polenta: the thick sauce is the most prized part of the dish."},"800":{"n":"Strauben","p":["Whisk flour, milk, eggs and grappa into a runny, lump-free batter.","Leave to rest for thirty minutes.","Pour the batter in a thin stream into the hot oil, moving your hand in a spiral to create a tangled shape.","Fry until golden and crisp, a couple of minutes.","Drain, dust with icing sugar and serve with lingonberry jam."],"o":"The spiral shape is made by pouring the batter in a thin stream while moving your hand, as if drawing a continuous curl."},"801":{"n":"Zelten","p":["Soak the figs and raisins in warm water with the grappa.","Knead flour, yeast, soft butter and a little sugar into a smooth dough.","Leave to rise for two hours.","Work in the drained dried fruit, nuts and candied peel, spreading them well through the dough.","Shape into a flattened loaf and bake at 170 °C for 50 minutes."],"o":"It's South Tyrol's Christmas cake par excellence: more fruit and nuts than dough, almost a leavened panforte."},"802":{"n":"Krapfen","p":["Dissolve the yeast in lukewarm milk with a spoonful of sugar.","Knead flour, eggs, the yeasted milk and sugar into a smooth dough.","Work in the soft butter a little at a time.","Leave to rise for two hours, roll out and cut discs, then leave to rise for another forty minutes.","Fry in oil at 170 °C until golden, then pipe in jam and roll in sugar."],"o":"They're South Tyrolean doughnuts: the jam is piped in after frying, never before, so it doesn't burn in the oil."},"803":{"n":"Kaiserschmarrn","p":["Whisk the yolks with flour, milk and half the sugar into a smooth batter.","Whisk the egg whites to stiff peaks with the remaining sugar.","Gently fold the whisked whites into the batter, along with the raisins.","Pour into a buttered pan and cook like a thick omelette, one side at a time.","When almost set, tear it roughly with two forks right in the pan and let it caramelise for a few minutes."],"o":"It's torn apart in the pan at the end, not sliced: the irregular, slightly caramelised pieces are the point of the dish."},"804":{"n":"Fileja with 'nduja","p":["Melt the 'nduja in oil over a low heat, crumbling it with a wooden spoon.","Add garlic and halved cherry tomatoes, cooking for ten minutes.","Boil the fileja, pasta rolled by hand around a thin rod, in salted water.","Drain al dente and toss in the spicy sauce.","Finish with grated pecorino."],"o":"'Nduja melts straight into the oil as a sauce: it isn't cut into pieces like an ordinary salami."},"805":{"n":"Fileja with goat ragù","p":["Brown the goat in pieces in oil with the onion.","Deglaze with red wine and let it evaporate.","Add the passata and cover with water.","Cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meat comes off the bone, then shred it into the sauce.","Boil the fileja and dress with the goat ragù and pecorino."],"o":"Goat, a robust and flavourful meat, is a tradition of the Sila mountains: it needs long cooking to tenderise."},"806":{"n":"Pitta calabrese","p":["Knead flour, yeast, lukewarm water and oil into a soft dough. Leave to rise for two hours.","Spread it in a tin with your fingers.","Cover with crushed peeled tomatoes, pieces of anchovy and chunks of caciocavallo.","Sprinkle with oregano and a drizzle of oil.","Bake at 220 °C for 25 minutes."],"o":"It's Calabria's traditional round bread, topped like a focaccia: the ring shape is its hallmark."},"807":{"n":"Pitta 'mpigliata","p":["Knead flour, wine and oil into a smooth, elastic dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Chop walnuts and soaked raisins and mix with honey and cinnamon for the filling.","Roll out thin strips of dough, spread with the filling and roll each strip up into a small swirl.","Pack the swirls tightly side by side to form a large spiral in a round tin.","Bake at 170 °C for 35 minutes, then drizzle with more warm honey straight from the oven."],"o":"The name means 'tangled', from the many small swirls woven together into the final spiral."},"808":{"n":"Maccarruni al ferretto","p":["Knead flour and water into a firm dough, without eggs.","Roll small pieces around a thin rod and slide them off to form long, twisted maccarruni.","Brown the meat with the onion, add the passata and cook over a low heat for an hour and a half.","Boil the maccarruni: they're thick and need longer than usual.","Drain and dress with the ragù and pecorino."],"o":"They're practically identical to Molise fusilli in technique, but the name and the ragù change from area to area."},"809":{"n":"Stroncatura with anchovies and breadcrumbs","p":["Toast the breadcrumbs in a pan with a drizzle of oil until golden and crisp, then set aside.","Fry garlic and chilli in oil, melting the anchovies.","Boil the pasta al dente and drain, keeping the cooking water.","Toss the pasta in the anchovy sauce, loosening with water if needed.","Finish with the crunchy breadcrumbs just before serving."],"o":"Stroncatura, made from the leftover flour of pasta milling, is a symbol of Calabrian poverty cooking: breadcrumbs replace cheese, once too expensive."},"810":{"n":"Calabrian baked pasta","p":["Make a ragù with meat and passata, cooking for an hour.","Boil the pasta very al dente and dress with some of the ragù.","Line a dish with the dressed pasta, layering it with sliced hard-boiled eggs, diced caciocavallo and pecorino.","Finish with a layer of pasta and ragù on top.","Bake at 190 °C for 30 minutes, until nicely browned."],"o":"Hard-boiled eggs in the layers are common to many southern timbales, from Neapolitan sartù to this baked pasta."},"811":{"n":"Calabrian minestra maritata","p":["Simmer the ribs in plenty of water for an hour, keeping the broth.","Wash the bitter greens well and chop them roughly.","Brown the sausage in pieces, then add it to the broth together with the greens.","Cook for forty minutes, until the greens are soft.","Serve hot with grated pecorino."],"o":"The 'marriage' is exactly the union of meat and bitter greens in the same broth, hence the soup's name."},"812":{"n":"Patate 'mpacchiuse","p":["Cut the potatoes into small, even pieces.","Fry garlic in oil and add the chopped cherry tomatoes.","Add the potatoes and a little water, just to cover.","Cook over a medium heat without a lid, stirring now and then, until the water has gone and the potatoes are creamy and sticky.","Finish with basil and pecorino."],"o":"'Mpacchiuse' means sticky: the potatoes should release their starch and almost stick together, not collapse into mash."},"813":{"n":"Calabrian aubergine parmigiana","p":["Slice the aubergines lengthways and fry them in oil until golden, draining on kitchen paper.","Make a quick sauce with passata, garlic and basil, cooking for fifteen minutes.","Layer fried aubergines, sauce, sliced caciocavallo and pecorino.","Finish with sauce and cheese on top.","Bake at 180 °C for 30 minutes, until well browned."],"o":"Caciocavallo instead of mozzarella is what sets this Calabrian parmigiana apart from the Campanian or Sicilian ones."},"814":{"n":"Stuffed aubergines","p":["Halve the aubergines and scoop out the flesh, leaving a thick border.","Chop the flesh and sauté it in a pan with oil until soft.","Mix the flesh with breadcrumbs, capers, chopped olives and pecorino.","Fill the aubergine halves with the mixture and arrange them in a dish with cherry tomatoes.","Bake at 190 °C for 35 minutes, until soft and golden."],"o":"Leave the aubergine shell thick enough to hold the filling without giving way as it cooks."},"815":{"n":"Morzeddhu catanzarese","p":["Boil the offal in water for twenty minutes, then cut it into strips.","Fry garlic and chilli in plenty of oil.","Add the offal and brown well.","Add the passata and cook over a low heat for an hour, until the sauce is thick and dark.","Serve the morzello inside a hollowed-out pitta, like a filled sandwich."],"o":"Catanzaro's signature dish is traditionally eaten inside the pitta, never from a plate with a fork."},"816":{"n":"Stocco alla mammolese","p":["Briefly fry the dried peppers in hot oil, just to crisp them, then set aside.","Fry garlic and chilli in the same oil.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and cook for ten minutes.","Lay in the stockfish pieces with potato chunks and olives, adding water just to cover.","Cook over a low heat for an hour, shaking the pot, and finish with the crisp dried peppers."],"o":"Crisp dried peppers added at the end are the feature that sets the Mammola version apart."},"817":{"n":"Calabrian salt cod","p":["Cut the salt cod into steaks.","Fry garlic and chilli, add the peppers in strips and let them soften.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and olives, cooking for ten minutes.","Lay the cod in the sauce.","Cook over a low heat for twenty minutes, shaking the pan without stirring, until the cod is cooked."],"o":"Fresh pepper strips together with chilli give this Calabrian version its typical colour and heat."},"818":{"n":"Stuffed anchovies","p":["Clean the anchovies, opening them like a book and removing the backbone.","Mix breadcrumbs, chopped capers, garlic and pecorino with a drizzle of oil.","Spread the filling over half the opened anchovies.","Cover with the other half, closing them like a book.","Bake at 190 °C for 20 minutes, until golden, and serve with lemon."],"o":"The anchovies are paired with the filling in between: no need to tie them, the moist crumbs hold them together."},"819":{"n":"Calabrian capocollo and soppressata","p":["Slice the capocollo thinly.","Slice the soppressata slightly thicker, as it's denser.","Arrange both on a board with the bread.","Leave at room temperature for a few minutes before serving.","Serve with a good local red wine, with nothing else added."],"o":"Both are spiced with Calabrian chilli during curing: the heat is part of their identity."},"820":{"n":"'Nduja di Spilinga","p":["Leave the 'nduja at room temperature: it should be soft and spreadable.","Toast the bread lightly, if you like.","Spread the 'nduja on the bread with a knife, like a spicy pâté.","Serve as a starter, perhaps with a fresh cheese on the side to balance the heat.","Don't heat it too much: it loses some of its aroma."],"o":"It's a salami for spreading, not slicing: its intense heat is its defining feature, from Spilinga near Vibo Valentia."},"821":{"n":"Griddled caciocavallo silano","p":["Cut the caciocavallo into 1 cm thick slices.","Heat a griddle or non-stick pan well, without adding fat.","Cook the slices for two minutes per side, until a golden crust forms.","Sprinkle with oregano as soon as it comes off the heat.","Serve immediately, while the cheese is still stringy inside."],"o":"Caciocavallo silano, a stretched-curd cheese from the Calabrian mountains, is cooked very briefly, like Sardinian casizolu."},"822":{"n":"Tartufo di Pizzo","p":["Let the ice creams soften slightly at room temperature.","Shape small central balls of chocolate ice cream and keep them in the freezer.","Wrap each chocolate ball in a layer of hazelnut ice cream, shaping it into a sphere.","Return to the freezer until firmly set.","Roll each tartufo in unsweetened cocoa before serving."],"o":"The chocolate heart hidden in the centre is the surprise of Pizzo Calabro's signature dessert."},"823":{"n":"Cuzzupa","p":["Knead flour, three eggs, sugar, soft butter and baking powder into a firm dough.","Shape into braids or rings, pressing well-washed raw eggs in their shells into the dough.","Secure the eggs with two crossed strips of dough.","Brush with the remaining beaten egg.","Bake at 180 °C for 30 minutes; the eggs hard-cook right in the dough."],"o":"Like Neapolitan casatiello, raw eggs in their shells bake along with the dough: the Calabrian Easter symbol."},"824":{"n":"Calabrian pignolata","p":["Knead flour and eggs into a firm, elastic dough.","Roll into thin ropes and cut small balls, like chickpeas.","Fry the balls in hot oil until golden.","Warm the honey until runny, without boiling.","Add the fried balls to the warm honey, mix and arrange in a pyramid, decorating with sprinkles."],"o":"It's the Calabrian version of Abruzzo's cicerchiata, on the same principle but shaped as a pyramid instead of a ring."},"825":{"n":"Torrone di Bagnara","p":["Heat the honey over a bain-marie for about an hour, stirring now and then, until it thickens.","Whisk the egg whites to very stiff peaks.","Fold the whites into the hot honey a little at a time, still cooking over the bain-marie.","After another hour, when the mixture is thick, add the toasted almonds.","Pour onto wafer paper, level it, let it cool and coat with melted chocolate before cutting into bars."],"o":"The dark chocolate coating is what sets Bagnara torrone apart from Sardinia's Tonara torrone, otherwise similar."},"826":{"n":"Calabrian mostaccioli","p":["Warm the honey slightly, without boiling.","Mix the flour with cinnamon and ground cloves.","Pour the warm honey over the flour and combine into a firm mixture.","Roll out 1 cm thick and cut with animal- or heart-shaped cutters, as local tradition has it.","Bake at 170 °C for 15 minutes, then decorate with coloured icing once cold."],"o":"The decorative shapes, often stylised animals, are an old tradition: they were given as small gifts at village fairs."},"827":{"n":"Nacatole","p":["Knead flour, wine, oil and sugar into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Roll thin and cut strips, plaiting or rolling them into small decorative shapes.","Fry in hot oil until golden and crisp.","Drain on kitchen paper.","Dip them while still hot in warm honey, or dust with sugar."],"o":"The hand-plaited shapes vary from family to family: there's no fixed pattern, just tradition handed down."},"828":{"n":"Pasta with breadcrumbs and peperoni cruschi","p":["Quickly fry the peperoni cruschi in hot oil for a few seconds until crisp, then crumble them.","Toast the breadcrumbs in the same oil with garlic and chilli until golden.","Boil the pasta al dente and drain.","Toss it in the toasted breadcrumbs.","Finish with the crumbled peperoni cruschi on top."],"o":"Peperoni cruschi, sun-dried peppers, fry in a few seconds: left in the oil too long they turn bitter and burnt."},"829":{"n":"Strascinati with breadcrumbs","p":["Toast the breadcrumbs in a pan with a drizzle of oil until golden and crisp.","Fry garlic and chilli, melting the anchovies.","Boil the strascinati, a hollowed pasta similar to orecchiette, in salted water.","Drain al dente and toss in the anchovy sauce.","Finish with the crunchy breadcrumbs on top."],"o":"Breadcrumbs replace cheese in Lucanian peasant tradition: keep them separate and add them only at the end."},"830":{"n":"Cavatelli with rafanata","p":["Knead flour and water for the cavatelli, shaping them by hand into hollows.","Finely grate the fresh horseradish, as pungent as wasabi.","Beat the eggs with pecorino and grated horseradish.","Boil the cavatelli and drain al dente.","Toss the pasta with butter, then pour in the egg mixture off the heat, stirring until it sets slightly."],"o":"Fresh horseradish is very pungent: add it cautiously, increasing only if you really want the typical hot-sharp kick."},"831":{"n":"Tumacë me tule","p":["Knead flour and water into a firm, egg-free dough.","Roll small pieces around a thin rod to form long, twisted tumacë.","Brown the mixed meats with the onion and chilli.","Add the passata and cook over a low heat for two hours, until the meats are very tender.","Boil the tumacë, drain and dress with the mixed ragù and pecorino."],"o":"The name comes from the Arbëresh (Italo-Albanian) communities of Basilicata: these are Lucanian fusilli in the local dialect."},"832":{"n":"Crapiata materana","p":["Soak the pulses and grains together in plenty of water overnight.","Drain them and put them in a pot with potato chunks, chopped tomatoes and onion.","Cover with plenty of water.","Cook over a low heat for two hours, until everything is soft and the soup has thickened.","Serve hot with a drizzle of raw oil."],"o":"It comes from Matera, made to celebrate the end of the harvest with all the leftover pulses and grains, a little of each."},"833":{"n":"Matera bread with vegetables","p":["Cut the bread into thick slices and toast lightly.","Quickly fry the peperoni cruschi in hot oil for a few seconds and crumble them.","Dress the still-hot bread with plenty of oil.","Top with sliced tomato and the crumbled peperoni cruschi.","Finish with oregano and serve at once."],"o":"Matera bread, baked in a wood oven with a thick crust, soaks up oil better than ordinary bread."},"834":{"n":"Rafanata lucana","p":["Boil the potatoes, peel and roughly mash them.","Finely grate the fresh horseradish.","Beat the eggs with pecorino, mashed potatoes and horseradish.","Pour into a pan with a drizzle of oil.","Cook over a low heat like a thick frittata, turning halfway, until well set."],"o":"It's a rustic frittata where horseradish gives all the spicy character: without it, it would be just a potato frittata."},"835":{"n":"Pane cotto","p":["Fry garlic and chilli in oil.","Add the vegetables in pieces and let them wilt.","Cover with plenty of water and cook until the vegetables are soft, twenty minutes.","Cut the stale bread into slices or large pieces.","Put the bread into the hot soup and let it soak for a few minutes before serving."],"o":"The name ('cooked bread') describes exactly what happens: old bread cooks in the soup until completely soft."},"836":{"n":"Salt cod with peperoni cruschi","p":["Cut the salt cod into steaks and dust lightly with flour.","Fry the steaks in hot oil until golden.","Quickly fry the peperoni cruschi in hot oil for a few seconds and crumble them.","Fry the garlic in the leftover oil.","Plate the fried cod topped with the crumbled peperoni cruschi and golden garlic."],"o":"The two elements, fried cod and peperoni cruschi, are fried separately and combined only at the end, never together."},"837":{"n":"Agnello alla pastora","p":["Brown the lamb in oil with garlic until well coloured.","Cover and cook over a low heat for thirty minutes, adding water if needed.","Beat the eggs with pecorino and lemon juice.","Pour the mixture over the hot meat off the heat, stirring until it sets slightly.","Serve at once, while the sauce is still soft and creamy."],"o":"Same technique as Abruzzo's lamb with cheese and egg: pour in the mixture off the heat so it stays creamy."},"838":{"n":"Pezzente sausage from Matera","p":["Prick the pezzente sausage with a cocktail stick.","Brown it in oil over a medium heat until well coloured.","Add the crushed peeled tomatoes and chilli.","Cook over a low heat for twenty-five minutes, until the sauce has reduced.","Serve hot, traditionally with polenta or bread."],"o":"Pezzente ('beggar') sausage, made from the humblest cuts of pork, was the cured meat of the poorest farmers: hence the name."},"839":{"n":"Lucanica sausage","p":["Finely chop the fennel seeds.","Mix the meat with fennel, chilli, salt and a little cold water.","Leave to take on flavour in the fridge for an hour.","Stuff into natural casing, tying the sausages.","Grill over embers for twenty minutes, turning often."],"o":"The name 'lucanica', origin of the word 'luganega' used across northern Italy, comes from Lucania, the ancient name for Basilicata."},"840":{"n":"Cutturiddi","p":["Brown the pieces of mutton in oil with the onion until well coloured.","Add chopped tomatoes and chilli, cooking for ten minutes.","Cover with plenty of water and cook over a low heat for an hour and a half.","Add the potatoes in large chunks and cook for another forty minutes.","Serve hot with grated pecorino on top."],"o":"It's the quintessential Lucanian shepherds' stew, traditionally cooked in a big cauldron for village festivals."},"841":{"n":"Lucanian roast kid","p":["Rub the pieces of kid with garlic, rosemary, salt and oil.","Arrange them in a tin alternating with potato chunks.","Pour over the white wine and a drizzle of oil.","Roast at 200 °C for an hour, turning the pieces halfway.","Sprinkle with grated pecorino for the last ten minutes."],"o":"The pecorino added in the last minutes forms a light golden crust over the meat and potatoes."},"842":{"n":"Chestnut calzoncelli","p":["Knead flour, wine and oil into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Push the boiled chestnuts through a sieve to make a purée.","Mix the purée with melted chocolate and cinnamon for the filling.","Roll out the dough and cut discs, filling them with the mixture.","Fold into half-moons and fry in hot oil until golden, or bake at 190 °C for 25 minutes."],"o":"The chestnut filling, typical of Lucanian Christmas tradition, must be made smooth through a sieve before use."},"843":{"n":"Sweet panzerotti","p":["Knead flour, wine and oil into a smooth dough. Rest for thirty minutes.","Push the boiled chickpeas through a sieve to make a smooth purée.","Mix the purée with melted chocolate and orange zest for the filling.","Roll out the dough and cut discs, filling them with the mixture.","Fold into half-moons and fry in hot oil until golden."],"o":"A chickpea filling instead of the more usual ricotta or chestnuts is the speciality of this Lucanian Christmas version."},"844":{"n":"Strazzate","p":["Chop the almonds roughly.","Mix flour, sugar, cocoa and almonds.","Add the oil and cold coffee, combining into a firm but crumbly mixture.","Shape small irregular diamonds by hand.","Bake at 160 °C for 15 minutes: they should stay soft inside; 'strazzate' means torn, irregular."],"o":"Don't overwork them: the cracked, irregular surface that gives them their name is deliberate and characteristic."},"845":{"n":"Egg taralli","p":["Knead flour, eggs, sugar, oil and baking powder into a firm, smooth dough.","Roll into ropes and close into rings, sealing the ends well.","Boil the taralli for a few seconds until they float, then drain.","Dry them on a cloth and bake at 180 °C for 20 minutes.","Glaze with icing sugar and a little water once cold."],"o":"Like savoury Apulian taralli, these are boiled first too: that's what makes them dense rather than crumbly."},"846":{"n":"Baklava (Greek)","p":["Roughly chop the walnuts with the cinnamon.","Butter a tin and lay in half the filo pastry one sheet at a time, brushing each sheet with melted butter.","Spread the chopped walnuts in an even layer, then cover with the remaining filo sheets, still buttering each layer.","With a sharp knife score the top into diamonds, cutting right through to the bottom.","Bake at 160 °C for 45 minutes, until the top is golden and crisp.","Meanwhile heat sugar, honey, lemon juice and a glass of water together for 5 minutes to make a syrup.","As soon as it comes out of the oven pour the hot syrup over the hot baklava: the temperature contrast is what keeps it crisp. Leave to rest for at least 3 hours before serving."],"o":"It keeps soft and fragrant for days, covered at room temperature."},"847":{"n":"Spring rolls","p":["Cut the savoy cabbage and carrot into thin strips.","In a very hot wok stir-fry the cabbage, carrot and bean sprouts with chopped garlic and ginger for 4-5 minutes: they should stay crunchy.","Add the soy sauce, turn off the heat and let the filling cool.","Spread the filling on the wrappers and roll up tightly, sealing the edge with a little water.","Fry the rolls in hot oil a few at a time, until golden and crisp on all sides.","Drain on kitchen paper and serve at once, piping hot."],"o":"For a lighter version, bake them at 200 °C for 20 minutes, turning halfway."},"848":{"n":"Mango pudding","p":["Soak the gelatine in cold water for 10 minutes.","Blend the mango flesh into a smooth purée.","Warm the cream gently with the sugar, dissolve the squeezed gelatine and mix well.","Add the mango purée and coconut milk and combine carefully.","Pour into small bowls and leave to set in the fridge for at least 4 hours.","Serve cold, with a few cubes of fresh mango on top if you like."],"o":"Make it with really ripe, sweet mango: it's the real star."},"849":{"n":"Bolinhos de bacalhau","p":["Simmer the salt cod for 15 minutes, drain, remove skin and bones and flake it by hand.","Boil the potatoes, drain and mash them while still hot.","Finely chop the onion and parsley and add to the cod and potatoes along with the eggs and flour.","Mix well: the mixture should be soft but firm enough to shape into balls with two spoons.","Fry the balls a few at a time in very hot oil until golden.","Drain on kitchen paper and serve hot, with a squeeze of lemon."],"o":"Don't overwork the mixture: the more you mix it, the less fluffy it stays."},"850":{"n":"Vegetable couscous (Middle Eastern)","p":["Dice the courgettes, carrot, pepper and onion.","Heat the oil in a casserole and soften the onion, then add the other vegetables, the cumin and the turmeric.","Cook for 10 minutes, stirring, add the drained chickpeas and the stock, then cook for 10 more minutes until the vegetables are soft.","Meanwhile put the couscous in a bowl, cover with an equal volume of boiling salted water and leave to swell for 5 minutes, covered.","Fluff the couscous with a fork, dress with a drizzle of oil and divide among the plates.","Spoon the vegetables and their juices over the top and serve hot."],"o":"The secret is fluffing the couscous with a fork, not a spoon: it stays lighter."},"851":{"n":"Mahalabia","p":["Mix the cornflour into a cup of cold milk, stirring well so there are no lumps.","Heat the rest of the milk with the sugar in a small pan.","When it's hot, pour in the starch mixture and keep stirring over a medium heat until it thickens, about 8-10 minutes.","Take off the heat, flavour with the orange-blossom water and pour into small bowls.","Leave to cool, then chill for at least 3 hours.","Serve cold topped with chopped pistachios."],"o":"A very delicate pudding: the fragrance of orange-blossom water is the signature of this dessert."},"852":{"n":"Potato and pea samosas","p":["Knead the flour with lukewarm water, a pinch of salt and two tablespoons of oil into a smooth dough. Rest, covered, for 20 minutes.","Boil the potatoes, drain and roughly mash them.","Heat a drizzle of oil in a pan and fry the chopped onion, ginger and chilli, then add the peas and garam masala.","Add the mashed potatoes, adjust the salt and cook for a few minutes: the filling should be dry.","Roll the dough thinly, cut out discs, halve each disc and shape into cones: fill with the mixture and seal the edges well with water.","Fry the samosas in very hot oil until golden, drain on kitchen paper and serve hot."],"o":"If you'd rather avoid frying, bake them at 200 °C for 25 minutes, brushed with oil."},"853":{"n":"Lentil dal","p":["Rinse the lentils and cook them in water with the turmeric for 20-25 minutes, until soft and starting to break down.","Meanwhile chop the onion, garlic and ginger and fry them in ghee with the cumin until the onion is golden.","Add the chopped tomatoes and cook for 5 more minutes, until they break down.","Pour this spiced mixture into the cooked lentils, stir, season with salt and cook for 5 more minutes.","Meanwhile cook the rice in salted water and drain it.","Serve the dal piping hot with the rice."],"o":"The final touch of ghee and spices poured hot over the dal is called tadka: it's what gives the fragrance."},"854":{"n":"Gulab jamun","p":["Make the syrup: dissolve the sugar in 300 ml of water with the cardamom seeds and boil for 5 minutes, then keep warm.","Mix the milk powder, flour and baking powder, then knead with the milk into a soft, smooth dough, without overworking it.","Shape into smooth, crack-free balls the size of a walnut.","Fry the balls in oil that isn't too hot (150 °C), stirring gently, until evenly golden: they need to cook through.","Drain the hot balls and drop them straight into the warm syrup.","Leave them in the syrup for at least an hour before serving so they soak it up well."],"o":"If they brown too fast outside and stay raw inside, the oil is too hot: turn the heat down."},"855":{"n":"Kimbap","p":["Cook the rice, drain it and while still hot season with a tablespoon of sesame oil and a pinch of salt. Leave to cool slightly.","Cut the carrot into thin sticks and sauté for a couple of minutes; blanch the spinach quickly in boiling water and squeeze it well.","Beat the eggs with a pinch of salt and cook in a pan as a thin omelette, then cut into strips.","Lay a sheet of nori on a sushi mat and cover with a thin layer of rice, leaving a strip free at the top.","Arrange the carrot, spinach and egg in parallel rows in the middle, then roll up tightly using the mat.","Brush the roll with a little sesame oil and slice with a wet knife."],"o":"A wet knife is the trick for slicing the rolls without squashing them."},"856":{"n":"Japchae","p":["Boil the noodles following the packet times, drain and toss with a drizzle of sesame oil so they don't stick.","Cut the beef, carrot, onion and mushrooms into thin strips.","In a wok brown the beef with the chopped garlic, splash in a little soy sauce and set aside.","In the same wok stir-fry the carrot, onion and mushrooms in turn, adding the spinach only at the end for a few seconds.","Return everything to the wok with the noodles and season with soy sauce, sugar and a drizzle of sesame oil.","Stir-fry for another couple of minutes, mixing well, and serve hot or warm."],"o":"Each ingredient is stir-fried separately and quickly: that's what keeps the vegetables crunchy."},"857":{"n":"Bulgogi","p":["Blend the pear with garlic and ginger into a smooth purée.","Mix the purée with the soy sauce, sugar and sesame oil: this is the marinade.","Put the beef slices in the marinade, cover and leave in the fridge for at least 30 minutes, ideally a few hours.","Heat a grill or griddle well and cook the meat for a few minutes per side, until caramelised outside.","Serve piping hot, scattered with thinly sliced spring onion."],"o":"The pear in the marinade isn't just for flavour: its enzymes make the meat more tender."},"858":{"n":"Hotteok","p":["Dissolve the yeast in lukewarm milk, then add it to the flour with a pinch of salt and a spoonful of sugar.","Knead into a soft, sticky dough, cover and leave to rise for an hour in a warm place.","Make the filling by mixing the remaining sugar with the cinnamon and roughly chopped walnuts.","With oiled hands take a portion of dough, flatten it on your palm, put a teaspoon of filling in the middle and close it, sealing well.","Heat a drizzle of oil in a pan and cook the pancakes, pressing them lightly with a spatula, a couple of minutes per side until golden.","Serve hot: the sugar inside melts into a syrup."],"o":"Eat them hot, freshly made: the melted sugar inside is what makes them so irresistible."},"859":{"n":"Dorayaki","p":["Whisk the eggs with the sugar and honey until pale and foamy.","Fold in the flour sifted with the baking powder, mixing gently so as not to knock out the air.","Leave the batter to rest for 15 minutes at room temperature.","Heat a non-stick pan without greasing it and pour in a small ladle of batter at a time, making discs like small pancakes.","Cook until bubbles form on the surface, then flip and cook for another minute. Repeat until the batter is used up.","Spread each disc with a generous spoonful of sweet red bean paste and close it like a sandwich with a second disc."],"o":"The discs should stay soft and slightly springy: cooking is brief, over a medium-low heat."}};
